(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1c.83aecfbcd8.js"]={"9":[{"id":"sdl09_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the dura mater, arachnoid mater, pia mater, and the spaces related to each layer.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cell layer forms the innermost aspect of the dura mater and represents a plane of structural weakness where pathologic bleeding can split it open?","choices":{"A":"The arachnoid barrier cell layer, composed of cells joined by numerous tight junctions that prevent CSF leakage","B":"The dural border cell layer, made of flattened fibroblasts with sparse matrix and few intercellular junctions","C":"The periosteal dura, composed of dense fibrous tissue firmly adherent to the inner table of the skull","D":"The pia-glial membrane, composed of pial cells intimately fused with underlying astrocyte end-feet","E":"The arachnoid trabeculae, composed of spindly fibroblasts that bridge the subarachnoid space"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The dural border cell layer is the innermost portion of the dura, made of flattened fibroblasts with sparse extracellular matrix and only occasional cell junctions, and this loose architecture is exactly why it constitutes a structurally weak plane that splits open to form the subdural hematoma space rather than a naturally occurring cavity. The arachnoid barrier cell layer instead has closely apposed tight junctions that resist CSF leakage rather than bleeding, the periosteal dura is firmly adherent to bone and is not the site of this weakness, the pia-glial membrane is far removed from the dura, and the arachnoid trabeculae bridge the subarachnoid space rather than lying within the dura itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognizing the dural border cell layer as the actual site that splits open in a subdural hematoma, rather than a true preexisting anatomic space, is a frequently tested conceptual correction. COMLEX: This same weak plane concept explains why subdural collections spread diffusely over a hemisphere rather than being confined by suture lines.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 1 — tracing CSF from the choroid plexus through the subarachnoid space, the same fluid-filled compartment defined by the arachnoid and pia mater here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the dura mater, arachnoid mater, pia mater, and the spaces related to each layer.","batch":1,"stem":"The brain is often described as floating in CSF, but it is actually suspended within the subarachnoid space, where it weighs only about 45 to 50 g instead of about 1400 g. Which structures suspend the brain within this space?","choices":{"A":"Denticulate ligaments anchoring the pia to the dura","B":"Bridging veins running from the cortex to the sinuses","C":"Dural border cells binding the dura to the arachnoid","D":"Arachnoid trabeculae linking the arachnoid to the pia","E":"Choroid plexus fronds tethering the ventricular walls"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The dura is adherent to the skull, the arachnoid to the dura, the arachnoid trabeculae to the pia, and the pia to the surface of the brain, so the brain is suspended through this chain by the delicate trabecular strands that cross the subarachnoid space. In CSF the brain loses about 97% of its weight, which these fine strands can support; because the trabeculae are not rigid, the brain can still move on its tethers in a closed head injury, causing concussion or contusion at the site of the blow or opposite it. Denticulate ligaments are pial specializations that anchor the spinal cord, not the brain. Bridging veins drain blood and tear in subdural hematoma rather than suspending the brain. Dural border cells form a weak plane within the inner dura, and choroid plexus secretes CSF inside the ventricles.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The subarachnoid space contains CSF, arachnoid trabeculae, arteries and veins, and cranial nerve roots. COMLEX: Movement of the brain on its trabecular tethers explains coup and contrecoup contusions after acceleration-deceleration injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 1 — tracing CSF from the choroid plexus through the subarachnoid space, the same fluid-filled compartment defined by the arachnoid and pia mater here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the dura mater, arachnoid mater, pia mater, and the spaces related to each layer.","batch":1,"stem":"As small blood vessels penetrate the brain parenchyma from the subarachnoid space, they pull a sleeve of which meningeal layer with them, creating a perivascular space?","choices":{"A":"The arachnoid barrier cell layer, creating the perivascular subarachnoid extension","B":"The dural border cell layer, creating the perivascular epidural extension","C":"The pia mater, creating the perivascular Virchow-Robin space","D":"The periosteal dura, creating the perivascular subdural extension","E":"The arachnoid trabeculae, creating the perivascular cisternal extension"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because the pia mater intimately follows every surface contour of the brain, penetrating vessels drag a thin sleeve of pia with them as they dive into the parenchyma, generating the perivascular Virchow-Robin space that is visible around small vessels on high-resolution MRI. The arachnoid barrier cell layer stays superficial at the pial-arachnoid interface rather than following vessels into the brain, the dural border cell layer and periosteal dura are far removed from the brain surface entirely, and the arachnoid trabeculae only span the subarachnoid space rather than penetrating the parenchyma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Virchow-Robin spaces as pia-lined perivascular channels are a recognized incidental MRI finding that students must distinguish from pathologic lacunar infarcts or cystic lesions. COMLEX: These same perivascular channels are increasingly discussed as a conduit for glymphatic clearance of interstitial waste from the brain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 1 — tracing CSF from the choroid plexus through the subarachnoid space, the same fluid-filled compartment defined by the arachnoid and pia mater here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare cranial and spinal meninges, especially the difference between cranial and spinal epidural spaces.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does the cranial cavity lack a naturally occurring epidural space, while the vertebral canal contains one?","choices":{"A":"The cranial dura fuses its periosteal and meningeal layers, whereas the spinal dura is only meningeal and separates from the vertebrae","B":"The cranial dura consists only of the meningeal layer, whereas the spinal dura fuses periosteal and meningeal layers tightly to the vertebrae","C":"The cranial arachnoid lacks a barrier cell layer entirely, whereas the spinal arachnoid retains a fully intact barrier layer","D":"The cranial pia mater is thicker than the spinal pia mater, physically preventing any epidural space from forming","E":"The cranial dura receives no arterial supply, whereas the spinal dura is richly vascularized by segmental arteries"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the skull, the periosteal and meningeal layers of the dura are fused together and firmly adherent to the inner table of bone, leaving no naturally occurring potential space, whereas the spinal dura is composed only of the meningeal layer because the vertebrae possess their own separate periosteum, and as development proceeds the spinal dura dissociates from the vertebral bodies to create a true epidural space filled with fat, veins, and lymphatics. Reversing which dura is single-layered versus fused describes the opposite relationship, arachnoid barrier cell layer integrity is unrelated to epidural space formation, pial thickness has no bearing on this distinction, and dural vascularity is not the mechanism responsible for this difference.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The cranial dura's fusion of periosteal and meningeal layers explaining the absence of a natural cranial epidural space is a frequently tested embryologic distinction. COMLEX: This same distinction is why every cranial epidural hematoma is purely pathologic, while a spinal epidural injection targets a normal anatomic compartment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 7 — applying lumbar puncture anatomy and safety, since the needle must safely traverse the meningeal layers and spaces compared here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare cranial and spinal meninges, especially the difference between cranial and spinal epidural spaces.","batch":1,"stem":"What does the naturally occurring spinal epidural space contain?","choices":{"A":"Cerebrospinal fluid and the roots of the cauda equina exclusively","B":"Only the denticulate ligaments anchoring the spinal cord laterally","C":"Arachnoid villi that reabsorb CSF into the epidural venous plexus","D":"The filum terminale internum and the conus medullaris directly","E":"Venous channels, lymphatics, and fat outside the spinal dural sac"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The spinal epidural space, created when the spinal dura separates from the vertebrae during development, is filled with venous channels, lymphatics, and fat lying entirely outside the dural sac, and this space is the target for epidural anesthesia and the site of pathologic spinal epidural hematomas or abscesses. CSF and the cauda equina roots instead occupy the lumbar cistern within the subarachnoid space, the denticulate ligaments anchor laterally to the dural sac itself rather than filling the epidural space, arachnoid villi are a cranial specialization absent in the spine, and the filum terminale internum and conus medullaris are intradural structures rather than epidural contents.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The fat, veins, and lymphatics of the spinal epidural space are the anatomic basis for both epidural anesthesia and spinal epidural abscess or hematoma formation. COMLEX: An epidural needle must be advanced only until loss of resistance is felt, confirming entry into this fatty, low-resistance compartment rather than the dural sac.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 7 — applying lumbar puncture anatomy and safety, since the needle must safely traverse the meningeal layers and spaces compared here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare cranial and spinal meninges, especially the difference between cranial and spinal epidural spaces.","batch":1,"stem":"Which pial specialization anchors the spinal cord laterally to the dural sac and is absent from the cranial meninges?","choices":{"A":"The arachnoid villi","B":"The dural border cell layer","C":"The denticulate ligaments","D":"The tentorium cerebelli","E":"The perivascular Virchow-Robin spaces"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The denticulate ligaments are tooth-shaped pial extensions that attach laterally to the dural sac at regular intervals along the length of the spinal cord, providing mechanical stability, and together with the filum terminale internum they represent spinal pial specializations that have no cranial counterpart. Arachnoid villi are instead a cranial arachnoid specialization used for CSF reabsorption and are absent in the spine, the dural border cell layer is a dural rather than pial structure present in both compartments, the tentorium cerebelli is a cranial dural fold with no spinal equivalent, and Virchow-Robin spaces occur wherever penetrating vessels are found rather than serving an anchoring function.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Denticulate ligaments as the lateral anchoring structures unique to spinal pia are occasionally tested as a contrast to cranial-only arachnoid villi. COMLEX: Surgeons use the denticulate ligaments as reliable intraoperative landmarks during procedures that require rotating or retracting the exposed spinal cord.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 7 — applying lumbar puncture anatomy and safety, since the needle must safely traverse the meningeal layers and spaces compared here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how dural folds and dural venous sinuses are formed, and why they matter clinically.","batch":1,"stem":"Dural folds such as the falx cerebri form when which event occurs within the cranial dura?","choices":{"A":"The outer periosteal dura detaches entirely from the skull and drapes freely over the brain surface","B":"The arachnoid barrier cell layer thickens locally and pushes the pia mater away from the brain","C":"The subarachnoid space herniates outward through a defect in the overlying dura","D":"The dural border cell layer proliferates and forms a solid septum between the cerebral hemispheres","E":"The meningeal dura leaves the periosteal dura to form a rigid infolding into the cranial cavity"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Dural folds such as the falx cerebri and tentorium cerebelli form when the inner meningeal dura separates locally from the outer periosteal dura, which remains fixed to the skull, producing a rigid double-layered infolding that projects inward to partition the cranial cavity into distinct compartments. Complete detachment of the periosteal dura from the skull does not occur under normal conditions, the arachnoid barrier cell layer is unrelated to dural fold formation, subarachnoid space herniation does not describe how these rigid partitions arise, and the dural border cell layer is a passive innermost layer rather than an active proliferative septum former.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Understanding dural folds as local separations of meningeal from periosteal dura, rather than entirely new tissue, explains why they are rigid rather than compressible under pressure. COMLEX: This rigidity is precisely what makes dural folds the mechanical edges over which herniation syndromes occur.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 6 — describing superficial and deep venous drainage, the same dural venous sinuses whose formation is explained here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how dural folds and dural venous sinuses are formed, and why they matter clinically.","batch":1,"stem":"Which dural fold separates the supratentorial compartment from the infratentorial compartment?","choices":{"A":"The falx cerebri","B":"The falx cerebelli","C":"The tentorium cerebelli","D":"The diaphragma sellae","E":"The anterior intercavernous sinus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The tentorium cerebelli is the roughly horizontal dural fold that separates the supratentorial compartment, containing the cerebral hemispheres, from the infratentorial compartment, containing the cerebellum and brainstem, and this division is central to classifying herniation syndromes as supratentorial or infratentorial. The falx cerebri instead separates the right and left cerebral hemispheres within the supratentorial compartment, the falx cerebelli is a small midline fold between the cerebellar hemispheres below the tentorium, the diaphragma sellae only roofs the hypophyseal fossa around the pituitary stalk, and the intercavernous sinuses are venous channels rather than dural folds.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The tentorium cerebelli defining supratentorial versus infratentorial compartments underlies the entire classification of herniation syndromes tested on boards. COMLEX: Lesions described as infratentorial should immediately raise concern for brainstem or cerebellar compression and contraindicated lumbar puncture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 6 — describing superficial and deep venous drainage, the same dural venous sinuses whose formation is explained here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how dural folds and dural venous sinuses are formed, and why they matter clinically.","batch":1,"stem":"Dural venous sinuses form at which specific location within the dura?","choices":{"A":"Points where a dural fold leaves the periosteal dura, creating an endothelium-lined channel","B":"Randomly distributed points throughout the arachnoid trabeculae unrelated to any dural fold","C":"The subarachnoid space itself, wherever CSF pressure locally exceeds venous pressure","D":"The dural border cell layer, wherever it splits open from minor everyday head movements","E":"The pia-glial membrane, wherever astrocyte end-feet fail to fully ensheath a penetrating vessel"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Dural venous sinuses form precisely at the points where a dural fold, such as the falx cerebri or tentorium cerebelli, separates from the periosteal dura, creating an endothelium-lined channel enclosed between the two dural layers that collects venous blood from the brain. Random distribution unrelated to dural folds does not describe an organized anatomic structure, the subarachnoid space is a separate CSF-filled compartment rather than the site of venous sinus formation, the dural border cell layer splitting describes subdural hematoma formation rather than normal venous sinus anatomy, and the pia-glial membrane is unrelated to dural venous sinus formation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Venous sinuses forming exactly along the attachment lines of dural folds explains why the superior sagittal sinus runs along the falx cerebri and the straight sinus runs along its junction with the tentorium. COMLEX: This predictable relationship lets a student infer sinus location from dural fold anatomy without separate memorization.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 6 — describing superficial and deep venous drainage, the same dural venous sinuses whose formation is explained here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Trace the major dural venous sinuses and recognize high-yield cavernous sinus relationships.","batch":1,"stem":"Following the normal pathway of venous drainage, blood in the superior sagittal sinus and straight sinus meets at which structure before continuing toward the internal jugular vein?","choices":{"A":"The cavernous sinus, located on either side of the sella turcica","B":"The confluence of sinuses, located near the internal occipital protuberance","C":"The inferior sagittal sinus, located along the free edge of the falx cerebri","D":"The great cerebral vein of Galen, located beneath the splenium of the corpus callosum","E":"The superior petrosal sinus, located along the attached edge of the tentorium cerebelli"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The superior sagittal sinus and the straight sinus converge at the confluence of sinuses, also called the confluens sinuum, located near the internal occipital protuberance, from which blood then typically drains predominantly into the right transverse sinus from the superior sagittal sinus and into the left transverse sinus from the straight sinus. The cavernous sinus is an entirely separate venous structure near the sella turcica unrelated to this posterior confluence, the inferior sagittal sinus and the great cerebral vein of Galen are upstream tributaries that join to form the straight sinus rather than the meeting point itself, and the superior petrosal sinus is a downstream drainage route from the cavernous sinus rather than this posterior confluence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The confluence of sinuses as the posterior meeting point of the superior sagittal and straight sinuses, with typical asymmetric right-left drainage into the transverse sinuses, is tested when interpreting venography for sinus thrombosis. COMLEX: This asymmetry explains why unilateral transverse sinus thrombosis is sometimes clinically silent if the dominant side remains patent.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 3 — using cranial nerve findings to localize lesions to the cavernous sinus, the same high-yield sinus relationships traced here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Trace the major dural venous sinuses and recognize high-yield cavernous sinus relationships.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structures are found within the cavernous sinus, running between the dura and the endothelial lining of the sinus?","choices":{"A":"The cavernous internal carotid artery and cranial nerves III, IV, VI, V1, and V2","B":"The petrous segment of the internal carotid artery and cranial nerves V3, VII, and VIII exclusively","C":"The basilar artery and cranial nerves IX, X, XI, and XII exclusively","D":"The vertebral artery and the sympathetic chain ganglia exclusively","E":"The ophthalmic artery alone, with no cranial nerves traversing the sinus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The cavernous sinus contains the cavernous segment of the internal carotid artery along with cranial nerves III, IV, and VI, plus the ophthalmic and maxillary divisions of the trigeminal nerve, all of which course through the lateral wall or lumen of the sinus between the dura and its endothelial lining, making this a densely packed and clinically critical region. The petrous carotid segment and CN V3, VII, and VIII instead relate to the internal acoustic meatus and jugular foramen region rather than the cavernous sinus, the basilar artery and lower cranial nerves IX through XII are brainstem and jugular foramen structures, the vertebral artery and sympathetic chain are unrelated to the cavernous sinus itself, and the ophthalmic artery alone drastically understates the sinus's dense cranial nerve content.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The cavernous sinus contents, ICA plus CN III, IV, VI, V1, and V2, form one of the single most heavily tested lists in neuroanatomy because a single expanding lesion can compress all of them at once. COMLEX: Memorizing this list is the fastest way to recognize cavernous sinus syndrome from a cluster of ocular motor and facial sensory deficits in a vignette.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 3 — using cranial nerve findings to localize lesions to the cavernous sinus, the same high-yield sinus relationships traced here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Trace the major dural venous sinuses and recognize high-yield cavernous sinus relationships.","batch":1,"stem":"The transverse sinuses curve inferiorly to become which paired venous sinus, which then exits the skull at the jugular foramen?","choices":{"A":"The petrosal sinuses","B":"The occipital sinuses","C":"The intercavernous sinuses","D":"The sigmoid sinuses","E":"The basilar venous plexus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The transverse sinuses curve inferiorly and medially to become the sigmoid sinuses, which then exit the cranial cavity through the jugular foramen to become the internal jugular veins, completing the final segment of the major dural venous drainage pathway. The petrosal sinuses instead drain the cavernous sinus into the sigmoid sinus rather than being a continuation of the transverse sinus itself, the occipital sinus is a small midline channel within the falx cerebelli, the intercavernous sinuses connect the paired cavernous sinuses across the diaphragma sellae, and the basilar venous plexus is a separate network near the clivus unrelated to this transverse-to-jugular pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The sequence superior sagittal sinus to confluence to transverse sinus to sigmoid sinus to internal jugular vein is the single most commonly tested venous drainage pathway in neuroanatomy. COMLEX: Sigmoid sinus thrombosis, often from adjacent mastoiditis, is a classic complication that follows this exact anatomic route.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 3 — using cranial nerve findings to localize lesions to the cavernous sinus, the same high-yield sinus relationships traced here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid hemorrhage using mechanism, vessel, location, imaging, and presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"An epidural hematoma most commonly results from laceration of which vessel?","choices":{"A":"A cortical bridging vein","B":"The middle meningeal artery","C":"The internal carotid artery within the cavernous sinus","D":"A ruptured berry aneurysm at the circle of Willis","E":"The great cerebral vein of Galen"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The middle meningeal artery, a branch of the maxillary artery that enters the skull through the foramen spinosum, is classically lacerated by a skull fracture of the temporal or parietal bone, and the resulting arterial bleeding rapidly dissects the periosteal dura away from the inner table of the skull to form an epidural hematoma. Bridging veins are instead the culprit in subdural hematoma rather than epidural bleeding, the cavernous internal carotid artery relates to carotid-cavernous fistula rather than epidural hematoma, a ruptured berry aneurysm classically causes subarachnoid hemorrhage, and the vein of Galen is a deep venous structure unrelated to epidural bleeding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Middle meningeal artery laceration from temporal or parietal skull fracture is the single most tested epidural hematoma fact and is almost always paired with a lens-shaped CT finding. COMLEX: The foramen spinosum entry point of this artery makes pterion-region skull fractures especially dangerous.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 5 — comparing epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid hemorrhage alongside ischemic and lacunar stroke, extending the hemorrhage differentiation introduced here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid hemorrhage using mechanism, vessel, location, imaging, and presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"A subdural hematoma most commonly results from tearing of which structure?","choices":{"A":"The middle meningeal artery as it enters the foramen spinosum","B":"A saccular aneurysm at the anterior communicating artery","C":"An arteriovenous malformation within the parenchyma","D":"Bridging veins crossing the subarachnoid space to a sinus","E":"The accessory meningeal artery as it enters the foramen ovale"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bridging veins, which travel from the cortical surface across the subarachnoid space to drain into a dural venous sinus, are fragile and can tear when the brain shifts suddenly relative to the fixed dura, a shear force that occurs even with minor trauma in elderly patients whose brain volume has decreased, producing bleeding into the dural border cell layer at the dura-arachnoid interface. The middle meningeal artery and accessory meningeal artery are instead implicated in epidural hematoma rather than subdural bleeding, an anterior communicating artery aneurysm classically causes subarachnoid hemorrhage, and an arteriovenous malformation is a parenchymal or subarachnoid lesion rather than the classic subdural mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bridging vein tears from brain-skull shift, especially after minor trauma in elderly or atrophic brains, is the defining mechanism tested for subdural hematoma. COMLEX: Chronic alcohol use and anticoagulant therapy both increase subdural hematoma risk by promoting brain atrophy or impairing clot formation after these same shear injuries.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 5 — comparing epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid hemorrhage alongside ischemic and lacunar stroke, extending the hemorrhage differentiation introduced here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid hemorrhage using mechanism, vessel, location, imaging, and presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"Spontaneous subarachnoid hemorrhage is most commonly caused by rupture of which lesion?","choices":{"A":"A subdural bridging vein","B":"The middle meningeal artery at the foramen spinosum","C":"An epidural venous plexus in the vertebral canal","D":"A cortical Virchow-Robin perivascular vessel","E":"An intracranial saccular, or berry, aneurysm"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Spontaneous subarachnoid hemorrhage is most commonly caused by rupture of an intracranial saccular, or berry, aneurysm, most often located at a branch point of the circle of Willis, which allows blood to percolate freely throughout the subarachnoid space and basal cisterns and appear diffusely hyperdense on CT outlining the sulci. Bridging vein tearing instead produces subdural rather than subarachnoid hemorrhage, middle meningeal artery laceration produces epidural hemorrhage, an epidural venous plexus is a spinal rather than intracranial structure, and Virchow-Robin perivascular vessels are far too small to be a source of clinically significant hemorrhage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Berry aneurysm rupture at a circle of Willis branch point as the classic cause of spontaneous subarachnoid hemorrhage is tested alongside its association with polycystic kidney disease and connective tissue disorders. COMLEX: The sudden thunderclap headache of subarachnoid hemorrhage should prompt an urgent noncontrast head CT before any lumbar puncture is considered.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 5 — comparing epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid hemorrhage alongside ischemic and lacunar stroke, extending the hemorrhage differentiation introduced here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply meningeal anatomy to lumbar puncture, spinal anesthesia, epidural anesthesia, meningitis, herniation syndromes, and meningioma.","batch":1,"stem":"A lumbar puncture needle inserted at the L4-L5 interspace enters the lumbar cistern, which contains which structures?","choices":{"A":"Only CSF and the descending nerve roots of the cauda equina","B":"The conus medullaris and the descending nerve roots of the cauda equina","C":"Only the filum terminale internum, with no CSF present at this level","D":"Epidural fat and venous channels surrounding the dural sac","E":"The denticulate ligaments and the spinal cord parenchyma itself"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because the spinal cord itself ends at approximately the L1-L2 level while the dural sac and subarachnoid space continue down to about S2, the lumbar cistern below the conus medullaris contains only CSF and the freely floating descending nerve roots of the cauda equina, allowing a needle inserted at L3-L4 or L4-L5 to obtain CSF safely without striking solid neural tissue. The conus medullaris itself lies above this level and is not normally encountered here, the filum terminale internum is present but CSF is abundant throughout the cistern, epidural fat and venous channels lie outside the dural sac rather than within the cistern, and the denticulate ligaments attach higher along the cord rather than existing within this caudal CSF-only space.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The lumbar cistern as a CSF-only space below the conus medullaris containing solely the cauda equina roots is the anatomic safety principle behind every lumbar puncture performed at L3-L4 or L4-L5. First Aid mnemonic: to keep the cord alive, keep the spinal needle between L3 and L5. COMLEX: Palpating the iliac crests to find Tuffier's line at approximately L4 is the bedside technique for locating this safe interspace.","crossRef":"📎 Identifying urgent neurologic red flags including meningitis, the same clinical scenario meningeal anatomy is applied to here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply meningeal anatomy to lumbar puncture, spinal anesthesia, epidural anesthesia, meningitis, herniation syndromes, and meningioma.","batch":1,"stem":"Meningiomas most commonly arise from which cell type?","choices":{"A":"Dural border cell fibroblasts of the innermost dural layer","B":"Choroid plexus epithelial cells within the ventricular system","C":"Schwann cells ensheathing the vestibular portion of cranial nerve VIII","D":"Arachnoid cap cells associated with the arachnoid villi","E":"Oligodendrocytes of the adjacent white matter tracts"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Meningiomas typically arise from arachnoid cap cells, specialized cells associated with arachnoid villi, which explains why these tumors are most commonly found along the falx cerebri, cerebral convexity, sphenoid ridge, and olfactory groove, sites where arachnoid villi and cap cells are concentrated. Dural border cell fibroblasts and choroid plexus epithelial cells give rise to different pathologies rather than typical meningiomas, Schwann cells give rise to vestibular schwannoma rather than meningioma, and oligodendrocytes give rise to oligodendroglioma, an intra-axial rather than extra-axial tumor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Arachnoid cap cell origin explains why meningiomas are extra-axial, dural-based masses that produce a dural tail sign on MRI, distinguishing them from intra-axial gliomas. COMLEX: The parasagittal location near the superior sagittal sinus is a classic meningioma site tested alongside its potential to compress the adjacent motor cortex.","crossRef":"📎 Identifying urgent neurologic red flags including meningitis, the same clinical scenario meningeal anatomy is applied to here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply meningeal anatomy to lumbar puncture, spinal anesthesia, epidural anesthesia, meningitis, herniation syndromes, and meningioma.","batch":1,"stem":"Uncal herniation occurs when the uncus of the temporal lobe is forced over which structure?","choices":{"A":"The free edge of the falx cerebri","B":"The edge of the tentorium cerebelli","C":"The foramen magnum","D":"The diaphragma sellae","E":"The confluence of sinuses"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Uncal herniation occurs when a rapidly expanding supratentorial lesion, usually a hematoma or surrounding edema, forces the uncus, a medial temporal lobe structure, over the edge of the tentorium cerebelli, compressing the midbrain and the ipsilateral oculomotor nerve as it courses along the tentorial edge. The falx cerebri is instead the site of subfalcine herniation involving the cingulate gyrus rather than the uncus, the foramen magnum is the site of tonsillar rather than uncal herniation, the diaphragma sellae surrounds the pituitary stalk and is not the structure involved in uncal herniation, and the confluence of sinuses is a venous landmark unrelated to any herniation pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Uncal herniation over the tentorial edge producing ipsilateral oculomotor nerve compression, a blown pupil, and contralateral hemiplegia is one of the highest-yield localization triads in clinical neuroscience. COMLEX: Recognizing this triad rapidly should prompt emergent imaging and neurosurgical evaluation before further deterioration occurs.","crossRef":"📎 Identifying urgent neurologic red flags including meningitis, the same clinical scenario meningeal anatomy is applied to here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Predict symptoms from meningeal irritation, dural innervation, venous sinus thrombosis, and cavernous sinus pathology.","batch":1,"stem":"The dura of the posterior cranial fossa is innervated primarily by which nerves?","choices":{"A":"The ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve exclusively","B":"The maxillary and mandibular divisions of the trigeminal nerve exclusively","C":"The glossopharyngeal nerve exclusively","D":"The facial nerve and its chorda tympani branch exclusively","E":"Sensory branches of C1 to C3, with some contribution from the vagus nerve"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The dura of the posterior fossa receives sensory innervation from the dorsal roots of the upper cervical spinal nerves C1 through C3, with an additional minor contribution from the vagus nerve, which explains why posterior fossa pathology can produce referred pain perceived in the occiput and upper neck. The ophthalmic division supplies the tentorium cerebelli specifically via the tentorial nerve rather than the entire posterior fossa, the maxillary and mandibular divisions supply the anterior and middle fossae rather than the posterior fossa, the glossopharyngeal nerve is not the primary posterior fossa dural sensory supply, and the facial nerve and chorda tympani carry taste and parasympathetic fibers unrelated to dural sensation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: C1 to C3 dorsal root innervation of the posterior fossa dura explains occipital referred pain from posterior fossa masses or hemorrhage, a pattern distinct from trigeminal referral for anterior and middle fossa lesions. COMLEX: This same C1 to C3 convergence is the anatomic basis for cervicogenic headache patterns evaluated with manual medicine techniques.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 8 — recognizing high-yield clinical patterns including cavernous sinus lesions, the same pathology predicted from meningeal anatomy here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Predict symptoms from meningeal irritation, dural innervation, venous sinus thrombosis, and cavernous sinus pathology.","batch":1,"stem":"Which dural venous sinus is the most common site of dural venous sinus thrombosis?","choices":{"A":"The cavernous sinus","B":"The superior sagittal sinus","C":"The straight sinus","D":"The inferior sagittal sinus","E":"The occipital sinus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The superior sagittal sinus is the most common site of dural venous sinus thrombosis, and its occlusion blocks both venous outflow and CSF reabsorption through the arachnoid villi that project into it, leading to brain edema, elevated intracranial pressure, and potential venous infarction or hemorrhage. The cavernous sinus is a separate, distinct site of thrombosis usually associated with facial infection rather than being the single most common location overall, the straight sinus and inferior sagittal sinus are less frequently affected, and the occipital sinus is a small, clinically minor channel rarely implicated as the primary thrombosis site.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Superior sagittal sinus thrombosis as the most common dural sinus thrombosis site, presenting with headache, papilledema, and possible venous infarction, is frequently paired with hypercoagulable states such as pregnancy or oral contraceptive use. COMLEX: MR venography, rather than standard CT, is typically required to confirm this diagnosis when clinical suspicion is high.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 8 — recognizing high-yield clinical patterns including cavernous sinus lesions, the same pathology predicted from meningeal anatomy here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Predict symptoms from meningeal irritation, dural innervation, venous sinus thrombosis, and cavernous sinus pathology.","batch":1,"stem":"Cavernous sinus pathology, such as an expanding aneurysm or thrombosis, classically produces which combination of findings?","choices":{"A":"Ipsilateral ophthalmoplegia with a dilated pupil and V1–V2 sensory loss","B":"Bilateral lower facial weakness with sparing of forehead movement on both sides","C":"Contralateral hemiplegia with ipsilateral tongue deviation toward the lesion","D":"Bilateral papilledema with a normal-appearing fundus and preserved pupillary reflexes","E":"Ipsilateral hearing loss with vertigo and a positive head impulse test"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because cranial nerves III, IV, and VI along with the ophthalmic and maxillary divisions of the trigeminal nerve all pass through or along the wall of the cavernous sinus, an expanding lesion within it classically produces ipsilateral ocular motor palsy with a dilated, unresponsive pupil from oculomotor involvement, loss of the corneal reflex from ophthalmic sensory loss, and severe forehead and cheek pain or paresthesias from ophthalmic and maxillary involvement. Bilateral lower facial weakness suggests a facial nerve or corticobulbar process unrelated to the cavernous sinus, contralateral hemiplegia with tongue deviation describes a medullary or corticospinal syndrome, bilateral papilledema with preserved pupils describes elevated intracranial pressure rather than focal cavernous sinus disease, and hearing loss with vertigo points toward a cerebellopontine angle or vestibular process rather than the cavernous sinus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The combined ocular motor, corneal, and V1-V2 sensory deficit pattern is the classic presentation of cavernous sinus syndrome and should immediately localize a lesion to this specific dural venous compartment. COMLEX: A carotid-cavernous fistula additionally produces a pulsatile exophthalmos and an audible bruit that further confirms this localization.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 8 — recognizing high-yield clinical patterns including cavernous sinus lesions, the same pathology predicted from meningeal anatomy here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q22","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the dura mater, arachnoid mater, pia mater, and the spaces related to each layer.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher studying meningeal barrier function applies a tracer dye to the outer surface of the arachnoid mater and finds that the dye cannot cross into the CSF-filled compartment beneath it, despite the tracer easily diffusing through the dural border cell layer above. Which structural feature accounts for this selective barrier?","choices":{"A":"Numerous tight junctions joining the cells of the arachnoid barrier cell layer","B":"A thick basement membrane surrounding the arachnoid trabeculae","C":"Fenestrated capillaries within the arachnoid barrier cell layer","D":"A dense collagen matrix within the dural border cell layer","E":"Continuous astrocyte end-feet lining the arachnoid barrier cell layer"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The arachnoid barrier cell layer is characterized by cells joined with numerous tight junctions, forming the actual physiologic barrier that prevents CSF leakage outward and blocks tracers from crossing inward, in sharp contrast to the loosely connected dural border cell layer above it that offers little resistance to diffusion. A thick basement membrane around the trabeculae is not the described barrier mechanism, fenestrated capillaries would promote rather than block diffusion and are not a feature of this cell layer, a dense collagen matrix in the dural border cell layer does not explain the sharp barrier seen only at the arachnoid, and astrocyte end-feet are a pial-glial rather than arachnoid feature.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The arachnoid barrier cell layer's tight junctions, not the dura or pia, are the true physical barrier preventing CSF leakage, a frequently misattributed detail on exams. COMLEX: This barrier is also why epidural anesthetic, if inadvertently injected too deep, does not automatically produce a total spinal block unless the dura and arachnoid are both breached.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — tracing CSF production and circulation, since arachnoid barrier cell tight junctions are what confine CSF to the subarachnoid compartment traced in that pathway","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q23","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the dura mater, arachnoid mater, pia mater, and the spaces related to each layer.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man undergoes brain MRI for evaluation of mild memory complaints. The radiologist notes several small, round, fluid-signal foci scattered around penetrating vessels in the basal ganglia, following the vessels' course and matching CSF signal intensity on all sequences, without surrounding edema or restricted diffusion. Which structure do these foci most likely represent?","choices":{"A":"Acute lacunar infarcts, small vessel occlusions producing restricted diffusion","B":"Dilated subarachnoid cisterns, enlargements of the true subarachnoid space","C":"Dilated Virchow-Robin spaces, pia-lined channels around penetrating vessels","D":"Arachnoid cysts, congenital collections trapped between arachnoid layers","E":"Subdural hygromas, chronic collections within the dural border cell layer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Perivascular Virchow-Robin spaces are pia-lined channels created when small penetrating vessels drag a sleeve of pia mater with them into the brain parenchyma, and when dilated they appear as small, round, CSF-signal foci that follow vessel courses without edema or restricted diffusion, a common and usually benign incidental finding, especially in the basal ganglia of older patients. Acute lacunar infarcts would show restricted diffusion and are typically not perfectly CSF-signal, dilated subarachnoid cisterns are much larger true subarachnoid enlargements rather than tiny parenchymal foci, arachnoid cysts are discrete extra-axial collections rather than perivascular parenchymal foci, and subdural hygromas are extra-axial collections along the brain surface rather than small foci following penetrating vessels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distinguishing benign dilated Virchow-Robin spaces from acute lacunar infarction on MRI, based on CSF signal and absence of restricted diffusion, is a classic imaging pattern recognition question. COMLEX: Reassuring an anxious patient that these incidental findings are typically benign avoids unnecessary further workup.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 6 — interpreting cross-sectional neuroimaging using anatomical landmarks, the same skill needed to correctly identify perivascular spaces rather than pathology on this scan","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q24","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the dura mater, arachnoid mater, pia mater, and the spaces related to each layer.","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old man on chronic anticoagulation falls and strikes his head on a low table. Over the next two weeks he develops progressive confusion and gait unsteadiness. CT shows a crescent-shaped collection of blood spread diffusely over the left hemisphere, not confined by the cranial sutures. At the microscopic level, this blood is collecting within which structurally weak plane?","choices":{"A":"The arachnoid barrier cell layer, split open by torn cortical arteries","B":"The subarachnoid space itself, expanded by torn arachnoid trabeculae","C":"The periosteal dura, dissected off the inner table of the skull","D":"The pia-glial membrane, separated from the underlying cortex","E":"The dural border cell layer, split open by torn bridging veins"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This presentation of a slowly progressive, sutures-unconstrained crescent-shaped collection following minor trauma in an anticoagulated elderly patient is the classic subdural hematoma, and the blood collects within the dural border cell layer, the structurally weak innermost dural plane that splits open when fragile bridging veins tear as brain volume shifts relative to the fixed skull and dura. The arachnoid barrier cell layer is instead a tight-junction barrier that does not split open in this manner, the subarachnoid space is the site of subarachnoid rather than subdural hemorrhage, the periosteal dura dissecting off the skull describes epidural rather than subdural hematoma, and the pia-glial membrane is not the plane involved in subdural bleeding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognizing that the true anatomic split in a subdural hematoma occurs within the dural border cell layer, not a preexisting subdural space, refines the classic imaging and mechanism teaching for this diagnosis. COMLEX: Anticoagulation and brain atrophy both independently raise subdural hematoma risk after even minor trauma in elderly patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 5 — differentiating epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid hemorrhage, since this dural border cell layer is exactly where subdural hemorrhage collects in that comparison","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q25","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare cranial and spinal meninges, especially the difference between cranial and spinal epidural spaces.","batch":2,"stem":"During a caudal approach to the sacral canal, a resident asks how the spinal dural sac is held in place at each end, since, unlike the cranial dura, it is not fused to surrounding bone. The sac is attached rostrally to the rim of the foramen magnum and ends at about the second sacral vertebra. Which structure anchors its caudal end to the coccyx?","choices":{"A":"The denticulate ligaments, lateral pial extensions between the spinal roots","B":"The filum terminale externum, the dural part of the filum terminale","C":"The filum terminale internum, the pial strand inside the dural sac","D":"The posterior longitudinal ligament, fused to the anterior dural surface","E":"The conus medullaris, the tapered caudal end of the spinal cord itself"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rostrally the spinal dural sac is attached to the rim of the foramen magnum; caudally it ends at about the level of the second sacral vertebra and is attached to the coccyx by the filum terminale externum, also called the dural part of the filum terminale or coccygeal ligament. Elsewhere the spinal dura is separated from the vertebrae by an epidural space containing fat, veins, and lymphatics, unlike the cranial dura, which is fused to the skull. The denticulate ligaments anchor the cord laterally to the dura, not the sac to bone. The filum terminale internum is the pial strand that runs from the conus within the sac. The posterior longitudinal ligament lies on the vertebral bodies, and the conus medullaris ends far above the sacrum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The dural sac ends at about S2 and the cord at about L1-L2; the filum terminale has a pial internal part inside the sac and a dural external part anchoring it to the coccyx. COMLEX: Because the dural sac stops near S2, injections into the lower sacral canal reach the epidural space rather than the CSF.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — gross spinal cord organization including the conus medullaris and cauda equina within the dural sac","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q26","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare cranial and spinal meninges, especially the difference between cranial and spinal epidural spaces.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn is found to have a tuft of hair and a small dimple overlying the lower lumbar spine, with an underlying tract that extends toward the meninges. Workup confirms a congenital dermal sinus caused by incomplete separation of the neuroectoderm from the overlying skin during development. This defect most directly disrupts the normal separation of which two structures from one another?","choices":{"A":"The surface ectoderm destined to become skin and the mesenchyme that forms the primitive meninges","B":"The spinal dura and the developing vertebral periosteum, producing an abnormally large epidural space","C":"The arachnoid barrier cell layer and the pia mater, producing an abnormally large subarachnoid space","D":"The notochord and the overlying neural plate, preventing normal neural tube closure entirely","E":"The dural border cell layer and the periosteal dura, producing a cranial-type fused dura in the spine"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A congenital dermal sinus results from a failure of the surface ectoderm, the future skin, to fully pinch off from the underlying neuroectoderm and the primitive meninges formed by migrating neural crest and mesodermal cells, leaving a persistent epithelium-lined tract connecting the skin to the meninges that predisposes to recurrent meningitis. The spinal dura separating from the vertebral periosteum describes normal formation of the spinal epidural space rather than this defect, the arachnoid and pia separating describes subarachnoid space formation rather than a dermal sinus, notochord-neural plate interaction concerns neurulation itself rather than this later ectoderm-meninges defect, and cranial-type dural fusion is unrelated to a spinal dermal sinus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A congenital dermal sinus as a persistent tract between skin and meninges, causing recurrent meningitis in a young child, links neural crest and mesodermal meningeal development directly to a testable clinical vignette. COMLEX: A visible sacral dimple or hair tuft in a newborn should always prompt evaluation for an underlying dermal sinus or other spinal dysraphism before any instrumentation of the area.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — differentiating neural tube, neural crest, surface ectoderm, and mesoderm derivatives, since this dermal sinus reflects a failure of separation between exactly these germ layer derivatives","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q27","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare cranial and spinal meninges, especially the difference between cranial and spinal epidural spaces.","batch":2,"stem":"A neurosurgery resident is explaining why a patient's skull fracture produced an epidural hematoma while a similar force applied to the vertebral column would instead produce bleeding into a preexisting anatomic compartment. Which statement best explains this difference?","choices":{"A":"The cranial epidural space is a large naturally occurring cavity, whereas the spinal epidural space forms only pathologically after trauma","B":"Both the cranial and spinal epidural spaces are naturally occurring cavities of equal size and content","C":"Neither the cranial nor spinal epidural space is a naturally occurring cavity, and both require trauma to form","D":"The cranial epidural space forms only pathologically, whereas the spinal epidural space is a normal anatomic compartment","E":"The cranial epidural space is filled with fat and venous channels at baseline, identical to the spinal epidural space"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because the cranial dura fuses its periosteal and meningeal layers into a single membrane tightly adherent to the skull, there is no preexisting cranial epidural space, and an epidural hematoma there must forcibly dissect the periosteal dura away from bone, whereas the spinal dura consists only of the meningeal layer and separates from the vertebrae during development, leaving a naturally occurring fat and venous channel-filled epidural space that trauma simply bleeds into. Describing the cranial epidural space as naturally occurring and the spinal one as pathologic reverses the true relationship, claiming both are naturally occurring or that neither is contradicts the well-established embryologic difference, and describing the cranial epidural space as normally fat- and vein-filled ignores that it does not exist as a baseline compartment at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This exact contrast, a pathologic cranial epidural space versus a natural spinal epidural space, is the conceptual foundation for why epidural hematomas are described differently in the skull versus the spine. COMLEX: This distinction also explains why spinal epidural hematomas can occur spontaneously in anticoagulated patients without any trauma at all, unlike cranial epidural hematomas which require force sufficient to dissect the dura from bone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 5 — differentiating epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid hemorrhage, since the absence of a natural cranial epidural space is precisely why cranial epidural hematomas are always pathologic collections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q28","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how dural folds and dural venous sinuses are formed, and why they matter clinically.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman has a slowly growing mass in the right frontal lobe near the midline. Imaging shows the mass deforming the falx cerebri and displacing the cingulate gyrus beneath its free edge into the left hemisphere. Which vascular structure is most likely to become compressed as a direct result of this displacement?","choices":{"A":"The middle cerebral artery, running along the lateral fissure far from the falx","B":"The posterior cerebral artery, running along the tentorial edge","C":"The anterior cerebral artery, running adjacent to the cingulate gyrus beneath the falx","D":"The internal carotid artery, running within the cavernous sinus","E":"The basilar artery, running along the ventral pons"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This presentation describes a subfalcine, or cingulate, herniation, in which an expanding hemispheric mass deforms the rigid falx cerebri and forces the cingulate gyrus beneath its free edge into the opposite hemisphere, and because the anterior cerebral artery runs immediately adjacent to the cingulate gyrus in this exact location, it is the vessel most likely to be compressed, potentially producing an ACA territory infarct. The middle cerebral artery runs laterally along the sylvian fissure well away from the falx, the posterior cerebral artery is instead vulnerable during central transtentorial herniation near the tentorial edge, the internal carotid artery within the cavernous sinus is unrelated to subfalcine displacement, and the basilar artery is a posterior fossa structure uninvolved in this supratentorial process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Subfalcine herniation compressing the anterior cerebral artery, producing a secondary ACA territory stroke, is a classic complication tested alongside the primary mass lesion itself. COMLEX: Bilateral lower extremity weakness developing after a known hemispheric mass should raise concern for this exact secondary ACA compromise from herniation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 6 — applying meningeal anatomy to herniation syndromes, since this vignette demonstrates the same rigid falx cerebri mechanics used to classify subfalcine herniation in that objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q29","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how dural folds and dural venous sinuses are formed, and why they matter clinically.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with a rapidly expanding midline mass develops downward displacement of the diencephalon through the tentorial incisura, a neurologic emergency associated with a high rate of serious disability or death. This process directly compresses structures located at which brainstem level?","choices":{"A":"The medulla, including the pyramids and inferior olives","B":"The pons, including the abducens and facial nerve nuclei","C":"The upper cervical spinal cord, including the anterior horn cells","D":"The midbrain, including the cerebral aqueduct and surrounding tegmentum","E":"The cerebellar deep nuclei, including the dentate and fastigial nuclei"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Central, or transtentorial, herniation forces the diencephalon downward through the tentorial incisura directly onto the midbrain immediately below it, compressing the cerebral aqueduct and surrounding tegmentum and often producing rapidly progressive impairment of consciousness along with pupillary and eye movement abnormalities. The medulla, pons, upper cervical cord, and cerebellar deep nuclei are all located either well below the tentorial incisura or in an entirely different compartment, and none is the structure directly compressed by this specific downward diencephalic displacement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Central herniation compressing the midbrain immediately below the tentorial incisura, with a roughly 90 percent rate of serious disability or death, underscores why this is treated as one of the most urgent neurosurgical emergencies. COMLEX: Sequential loss of pupillary reactivity progressing from small and reactive to fixed and dilated bilaterally tracks the rostral-to-caudal progression of this herniation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — identifying the midbrain's external and internal regions including the tectum, tegmentum, and cerebral aqueduct, the exact structures compressed when this mass forces the diencephalon through the tentorial notch","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q30","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain how dural folds and dural venous sinuses are formed, and why they matter clinically.","batch":2,"stem":"On a midsagittal MRI, a radiology resident is asked to identify the venous sinus running along the attached, superior edge of the falx cerebri, immediately adjacent to the skull. Which sinus is being described?","choices":{"A":"The inferior sagittal sinus","B":"The straight sinus","C":"The transverse sinus","D":"The sigmoid sinus","E":"The superior sagittal sinus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The superior sagittal sinus runs precisely along the attached, superior edge of the falx cerebri where it meets the inner table of the skull, forming a landmark that is directly visible on midsagittal imaging just beneath the calvarium. The inferior sagittal sinus instead runs along the free, inferior edge of the falx deep within the interhemispheric fissure, the straight sinus forms where the inferior sagittal sinus joins the vein of Galen at the junction of the falx and tentorium, and the transverse and sigmoid sinuses are found more posteriorly and inferiorly along the tentorium and skull base rather than along the falx's attached edge.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Localizing the superior sagittal sinus at the falx cerebri's attached superior edge is essential for both interpreting midsagittal imaging and avoiding this sinus during midline neurosurgical approaches. COMLEX: A parasagittal meningioma frequently invades or occludes this exact sinus, sometimes requiring venous sacrifice during surgical resection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 6 — interpreting cross-sectional neuroimaging using anatomical landmarks, applied here to correctly identify the dural venous sinus running along the attached edge of this fold","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q31","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Trace the major dural venous sinuses and recognize high-yield cavernous sinus relationships.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man develops a furuncle on the side of his nose that he repeatedly manipulates. Several days later he presents with fever, periorbital edema, proptosis, and painful ophthalmoplegia of the right eye, with rapid progression to involve the left eye as well. Which venous pathway most directly explains the spread of infection from the face to produce bilateral findings?","choices":{"A":"Venous spread through valveless facial veins to the cavernous sinus and across via the intercavernous sinuses","B":"Lymphatic drainage from the face directly into the deep cervical lymph nodes and then into the cranial cavity","C":"Arterial spread through the ophthalmic artery directly into the contralateral internal carotid artery","D":"Direct extension through the cribriform plate into the contralateral olfactory bulb and tract","E":"Retrograde CSF flow through the fourth ventricular apertures into the contralateral lateral ventricle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This presentation describes cavernous sinus thrombosis spreading from a facial infection in the danger triangle of the face, where valveless facial veins allow retrograde bacterial spread into the cavernous sinus, and because the intercavernous sinuses connect the right and left cavernous sinuses across the midline via the diaphragma sellae, infection and thrombosis can rapidly become bilateral. Lymphatic drainage to deep cervical nodes does not explain direct spread into the cranial venous system, arterial spread through the ophthalmic and internal carotid arteries does not account for a venous thrombotic process, cribriform plate extension relates to olfactory rather than venous pathways, and CSF flow through the ventricular system is unrelated to this venous spread mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The valveless facial vein connection to the cavernous sinus, enabling bilateral spread through the intercavernous sinuses, is the classic mechanism tested for cavernous sinus thrombosis following facial infection, especially from the danger triangle of the face. COMLEX: Aggressive management of facial furuncles, avoiding manipulation, is a practical preventive measure directly tied to this anatomic risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 3 — using cranial nerve findings to localize lesions to the cavernous sinus, the same localization principle applied to this facial infection spreading into that compartment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q32","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Trace the major dural venous sinuses and recognize high-yield cavernous sinus relationships.","batch":2,"stem":"During a cerebral venography procedure, contrast injected into a superficial cortical vein is tracked as it drains toward the neck. The contrast passes through the superior sagittal sinus, then the confluence of sinuses, then the right transverse sinus, and then a sinus that curves inferiorly before exiting the skull. Which structure does the contrast enter immediately before reaching the internal jugular vein?","choices":{"A":"The cavernous sinus","B":"The sigmoid sinus","C":"The superior petrosal sinus","D":"The straight sinus","E":"The inferior sagittal sinus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Following the normal venous drainage sequence, blood from the transverse sinus curves inferiorly to enter the sigmoid sinus, which then exits the skull through the jugular foramen to become the internal jugular vein, making the sigmoid sinus the final structure traversed immediately before this venous exit. The cavernous sinus is an entirely separate anterior venous structure not part of this posterior drainage sequence, the superior petrosal sinus drains the cavernous sinus into the sigmoid or transverse sinus rather than being downstream of the transverse sinus itself, and the straight sinus and inferior sagittal sinus are upstream tributaries that feed into the confluence long before this point in the pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tracing the full venous sequence, superior sagittal sinus to confluence to transverse sinus to sigmoid sinus to internal jugular vein, is essential for interpreting venography and localizing sinus thrombosis. COMLEX: Sigmoid sinus thrombosis secondary to adjacent mastoiditis is a classic complication that follows this exact final segment of the drainage pathway.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 6 — describing superficial and deep venous drainage, tracing the same complete pathway from cortical veins through the dural sinuses to the internal jugular vein used in this vignette","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q33","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Trace the major dural venous sinuses and recognize high-yield cavernous sinus relationships.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient involved in a high-speed motor vehicle collision develops a direct communication between the cavernous segment of the internal carotid artery and the surrounding venous sinus. On examination there is pulsatile bulging of the eye, a bruit audible over the orbit, and conjunctival congestion. Which additional finding would most strongly support this diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Bilateral lower extremity weakness from compression of the anterior cerebral arteries","B":"Ipsilateral facial droop sparing the forehead from a lower motor neuron facial palsy","C":"Sensorineural hearing loss from compression of the cochlear division of cranial nerve VIII","D":"Ophthalmoplegia from compression of cranial nerves III, IV, and VI within the sinus","E":"Contralateral homonymous hemianopia from compression of the optic radiations"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This presentation describes a traumatic carotid-cavernous fistula, a direct arteriovenous communication within the cavernous sinus, and because cranial nerves III, IV, and VI travel through this same sinus alongside the fistula, arterialized high pressure and mass effect classically produce ophthalmoplegia in addition to the pulsatile proptosis, orbital bruit, and conjunctival congestion already described. Bilateral lower extremity weakness from ACA compression would instead suggest subfalcine herniation rather than cavernous sinus pathology, facial droop sparing the forehead points to a facial nerve lesion outside the cavernous sinus, cochlear nerve compression points to a cerebellopontine angle process, and optic radiation compression produces a retrochiasmal visual field defect unrelated to the cavernous sinus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Carotid-cavernous fistula producing the classic triad of pulsatile proptosis, orbital bruit, and ophthalmoplegia directly demonstrates why the cavernous sinus's shared vascular and cranial nerve compartment makes this injury so clinically dramatic. COMLEX: Endovascular embolization is the definitive treatment for this fistula, directly targeting the abnormal arteriovenous connection within the sinus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 8 — recognizing high-yield clinical patterns including cavernous sinus lesions, directly extending this fistula's ocular motor findings to that broader category of cavernous sinus pathology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q34","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid hemorrhage using mechanism, vessel, location, imaging, and presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old man is struck in the temple by a baseball. He is briefly dazed but recovers and appears entirely normal for the next 45 minutes, conversing appropriately with paramedics. He then abruptly develops a severe headache, vomiting, and a rapidly declining level of consciousness. Noncontrast head CT shows a biconvex, lens-shaped hyperdensity that does not cross the coronal suture line. Which vessel is most likely responsible?","choices":{"A":"A cortical bridging vein, torn as the brain shifted relative to the skull","B":"A ruptured berry aneurysm at the middle cerebral artery bifurcation","C":"The great cerebral vein of Galen, torn during the initial impact","D":"The superior sagittal sinus, lacerated directly by the temporal impact","E":"The middle meningeal artery, lacerated by an underlying temporal bone fracture"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This vignette is the classic presentation of an epidural hematoma, a lucid interval followed by rapid deterioration after temporal trauma, with a lens-shaped, suture-limited hyperdensity on CT reflecting bleeding constrained by dural attachments at the suture lines, and the vessel responsible is almost always the middle meningeal artery lacerated by an overlying temporal bone fracture. A torn bridging vein instead produces a crescent-shaped subdural hematoma that crosses suture lines rather than being constrained by them, a ruptured berry aneurysm produces subarachnoid rather than epidural hemorrhage, the vein of Galen is a deep structure unrelated to this temporal trauma pattern, and the superior sagittal sinus runs along the midline vertex rather than the temporal region struck in this vignette.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The lucid interval followed by rapid decline after temporal trauma, paired with a lens-shaped, suture-limited CT hyperdensity, is one of the single most reliably tested clinical-imaging pairs in all of neuroscience. COMLEX: Rapid neurosurgical evacuation is time-critical in this scenario because ongoing arterial bleeding can cause herniation within hours of the initial lucid interval.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 5 — comparing epidural hemorrhage with ischemic stroke, lacunar infarction, and other hemorrhage subtypes, extending this classic lucid interval presentation into the broader stroke and hemorrhage differential","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q35","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid hemorrhage using mechanism, vessel, location, imaging, and presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"An 81-year-old woman on warfarin for atrial fibrillation is brought in by her family for two weeks of progressive confusion and unsteady gait after a minor fall against a doorframe. She has no memory of striking her head. CT shows a crescent-shaped hyperdensity along the right convexity that extends across multiple suture lines with mild midline shift. Which mechanism best explains this hematoma's characteristic shape and distribution?","choices":{"A":"A lacerated middle meningeal artery bleeds into a space constrained by dural attachments at the sutures","B":"A ruptured berry aneurysm releases blood that spreads freely through the basal cisterns","C":"Torn bridging veins bleed into the dural border cell layer, a plane that is not constrained by the cranial sutures","D":"An arteriovenous malformation ruptures directly into the brain parenchyma","E":"The dorsal roots of C1 to C3 are compressed, producing referred bleeding into the occipital dura"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This vignette is the classic subdural hematoma presentation, an elderly anticoagulated patient with minor or forgotten trauma and a delayed, progressive course, and the hematoma's crescent shape crossing suture lines reflects bleeding from torn bridging veins into the dural border cell layer, a plane of weakness that is not anchored at the sutures the way the periosteal dura is, allowing blood to spread diffusely over the hemisphere. A lacerated middle meningeal artery instead produces the suture-limited, lens-shaped epidural hematoma pattern rather than this crescent shape, a ruptured aneurysm produces subarachnoid rather than subdural hemorrhage, an arteriovenous malformation rupturing into parenchyma describes intraparenchymal rather than subdural hemorrhage, and dural nerve root compression is unrelated to hematoma formation entirely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A crescent-shaped, suture-crossing hematoma in an elderly, anticoagulated patient after minor or unwitnessed trauma is the classic subdural hematoma pattern, distinguished from epidural hematoma by both shape and clinical tempo. COMLEX: Subacute or chronic subdural hematomas can mimic dementia or stroke in elderly patients, making a history of even minor falls essential to elicit.","crossRef":"📖 Identifying urgent neurologic red flags requiring rapid evaluation, since a subacute subdural hematoma presenting as progressive confusion is a red flag easily mistaken for a purely medical decline","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q36","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid hemorrhage using mechanism, vessel, location, imaging, and presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman develops the sudden onset of the worst headache of her life while exercising, describing it as instantaneous and explosive, followed by neck stiffness and photophobia. Noncontrast head CT shows diffuse hyperdensity outlining the basal cisterns and sulci without any parenchymal hematoma. Which mechanism and vessel most likely account for this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Laceration of the middle meningeal artery, releasing blood between the periosteal dura and the skull","B":"Rupture of a circle of Willis berry aneurysm, releasing blood freely into the subarachnoid space","C":"Tearing of cortical bridging veins, releasing blood into the dural border cell layer","D":"Occlusion of the superior sagittal sinus, causing venous congestion and cortical edema","E":"Compression of the cauda equina within the lumbar cistern by an epidural mass"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This presentation, a thunderclap headache with meningismus and CT showing diffuse hyperdensity outlining the basal cisterns and sulci rather than a discrete parenchymal or extra-axial collection, is the classic pattern of subarachnoid hemorrhage from rupture of a berry aneurysm at a circle of Willis branch point, which allows blood to percolate freely throughout the subarachnoid space. Middle meningeal artery laceration produces a lens-shaped epidural collection rather than diffuse cisternal hyperdensity, bridging vein tearing produces a crescent-shaped subdural collection, superior sagittal sinus occlusion produces venous infarction and edema rather than this acute hyperdense hemorrhagic pattern, and cauda equina compression is unrelated to intracranial headache presentations entirely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thunderclap headache with diffuse cisternal hyperdensity on CT is the classic presentation of berry aneurysm rupture and should prompt urgent CT angiography to localize the aneurysm before rebleeding occurs. COMLEX: If the initial CT is performed too early or is equivocal, a lumbar puncture showing persistent blood and xanthochromia across all three tubes confirms the diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 6 — interpreting high-yield CSF patterns in subarachnoid hemorrhage, extending the xanthochromia and three-tube test used to confirm this diagnosis when CT findings are equivocal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q37","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply meningeal anatomy to lumbar puncture, spinal anesthesia, epidural anesthesia, meningitis, herniation syndromes, and meningioma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man presents with progressive headache, vomiting, and ataxia over several weeks. Neurologic examination reveals nystagmus and dysmetria on finger-to-nose testing. The physician suspects a cerebellar mass and is considering a diagnostic lumbar puncture to evaluate for possible leptomeningeal spread of tumor. What is the most appropriate next step before proceeding with the lumbar puncture?","choices":{"A":"Proceed directly with lumbar puncture, since posterior fossa masses do not increase herniation risk","B":"Proceed directly with lumbar puncture, since CSF cytology can only be obtained through this route","C":"Administer intravenous mannitol and then proceed with lumbar puncture without imaging","D":"Obtain neuroimaging first, since a posterior fossa mass raises the risk of tonsillar herniation","E":"Perform the lumbar puncture at the C7-T1 interspace instead of the lumbar region to avoid herniation risk"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This patient's symptoms and examination findings strongly suggest a posterior fossa mass, and lumbar puncture is relatively contraindicated in this setting because sudden withdrawal of lumbar CSF can steepen the pressure gradient between the cranial vault and spinal canal, forcing the cerebellar tonsils through the foramen magnum in a fatal tonsillar herniation, so neuroimaging to assess for mass effect must precede any lumbar puncture. Proceeding directly, whether without imaging or after mannitol alone, ignores this herniation risk rather than addressing it with imaging first, and moving the puncture site to the cervical spine does not eliminate the pressure gradient risk and is not standard lumbar puncture technique regardless.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Imaging before lumbar puncture whenever a posterior fossa or other mass lesion is suspected is one of the most consistently tested safety principles in clinical neuroscience. COMLEX: A funduscopic examination for papilledema at the bedside can provide a rapid clue to elevated intracranial pressure while imaging is being arranged.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 7 — lumbar puncture anatomy and safety, applying the identical contraindication logic from that objective to this posterior fossa mass","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q38","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply meningeal anatomy to lumbar puncture, spinal anesthesia, epidural anesthesia, meningitis, herniation syndromes, and meningioma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has a several-month history of progressive left leg weakness, with little or no facial or arm involvement. MRI shows an extra-axial, dural-based mass along the right parasagittal frontal convexity with a thin linear enhancement extending along the adjacent dura, and the mass appears to compress the underlying superior frontal gyrus. Which additional feature would most strongly support the diagnosis suggested by this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Restricted diffusion throughout the mass, reflecting densely packed malignant cells","B":"Hyperostosis of the overlying skull on bone-windowed CT, reflecting reactive bone thickening beneath the mass","C":"Ring enhancement with a necrotic center, reflecting rapid tumor growth outstripping its blood supply","D":"Multiple enhancing lesions at the gray-white junction, reflecting hematogenous metastatic spread","E":"Diffuse leptomeningeal enhancement along the entire cerebral convexity, reflecting carcinomatous meningitis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This presentation, an extra-axial dural-based mass with a dural tail sign compressing the leg-dominant paracentral region and producing leg-predominant weakness from its parasagittal location near the anterior cerebral artery territory, is classic for meningioma, and reactive hyperostosis of the overlying skull is a well-recognized supportive imaging feature of these arachnoid cap cell tumors. Restricted diffusion and necrotic ring enhancement are instead more typical of aggressive intra-axial tumors such as glioblastoma, multiple enhancing lesions at the gray-white junction suggest hematogenous metastases rather than a single dural-based mass, and diffuse leptomeningeal enhancement describes carcinomatous meningitis rather than a discrete extra-axial mass.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The dural tail sign combined with reactive skull hyperostosis is a classic imaging pairing that confirms an extra-axial, dural-based meningioma rather than an intra-axial glial tumor. COMLEX: Leg-predominant weakness from a parasagittal mass reflects compression of the medial paracentral lobule, the same somatotopic region supplied by the anterior cerebral artery.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — relating anterior cerebral artery cortical territory to expected neurologic deficits, since this parasagittal meningioma's leg-predominant weakness reflects compression of that exact ACA-supplied territory","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q39","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Apply meningeal anatomy to lumbar puncture, spinal anesthesia, epidural anesthesia, meningitis, herniation syndromes, and meningioma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man with a rapidly expanding right temporal lobe hematoma is found to have a fixed and dilated right pupil, complete loss of right eye adduction and elevation, and left-sided hemiparesis. Which mechanism best explains this specific combination of findings?","choices":{"A":"Uncal herniation compressing the right CN III and right cerebral peduncle","B":"Subfalcine herniation compressing the anterior cerebral artery bilaterally","C":"Central herniation compressing the pituitary stalk and hypothalamus symmetrically","D":"Tonsillar herniation compressing the medullary respiratory centers","E":"Upward cerebellar herniation compressing the superior cerebellar peduncles"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This triad of an ipsilateral fixed, dilated pupil with loss of adduction and elevation from oculomotor nerve compression, plus contralateral hemiparesis, is the classic presentation of uncal herniation: the medial temporal lobe is forced over the tentorial edge, directly compressing the ipsilateral oculomotor nerve as it courses along that edge, while the herniating uncus also compresses the ipsilateral cerebral peduncle, whose not-yet-decussated corticospinal fibers produce contralateral (left) hemiparesis. Subfalcine herniation instead produces leg-predominant weakness from ACA compression rather than this pupillary and hemiparetic pattern, central herniation produces bilateral symmetric findings from diencephalic compression rather than this lateralized triad, tonsillar herniation threatens the medulla and vital signs rather than producing this oculomotor and motor pattern, and upward cerebellar herniation compresses the midbrain from below rather than producing this classic downward uncal pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The uncal herniation triad of ipsilateral blown pupil, ipsilateral oculomotor palsy, and contralateral hemiparesis is one of the highest-yield localization patterns tested in clinical neuroscience and demands emergent recognition. COMLEX: Occasionally the contralateral cerebral peduncle is compressed instead, producing a false localizing sign of hemiparesis on the same side as the lesion, a well-known exception worth remembering.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 3 — interpreting cranial nerve findings and relating them to lesions in the cortex, brainstem, cavernous sinus, orbit, skull base, or peripheral nerve, the same localization principle applied here to oculomotor compression from uncal herniation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q40","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Predict symptoms from meningeal irritation, dural innervation, venous sinus thrombosis, and cavernous sinus pathology.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old college student presents with two days of fever, severe headache, and neck stiffness. On examination, passive flexion of the neck produces involuntary flexion of the hips and knees, and passive extension of the flexed knee with the hip flexed produces pain and resistance. Which structure being irritated best accounts for these examination findings?","choices":{"A":"The corticospinal tracts within the lateral columns of the spinal cord","B":"The dorsal columns within the posterior spinal cord","C":"The inflamed, pain-sensitive meninges and nerve roots","D":"The anterior horn cells within the spinal cord gray matter","E":"The peripheral nerve fibers of the lumbosacral plexus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This vignette describes Brudzinski's sign, involuntary hip and knee flexion with neck flexion, and Kernig's sign, pain and resistance with knee extension after hip flexion, both classic findings of meningeal irritation in bacterial meningitis, and these signs arise because inflammation of the pain-sensitive meninges and nerve roots within the subarachnoid space triggers reflexive muscle guarding when stretch is applied to these irritated structures. Corticospinal tract or dorsal column involvement would instead produce weakness or sensory loss rather than these specific stretch-provoked reflex signs, anterior horn cell pathology would produce lower motor neuron weakness rather than meningeal signs, and peripheral lumbosacral plexus pathology would not explain a centrally mediated meningeal irritation sign.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kernig and Brudzinski signs as bedside markers of meningeal irritation from inflamed, pain-sensitive meninges and nerve roots are classic examination findings paired with suspected bacterial meningitis. COMLEX: These signs should prompt immediate empiric antibiotic therapy and blood cultures before awaiting confirmatory lumbar puncture results.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 6 — interpreting high-yield CSF patterns in meningitis, confirming the diagnosis suggested by this classic meningeal irritation examination in the current vignette","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q41","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Predict symptoms from meningeal irritation, dural innervation, venous sinus thrombosis, and cavernous sinus pathology.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman with poorly controlled diabetes develops sinusitis that progresses over several days to fever, severe unilateral headache, and periorbital swelling. She then develops ptosis, a fixed and dilated pupil, complete ophthalmoplegia, and diminished sensation over the forehead and upper cheek, all on the same side. Which single anatomic compartment best explains this complete cluster of findings?","choices":{"A":"The internal acoustic meatus, where cranial nerves VII and VIII travel together","B":"The jugular foramen, where cranial nerves IX, X, and XI travel together","C":"The superior orbital fissure alone, without involvement of any adjacent venous structure","D":"The petrous apex, where the trigeminal ganglion and abducens nerve travel in isolation","E":"The cavernous sinus, where CN III, IV, VI, V1, and V2 run beside the internal carotid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This progression from sinusitis to fever, headache, and then a complete oculomotor, trochlear, and abducens palsy with V1 and V2 sensory loss localizes precisely to the cavernous sinus, the single compartment where all of these structures travel together immediately adjacent to the internal carotid artery, making cavernous sinus thrombosis, often from contiguous spread of sinus or facial infection, the unifying diagnosis. The internal acoustic meatus involves only CN VII and VIII and would not explain this pattern, the jugular foramen involves the lower cranial nerves IX through XI rather than the ocular motor and upper trigeminal nerves described here, the superior orbital fissure alone does not account for the maxillary division sensory loss which exits separately through foramen rotundum, and the petrous apex would not produce this full combination of ocular motor and multi-division trigeminal findings together.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognizing cavernous sinus thrombosis from the combination of multiple ocular motor palsies plus V1 and V2 sensory loss following sinus or facial infection is a high-yield localizing vignette. COMLEX: Diabetic patients are additionally at risk for invasive fungal sinusitis extending into the cavernous sinus, a life-threatening variant that must be considered in this clinical context.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 3 — using cranial nerve findings to localize lesions to the cavernous sinus, directly matching this multi-nerve palsy pattern to that same localization framework","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q42","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Predict symptoms from meningeal irritation, dural innervation, venous sinus thrombosis, and cavernous sinus pathology.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman who is three weeks postpartum presents with a progressively worsening headache, worse when lying flat, along with blurred vision. Fundoscopic examination reveals bilateral papilledema. MR venography shows an absence of flow signal within a major dural sinus running along the vertex. Which mechanism best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Venous sinus thrombosis impairing CSF absorption and venous outflow","B":"Aqueductal stenosis obstructing CSF flow between the third and fourth ventricles","C":"Rupture of a berry aneurysm causing diffuse subarachnoid hemorrhage","D":"Bacterial meningitis producing purulent exudate within the subarachnoid space","E":"Uncal herniation compressing the ipsilateral oculomotor nerve and midbrain"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The postpartum hypercoagulable state is a well-recognized risk factor for dural venous sinus thrombosis, most commonly involving the superior sagittal sinus running along the vertex, and occlusion of this sinus impairs both CSF reabsorption through the arachnoid villi that project into it and normal venous outflow, together raising intracranial pressure and producing headache, positional worsening, and papilledema, with the diagnosis confirmed by absent flow signal on MR venography. Aqueductal stenosis would produce a distinct triventricular hydrocephalus pattern rather than an absent venous flow signal, a ruptured aneurysm would show diffuse subarachnoid hyperdensity rather than a venous flow defect, bacterial meningitis would present with fever and meningismus rather than this venographic finding, and uncal herniation produces a lateralized oculomotor and motor syndrome rather than these more diffuse elevated pressure symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Postpartum or oral contraceptive-associated dural venous sinus thrombosis presenting with headache and papilledema, confirmed by MR venography, is a classic and frequently tested hypercoagulable state vignette. COMLEX: Anticoagulation, rather than antiplatelet therapy, is the standard treatment for venous sinus thrombosis even though intracranial hemorrhage may coexist in some cases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 6 — describing superficial and deep venous drainage and identifying clinically important venous sinus problems, extending this postpartum hypercoagulable presentation to that broader venous drainage framework","isHighYield":true}],"10":[{"id":"sdl10_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Trace blood flow from the aortic arch/carotid and vertebral systems into the circle of Willis.","batch":1,"stem":"The right vertebral artery arises from which vessel before ascending through the transverse foramina of the cervical vertebrae?","choices":{"A":"The right subclavian artery","B":"The brachiocephalic trunk directly","C":"The right common carotid artery","D":"The aortic arch directly","E":"The right internal carotid artery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The right vertebral artery is the first branch of the right subclavian artery, which itself arises from the brachiocephalic trunk; the left vertebral artery likewise usually arises from the left subclavian artery, and only about 5% arise directly from the aortic arch. The brachiocephalic trunk gives rise to the right subclavian and right common carotid arteries but is not itself the direct origin of the vertebral artery, the right common carotid artery instead gives rise to the internal and external carotid arteries, a direct aortic-arch origin is an uncommon variant seen on the left rather than the right, and the internal carotid artery is a separate terminal branch of the common carotid unrelated to vertebral origin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Both vertebral arteries are normally the first branches of the subclavian arteries, which is why proximal subclavian stenosis can reverse vertebral flow (subclavian steal). COMLEX: Subclavian steal syndrome produces vertebrobasilar symptoms on exertion of the affected arm.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — recognizing high-yield cavernous sinus relationships, since the cavernous part of the internal carotid artery traced here runs directly through that same sinus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Trace blood flow from the aortic arch/carotid and vertebral systems into the circle of Willis.","batch":1,"stem":"After piercing the dura, the cerebral part of the internal carotid artery gives rise to the ophthalmic, posterior communicating, and anterior choroidal arteries before terminating in which paired branches?","choices":{"A":"The posterior cerebral artery and the superior cerebellar artery","B":"The basilar artery and the posterior communicating artery","C":"The anterior choroidal artery and the ophthalmic artery","D":"The anterior cerebral artery and the middle cerebral artery","E":"The pericallosal artery and the callosomarginal artery"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The internal carotid artery terminates by bifurcating into its two largest branches, the anterior cerebral artery and the middle cerebral artery, which together supply nearly the entire anterior circulation territory. The posterior cerebral and superior cerebellar arteries instead arise from the basilar artery of the posterior circulation, the basilar artery is itself formed by the vertebral arteries rather than being a terminal ICA branch, the anterior choroidal and ophthalmic arteries are earlier, smaller branches given off before the terminal bifurcation, and the pericallosal and callosomarginal arteries are distal branches of the anterior cerebral artery itself rather than the ICA terminus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knowing the ICA terminal bifurcation into ACA and MCA is essential for reading a carotid angiogram and for understanding why carotid disease produces anterior, not posterior, circulation symptoms. COMLEX: This bifurcation point is also a common site for atherosclerotic plaque and embolic stroke.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — recognizing high-yield cavernous sinus relationships, since the cavernous part of the internal carotid artery traced here runs directly through that same sinus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Trace blood flow from the aortic arch/carotid and vertebral systems into the circle of Willis.","batch":1,"stem":"At the pontomedullary junction, the two V4 segments of the vertebral arteries merge to form which single midline vessel?","choices":{"A":"The anterior spinal artery","B":"The single midline basilar artery","C":"A single common posterior cerebral trunk","D":"The posterior communicating artery","E":"The anterior communicating artery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The right and left V4 segments merge at the pontomedullary junction to form the single midline basilar artery, which then ascends the ventral pons and bifurcates into the two posterior cerebral arteries at its rostral end. The anterior spinal artery instead arises from small paired branches of the V4 segments before their union rather than from the union itself, there is no single common posterior cerebral trunk since the basilar bifurcates into two separate PCAs, and the single anterior communicating artery and the paired posterior communicating arteries are connector vessels of the circle of Willis rather than the product of vertebral artery fusion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The vertebral-to-basilar fusion at the pontomedullary junction is the anatomic basis for the term vertebrobasilar system and explains why bilateral vertebral disease can produce a single midline basilar occlusion syndrome. COMLEX: Basilar artery thrombosis is a life-threatening emergency producing locked-in syndrome, making this junction clinically critical.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — recognizing high-yield cavernous sinus relationships, since the cavernous part of the internal carotid artery traced here runs directly through that same sinus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish anterior circulation from posterior circulation.","batch":1,"stem":"The vertebrobasilar system, forming the posterior circulation, primarily supplies which set of structures?","choices":{"A":"The brainstem, cerebellum, and posterior/inferior cerebrum","B":"The lateral frontal, parietal, and temporal cortex and basal nuclei","C":"The medial frontal and parietal cortex near the longitudinal fissure","D":"The cavernous sinus, orbit, and anterior pituitary gland","E":"The entire cerebral hemisphere excluding the brainstem"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The posterior circulation supplies the brainstem, cerebellum, and the posterior and inferior aspects of the cerebrum, including the occipital lobe and inferior temporal lobe via the terminal posterior cerebral arteries. The lateral frontal, parietal, and temporal cortex with the basal nuclei is instead the territory of the anterior circulation's middle cerebral artery, the medial frontal and parietal cortex near the longitudinal fissure belongs to the anterior cerebral artery, the cavernous sinus and orbit are supplied by carotid branches rather than the vertebrobasilar system, and the entire hemisphere is never supplied by one circulation alone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distinguishing anterior circulation, cerebral hemispheres via the carotids, from posterior circulation, brainstem/cerebellum/occipital lobe via the vertebrobasilar system, is a foundational stroke-localization concept. COMLEX: This distinction underlies why posterior circulation strokes often present with vertigo, diplopia, and ataxia rather than hemiparesis alone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — relating ACA, MCA, and PCA cortical territories to expected deficits, mapping onto the anterior-versus-posterior circulation split distinguished here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish anterior circulation from posterior circulation.","batch":1,"stem":"Which artery is the terminal branch of the posterior circulation that supplies the primary visual cortex along the calcarine sulcus?","choices":{"A":"The middle cerebral artery","B":"The posterior cerebral artery","C":"The anterior cerebral artery","D":"The superior cerebellar artery","E":"The anterior choroidal artery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The posterior cerebral artery is the terminal branch of the basilar artery and supplies the medial occipital lobe, including the calcarine sulcus that contains the primary visual cortex, along with the inferior temporal lobe and portions of the thalamus. The middle and anterior cerebral arteries instead belong to the anterior, carotid-derived circulation and do not reach the occipital pole, the superior cerebellar artery supplies the superior cerebellum rather than visual cortex, and the anterior choroidal artery is a carotid branch supplying the optic tract and internal capsule rather than the occipital cortex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PCA occlusion causing contralateral homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing, due to collateral MCA supply to the occipital pole, is one of the most frequently tested vascular syndromes. COMLEX: Macular sparing is a key clinical clue that localizes the lesion to the occipital cortex rather than to the optic pathway itself.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — relating ACA, MCA, and PCA cortical territories to expected deficits, mapping onto the anterior-versus-posterior circulation split distinguished here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish anterior circulation from posterior circulation.","batch":1,"stem":"Which paired communicating arteries physically link the anterior, carotid-derived circulation to the posterior, vertebrobasilar circulation at the base of the brain?","choices":{"A":"The single anterior communicating artery","B":"The small recurrent artery of Heubner","C":"The superior hypophysial arteries","D":"The posterior communicating arteries","E":"The paired ophthalmic arteries"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The paired posterior communicating arteries connect the internal carotid arteries to the posterior cerebral arteries, forming the actual anastomotic link between anterior and posterior circulation within the circle of Willis. The single anterior communicating artery instead joins the two anterior cerebral arteries to each other rather than linking anterior to posterior circulation, the recurrent artery of Heubner is a small ACA branch supplying the head of the caudate, the superior hypophysial arteries supply the pituitary stalk, and the ophthalmic arteries travel into the orbit rather than joining the two circulations together.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The posterior communicating arteries as the anatomic bridge between carotid and vertebrobasilar systems are essential for understanding collateral flow and are a classic aneurysm site producing an isolated CN III palsy. COMLEX: A posterior communicating artery aneurysm classically presents with a painful, pupil-involving third nerve palsy, distinguishing it from a microvascular, pupil-sparing palsy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — relating ACA, MCA, and PCA cortical territories to expected deficits, mapping onto the anterior-versus-posterior circulation split distinguished here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Predict the major deficits caused by ACA, MCA, PCA, anterior choroidal, lenticulostriate, PICA, AICA, and SCA lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"Occlusion of the anterior cerebral artery classically produces contralateral motor and sensory loss that is most severe in which body region?","choices":{"A":"The lower extremity, leg and foot","B":"The face, upper extremity, and trunk","C":"The upper extremity only, sparing the leg and face","D":"The entire contralateral hemibody equally","E":"The face and tongue with dysarthria"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The anterior cerebral artery supplies the medial paracentral lobule, where the somatotopic homunculus represents the leg and foot, so ACA occlusion produces contralateral weakness and sensory loss that is greatest in the lower extremity. The face, upper extremity, and trunk pattern instead reflects MCA territory involving the lateral homunculus, an upper extremity-only pattern without leg or face involvement is not the classic ACA presentation, equal involvement of the entire hemibody suggests a subcortical lesion such as the internal capsule rather than a cortical ACA infarct, and isolated face and tongue dysarthria points toward a corticobulbar or brainstem process rather than ACA territory.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The leg-predominant motor and sensory deficit of ACA stroke, reflecting the medial homunculus, is one of the most reliably tested territory-deficit pairings. COMLEX: Behavioral changes such as apathy or urinary incontinence may accompany ACA stroke because it also supplies medial frontal lobe structures.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — the same ACA, MCA, and PCA cortical territory-deficit relationships, extended here to anterior choroidal, lenticulostriate, and posterior fossa vessels","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Predict the major deficits caused by ACA, MCA, PCA, anterior choroidal, lenticulostriate, PICA, AICA, and SCA lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"Occlusion of the lenticulostriate branches of the middle cerebral artery classically produces which pattern of deficit?","choices":{"A":"Contralateral homonymous hemianopia sparing the macular region","B":"Ipsilateral ataxia with contralateral pain and temperature loss","C":"Contralateral hemiplegia and hemianesthesia without cortical signs","D":"Contralateral leg weakness accompanied by urinary incontinence","E":"Severe aphasia accompanied by contralateral face and arm weakness noted"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The lenticulostriate arteries are small penetrating branches of the MCA that supply the internal capsule and basal nuclei, and their occlusion produces a pure motor or mixed sensorimotor lacunar stroke with dense contralateral hemiplegia and hemianesthesia but no cortical signs like aphasia or neglect, since the cortex itself is spared. Homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing instead reflects PCA occlusion of the occipital cortex, ipsilateral ataxia with contralateral pain and temperature loss describes lateral medullary syndrome from PICA occlusion, leg weakness with incontinence describes ACA territory, and severe aphasia with face and arm weakness reflects cortical MCA involvement rather than the deep penetrating branches.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lenticulostriate lacunar strokes producing pure motor hemiparesis without cortical signs, unlike cortical MCA strokes, is a heavily tested distinction on both exams. COMLEX: Chronic hypertension is the dominant risk factor for lenticulostriate lacunar infarcts, reinforcing blood pressure control as secondary prevention.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — the same ACA, MCA, and PCA cortical territory-deficit relationships, extended here to anterior choroidal, lenticulostriate, and posterior fossa vessels","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Predict the major deficits caused by ACA, MCA, PCA, anterior choroidal, lenticulostriate, PICA, AICA, and SCA lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"Occlusion of the anterior choroidal artery produces a classic triad including contralateral hemiplegia, contralateral hemianesthesia, and which additional finding?","choices":{"A":"Ipsilateral facial paralysis with hyperacusis","B":"Ipsilateral Horner syndrome with ataxia","C":"Bilateral loss of pain and temperature sensation","D":"Monaural hearing loss with vertigo","E":"Contralateral homonymous hemianopia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The anterior choroidal artery courses along the optic tract before supplying the inferior posterior limb of the internal capsule, so its occlusion classically produces the triad of contralateral hemiplegia, hemianesthesia, and homonymous hemianopia from combined optic tract and internal capsule damage. Ipsilateral facial paralysis with hyperacusis instead describes a facial nerve or AICA-territory lesion, ipsilateral Horner syndrome with ataxia reflects lateral medullary syndrome from PICA occlusion, bilateral pain and temperature loss suggests a central cord lesion rather than an arterial territory, and monaural hearing loss with vertigo is the classic AICA presentation from labyrinthine artery involvement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The anterior choroidal artery syndrome triad, hemiplegia plus hemianesthesia plus hemianopia, is a named, frequently tested syndrome distinct from both MCA and PCA strokes. COMLEX: Recognizing this triad helps localize a lesion to the optic tract and posterior limb of the internal capsule rather than to a purely cortical territory.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — the same ACA, MCA, and PCA cortical territory-deficit relationships, extended here to anterior choroidal, lenticulostriate, and posterior fossa vessels","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the clinical importance of the circle of Willis and common saccular aneurysm sites.","batch":1,"stem":"The circle of Willis functions primarily as which type of anatomic structure at the base of the brain?","choices":{"A":"A rigid dural venous channel draining blood toward the internal jugular vein","B":"A choroid plexus structure that filters plasma to help produce cerebrospinal fluid","C":"A collateral anastomotic ring that can reroute flow if a major vessel slowly occludes","D":"A bony canal that protects the paired vertebral arteries as they ascend the neck","E":"A lymphatic-like channel that clears interstitial waste from the brain parenchyma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The circle of Willis is an anastomotic arterial ring formed by the joined carotid and vertebrobasilar systems, and its clinical importance lies in serving as a collateral bypass that can reroute blood across the communicating arteries when one major vessel undergoes gradual occlusion, preserving perfusion. It is not a dural venous channel, since venous sinuses are a separate structure entirely, it is not related to choroid plexus or CSF production, it is not a bony protective canal like the transverse foramina that shelter the vertebral arteries, and it is not a lymphatic clearance pathway, a role instead attributed to the glymphatic and perivascular systems.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The circle of Willis as a collateral safety net for gradual, but not sudden, vascular occlusion is a core physiologic concept tested alongside its aneurysm-prone branch points. COMLEX: This collateral capacity explains why slowly progressive carotid stenosis may remain asymptomatic while acute embolic occlusion of the same vessel causes a devastating stroke.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 6 — interpreting high-yield CSF patterns in subarachnoid hemorrhage, the classic presentation of ruptured aneurysms at the sites described here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the clinical importance of the circle of Willis and common saccular aneurysm sites.","batch":1,"stem":"The single most common site for an intracranial saccular aneurysm, accounting for roughly 30% to 35% of cases, is which location?","choices":{"A":"The basilar artery bifurcation near the top of the pons","B":"The junction of the PICA with the distal vertebral artery","C":"The M1 segment bifurcation of the middle cerebral artery","D":"The anterior communicating artery or its junction with the ACA","E":"The internal carotid to ophthalmic artery junction site"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The anterior communicating artery, or its junction with the anterior cerebral artery, is the single most common intracranial aneurysm site, accounting for roughly 30% to 35% of cases, and its proximity to the optic chiasm can produce visual field deficits when it expands. The basilar bifurcation is instead the most common site among posterior circulation aneurysms specifically, though far less frequent overall, the PICA-vertebral junction is a less common posterior circulation site, the M1 bifurcation of the MCA accounts for about 20% of cases, and the carotid-ophthalmic junction is a less frequent anterior site associated with monocular visual loss rather than the leading overall location.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anterior communicating artery aneurysms as the leading overall site, associated with visual field defects and subarachnoid hemorrhage into the interhemispheric fissure, are one of the most tested aneurysm facts. COMLEX: Rupture at this site frequently produces blood tracking into the frontal interhemispheric fissure and sometimes intraventricular extension, both visible on noncontrast head CT.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 6 — interpreting high-yield CSF patterns in subarachnoid hemorrhage, the classic presentation of ruptured aneurysms at the sites described here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the clinical importance of the circle of Willis and common saccular aneurysm sites.","batch":1,"stem":"An expanding basilar tip aneurysm classically compresses which cranial nerve as it courses between the PCA and SCA?","choices":{"A":"The oculomotor nerve, or CN III","B":"The trochlear nerve, or CN IV","C":"The abducens nerve, or CN VI","D":"The trigeminal nerve, or CN V","E":"The facial nerve, or CN VII"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The oculomotor nerve, CN III, passes directly between the posterior cerebral and superior cerebellar arteries, so an expanding basilar tip aneurysm at this location compresses CN III, initially producing a dilated, poorly reactive pupil followed by progressive extraocular muscle paralysis and diplopia. The trochlear nerve courses more dorsally around the midbrain and is not the classic vessel-compressed nerve here, the abducens nerve runs along the clivus far from the basilar tip, the trigeminal nerve emerges from the lateral pons rather than the interpeduncular region, and the facial nerve exits at the pontomedullary junction, also distant from the basilar bifurcation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The basilar tip aneurysm compressing CN III between the PCA and SCA, with a blown pupil as the earliest sign, is a classic localizing fact that also appears in uncal herniation. COMLEX: A pupil-involving CN III palsy is a neurosurgical emergency requiring urgent vascular imaging to exclude a compressive aneurysm, unlike a pupil-sparing palsy from microvascular ischemia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 6 — interpreting high-yield CSF patterns in subarachnoid hemorrhage, the classic presentation of ruptured aneurysms at the sites described here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare ischemic stroke, lacunar infarction, subarachnoid hemorrhage, intraparenchymal hemorrhage, epidural hemorrhage, and subdural hemorrhage.","batch":1,"stem":"A lacunar infarction is best defined as a small ischemic stroke caused by occlusion of which type of vessel?","choices":{"A":"A large cortical branch such as the M4 segment of the MCA","B":"A fragile bridging vein at the dura-arachnoid interface","C":"The main trunk of the middle meningeal artery itself","D":"A major dural venous sinus such as the superior sagittal sinus","E":"Tiny, deep penetrating arteries such as the lenticulostriate arteries"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lacunar infarction results from occlusion of tiny, deep penetrating vessels, such as the lenticulostriate or thalamogeniculate arteries, and is strongly associated with chronic hypertension causing lipohyalinosis of these small vessels. Occlusion of a large cortical branch like M4 instead produces a cortical territory infarct with cortical signs rather than a small subcortical lacune, bridging veins and the middle meningeal artery are implicated in subdural and epidural hemorrhage rather than ischemic infarction, and dural venous sinus occlusion causes venous infarction and edema through a very different mechanism than arterial lacunar disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lacunar infarcts from small-vessel hypertensive disease, producing pure motor or pure sensory strokes without cortical signs, are distinguished from large-vessel cortical strokes on both exams. COMLEX: Aggressive blood pressure control is the mainstay of secondary prevention for recurrent lacunar strokes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 5 — differentiating epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid hemorrhage by mechanism, vessel, location, imaging, and presentation, extended here to ischemic and lacunar stroke","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare ischemic stroke, lacunar infarction, subarachnoid hemorrhage, intraparenchymal hemorrhage, epidural hemorrhage, and subdural hemorrhage.","batch":1,"stem":"In an acute occlusive stroke, a central region of brain tissue is permanently lost. What term describes the surrounding zone of ischemic tissue that rapid treatment can still save?","choices":{"A":"The penumbra, at-risk tissue around the core that reperfusion can save","B":"The border zone, tissue lying between two adjacent arterial territories","C":"The lacune, a small cavity left by a deep penetrating artery occlusion","D":"The venous infarct, tissue lost behind an obstructed draining sinus","E":"The core infarct, tissue already necrotic before treatment can begin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"After an occlusive stroke, a central portion of the ischemic area is permanently lost, while the immediately surrounding area, the penumbra, may be salvaged by rapid treatment such as tissue plasminogen activator within 3 to 4.5 hours of onset or endovascular clot removal within about 8 hours; untreated, the penumbra is recruited into the permanent loss. A border zone is the watershed region between two arterial territories that is vulnerable to hypoperfusion. A lacune is a small deep infarct from a penetrating artery. A venous infarct results from venous outflow obstruction, and the core is the part that cannot be saved.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Time is brain: thrombolysis and thrombectomy aim to rescue the penumbra; hemorrhagic stroke must be excluded with CT first because hemorrhage cannot be treated with tPA. COMLEX: Establish the last-known-well time immediately, because eligibility for reperfusion depends on it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 5 — differentiating epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid hemorrhage, the hemorrhagic strokes that must be excluded before reperfusion therapy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare ischemic stroke, lacunar infarction, subarachnoid hemorrhage, intraparenchymal hemorrhage, epidural hemorrhage, and subdural hemorrhage.","batch":1,"stem":"Intraparenchymal hemorrhage caused by severe chronic hypertension most frequently results from rupture of which vessels?","choices":{"A":"The bridging veins draining into the superior sagittal sinus","B":"The middle meningeal artery beneath the pterion","C":"A ruptured berry aneurysm at the anterior communicating artery","D":"The deep lenticulostriate penetrating arteries","E":"The superficial cortical veins over the cerebral convexity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Chronic hypertension damages the small, deep lenticulostriate penetrating arteries, and their rupture causes intraparenchymal hemorrhage with a massive internal hematoma, classically located in the basal ganglia, thalamus, pons, or cerebellum. Bridging veins and cortical veins are instead the source of subdural bleeding rather than deep parenchymal hemorrhage, the middle meningeal artery is the classic epidural hemorrhage vessel following trauma, and a ruptured anterior communicating artery aneurysm produces subarachnoid rather than intraparenchymal hemorrhage as its primary compartment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypertensive intraparenchymal hemorrhage in the basal ganglia, thalamus, pons, or cerebellum from lenticulostriate or similar penetrating vessel rupture is distinct from lobar hemorrhage, which raises suspicion for amyloid angiopathy in older patients. COMLEX: Blood pressure management remains central to both acute treatment and long-term prevention of hypertensive intraparenchymal hemorrhage.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 5 — differentiating epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid hemorrhage by mechanism, vessel, location, imaging, and presentation, extended here to ischemic and lacunar stroke","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe superficial and deep venous drainage and identify clinically important venous sinus problems.","batch":1,"stem":"The superficial middle cerebral vein, which drains the lateral surface of the hemisphere, empties directly into which structure?","choices":{"A":"The superior sagittal sinus","B":"The straight sinus","C":"The great cerebral vein of Galen","D":"The internal jugular vein directly","E":"The cavernous sinus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The superficial middle cerebral vein courses medially around the temporal pole and drains directly into the cavernous sinus, distinguishing it from the superior cerebral veins that instead drain into the superior sagittal sinus. The superior sagittal sinus receives superior cerebral veins rather than the superficial middle cerebral vein, the straight sinus receives the great cerebral vein of Galen and inferior sagittal sinus rather than superficial hemispheric veins, the great cerebral vein of Galen drains deep structures via the internal cerebral and basal veins, and the internal jugular vein receives blood only after it has already passed through the dural sinuses rather than directly from cortical veins.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knowing that the superficial middle cerebral vein drains into the cavernous sinus explains how superficial cortical infections or thrombophlebitis can propagate into the cavernous sinus. COMLEX: This same venous connection is relevant when cavernous sinus thrombosis produces retrograde congestion visible in superficial facial and orbital veins.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — explaining how dural folds and dural venous sinuses form, the same venous channels whose drainage patterns and thrombosis risk are described here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe superficial and deep venous drainage and identify clinically important venous sinus problems.","batch":1,"stem":"The paired internal cerebral veins, draining deep hemispheric structures, join in the midline to form which single venous channel?","choices":{"A":"The basal vein of Rosenthal directly","B":"The great cerebral vein of Galen","C":"The straight sinus itself directly","D":"The posterior confluence of sinuses","E":"The superior sagittal sinus alone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The two internal cerebral veins join in the midline to form the great cerebral vein of Galen, which then continues caudally into the straight sinus, making it the key deep venous confluence of the brain. The basal vein of Rosenthal instead forms from the union of the anterior cerebral and deep middle cerebral veins and joins the vein of Galen rather than being formed by the internal cerebral veins directly, the straight sinus is the next downstream channel rather than the point of union itself, the confluence of sinuses is a more posterior junction of the straight, superior sagittal, and transverse sinuses, and the superior sagittal sinus drains superficial rather than deep venous structures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The great cerebral vein of Galen as the confluence point of the paired internal cerebral veins is essential for understanding deep venous drainage and vein of Galen malformations in neonates. COMLEX: A vein of Galen malformation can cause high-output cardiac failure in infants due to massive arteriovenous shunting into this venous structure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — explaining how dural folds and dural venous sinuses form, the same venous channels whose drainage patterns and thrombosis risk are described here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe superficial and deep venous drainage and identify clinically important venous sinus problems.","batch":1,"stem":"Superficial cerebral veins on the lateral surface of the hemisphere are linked to the dural sinuses by large anastomotic channels. Which vein connects the superficial middle cerebral vein with the transverse sinus?","choices":{"A":"The superior anastomotic vein of Trolard","B":"The great cerebral vein of Galen","C":"The inferior anastomotic vein of Labbé","D":"The basal vein of Rosenthal","E":"The internal cerebral vein"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The cerebral veins on the lateral surface of the hemisphere drain into the superior sagittal and transverse sinuses through two large anastomotic veins: the superior anastomotic vein of Trolard runs to the superior sagittal sinus, and the inferior anastomotic vein of Labbé runs to the transverse sinus; both connect with the superficial middle cerebral vein, which itself ends in the cavernous sinus. The great cerebral vein of Galen receives the internal cerebral and basal veins and continues as the straight sinus. The basal vein of Rosenthal drains the orbital cortex, insula, and medial temporal lobe into the great cerebral vein. The internal cerebral veins drain deep hemispheric structures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trolard runs up to the superior sagittal sinus; Labbé runs down and back to the transverse sinus; the superficial middle cerebral vein itself ends in the cavernous sinus. COMLEX: Because these superficial veins interconnect the sinuses, a thrombus in one sinus can impair drainage of a wide cortical region.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — explaining how dural folds and dural venous sinuses form, the same venous channels whose drainage patterns are described here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use a stroke-localization algorithm to move from neurologic deficit to likely vascular territory.","batch":1,"stem":"In the stroke-localization algorithm, contralateral arm and lower face weakness accompanied by aphasia should be localized to which vascular territory?","choices":{"A":"The anterior cerebral artery","B":"The middle cerebral artery","C":"The posterior cerebral artery","D":"The posterior inferior cerebellar artery","E":"The internal capsule via lenticulostriate vessels"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Contralateral arm and lower facial weakness with language dysfunction localizes to the MCA territory, which supplies the lateral convexity including the motor and language cortex. The ACA territory instead produces leg-predominant, not arm and face, deficits, the PCA territory produces visual field loss rather than motor weakness with aphasia, the PICA territory produces crossed brainstem signs rather than cortical language deficits, and a pure lenticulostriate lesion of the internal capsule produces dense hemiplegia without cortical signs like aphasia, distinguishing it from a cortical MCA stroke.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Following the localization algorithm from deficit pattern to territory, arm/face plus aphasia points to MCA, is the exact logic tested in vignette-based stroke questions. COMLEX: This same algorithm helps distinguish cortical strokes, which have cognitive or language signs, from purely subcortical lacunar strokes.","crossRef":"📎 Constructing a concise neurologic localization statement from a vignette, the same skill applied here to move from deficit to vascular territory","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use a stroke-localization algorithm to move from neurologic deficit to likely vascular territory.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the stroke-localization algorithm, an ipsilateral CN III palsy combined with contralateral hemiplegia should be localized to which region and syndrome?","choices":{"A":"The lateral medulla, consistent with Wallenberg syndrome","B":"The pons, consistent with locked-in syndrome","C":"The internal capsule, consistent with a pure lacunar stroke","D":"The cerebellum, consistent with an SCA infarct","E":"The midbrain, consistent with Weber syndrome"},"correct":"E","explanation":"An ipsilateral CN III palsy with contralateral hemiplegia is the classic alternating, or crossed, sign of Weber syndrome, localizing to the midbrain because the exiting oculomotor nerve is uncrossed while the descending corticospinal fibers that will later cross are affected on the same side before decussation. The lateral medulla and Wallenberg syndrome instead present with crossed facial and body pain-temperature loss rather than a CN III palsy, locked-in syndrome from pontine injury causes quadriplegia with preserved consciousness and vertical eye movement rather than an isolated CN III finding, a pure lacunar internal capsule stroke produces dense contralateral hemiplegia without any cranial nerve finding, and cerebellar SCA infarcts produce ipsilateral ataxia rather than a crossed brainstem pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognizing alternating brainstem syndromes, a cranial nerve deficit on one side plus a long-tract deficit on the other, as the hallmark of brainstem localization is one of the highest-yield neuroanatomy concepts tested. COMLEX: Weber syndrome results from paramedian branches of the PCA or basilar tip affecting the ventral midbrain, tying directly into aneurysm and stroke localization at that site.","crossRef":"📎 Constructing a concise neurologic localization statement from a vignette, the same skill applied here to move from deficit to vascular territory","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use a stroke-localization algorithm to move from neurologic deficit to likely vascular territory.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient presenting with ipsilateral limb ataxia, intention tremor, and dysmetria without weakness should be localized to a lesion in which vascular distribution?","choices":{"A":"The middle cerebral artery cortical territory","B":"The anterior cerebral artery territory","C":"A cerebellar artery territory","D":"The anterior choroidal artery territory","E":"The lenticulostriate penetrating vessels"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ataxia, intention tremor, and dysmetria without accompanying weakness point to a cerebellar lesion, which the algorithm assigns ipsilateral to the deficit and localizes to one of the cerebellar arteries, SCA, AICA, or PICA, depending on associated findings such as hearing loss or crossed sensory signs. MCA cortical territory instead produces contralateral motor, sensory, or language deficits rather than isolated ipsilateral ataxia, ACA territory produces contralateral leg weakness rather than cerebellar signs, the anterior choroidal artery produces a hemiplegia-hemianesthesia-hemianopia triad rather than isolated ataxia, and lenticulostriate occlusion produces pure motor or sensorimotor lacunar stroke without cerebellar findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognizing that cerebellar signs are ipsilateral to the lesion, unlike cortical and most long-tract signs, is a frequently missed but heavily tested localization rule. COMLEX: Distinguishing which cerebellar artery is involved by associated findings, deafness for AICA, crossed sensory loss for PICA, is the next step once a cerebellar lesion is suspected.","crossRef":"📎 Constructing a concise neurologic localization statement from a vignette, the same skill applied here to move from deficit to vascular territory","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q22","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Trace blood flow from the aortic arch/carotid and vertebral systems into the circle of Willis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man undergoes catheter angiography for evaluation of transient vertigo and diplopia. The catheter is threaded from the femoral artery through the aortic arch and enters a vessel that ascends through the transverse foramina of C6 through C1 before entering the cranial cavity through the foramen magnum. Which vessel is being catheterized?","choices":{"A":"The internal carotid artery","B":"The vertebral artery","C":"The external carotid artery","D":"The posterior communicating artery","E":"The ascending pharyngeal artery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The vertebral artery ascends through the transverse foramina of C6 to C1 and enters the cranial cavity through the foramen magnum before joining its counterpart to form the basilar artery, matching this description precisely. The internal carotid artery instead enters the skull base through the carotid canal rather than the foramen magnum and does not travel through cervical transverse foramina, the external carotid artery remains extracranial and supplies the face and scalp, the posterior communicating artery is an intracranial connector rather than a cervical ascending vessel, and the ascending pharyngeal artery is a small external carotid branch supplying the pharynx rather than the brain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The vertebral artery's characteristic course through the cervical transverse foramina and foramen magnum is a frequently tested angiographic landmark. COMLEX: This vertebral course through mobile cervical vertebrae is also why cervical spine manipulation carries a rare but serious risk of vertebral artery dissection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — dural venous sinuses and cavernous sinus, since the ICA that also enters the skull base runs through that same sinus after piercing the dura","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q23","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Trace blood flow from the aortic arch/carotid and vertebral systems into the circle of Willis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation is found to have an embolus lodged in a vessel that, after entering the carotid canal and traversing the cavernous sinus, pierced the dura just anterior to the optic nerve. Which named part of a specific artery is being described?","choices":{"A":"The cerebral part of the internal carotid artery","B":"The cervical part of the internal carotid artery","C":"The petrous part of the internal carotid artery","D":"The M1 segment of the middle cerebral artery","E":"The A1 segment of the anterior cerebral artery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The cerebral part of the internal carotid artery begins where the vessel pierces the dura just anterior to the optic nerve, after its cervical, petrous, and cavernous parts, and this segment gives off the ophthalmic, posterior communicating, and anterior choroidal arteries before terminating in the ACA and MCA. The cervical part is the earlier neck segment before the carotid canal, the petrous part travels within the carotid canal itself without dural penetration, the M1 segment is a distal branch of the MCA well beyond the dural crossing, and the A1 segment is the proximal ACA segment arising after the terminal ICA bifurcation rather than describing the dural crossing point itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sequencing the ICA's cervical, petrous, cavernous, and cerebral parts in order is essential for correlating imaging findings with the level of an embolic or dissecting lesion. COMLEX: Emboli in atrial fibrillation preferentially travel into the larger-caliber anterior circulation, making ICA territory strokes common in this population.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — dural venous sinuses and cavernous sinus, the same sinus this artery's cavernous part traverses before piercing the dura","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q24","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Trace blood flow from the aortic arch/carotid and vertebral systems into the circle of Willis.","batch":2,"stem":"A vascular surgeon is tracing the path of blood flow into the circle of Willis on a diagram for a resident. She points to the paired arteries that arise at the pons–midbrain junction, in the interpeduncular cistern, as the basilar artery bifurcates. Which arteries is she indicating?","choices":{"A":"The posterior inferior cerebellar arteries","B":"The superior cerebellar arteries","C":"The posterior cerebral arteries","D":"The anterior inferior cerebellar arteries","E":"The posterior communicating arteries"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The basilar artery ascends the ventral pons and bifurcates in the interpeduncular cistern into the paired posterior cerebral arteries, which then complete the posterior portion of the circle of Willis and are joined to the internal carotid system via the posterior communicating arteries. The posterior inferior cerebellar arteries instead arise from the vertebral arteries near the medulla rather than from the basilar bifurcation, the superior cerebellar arteries arise just proximal to the basilar bifurcation rather than from the bifurcation point itself, the anterior inferior cerebellar arteries arise from the mid-basilar artery, and the posterior communicating arteries are separate connector vessels linking the PCAs to the internal carotid system rather than being the terminal basilar branches themselves.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ordering the basilar artery's branches from caudal to rostral, AICA, then SCA, then terminal PCA bifurcation, is a classic sequencing question for posterior circulation anatomy. COMLEX: A basilar tip occlusion simultaneously threatens both PCA territories, producing bilateral occipital infarction with cortical blindness.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — PCA cortical territory, the same terminal branch traced here from the basilar artery bifurcation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q25","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish anterior circulation from posterior circulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman presents with acute vertigo, diplopia, dysarthria, and left-sided limb ataxia. There is no facial droop, no arm or leg weakness on formal strength testing, and no aphasia. Based on this constellation, which circulation is most likely involved?","choices":{"A":"The anterior circulation via the internal carotid","B":"The anterior circulation via the middle cerebral artery","C":"The anterior circulation via the anterior cerebral artery","D":"A combination of anterior and posterior circulation equally","E":"The posterior circulation via the vertebrobasilar system"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Vertigo, diplopia, dysarthria, and ataxia without hemiparesis or aphasia are the classic constellation of posterior circulation dysfunction, reflecting brainstem and cerebellar involvement supplied by the vertebrobasilar system. The internal carotid and middle cerebral artery territories of the anterior circulation instead typically produce contralateral hemiparesis, sensory loss, or aphasia rather than this brainstem-cerebellar pattern, the anterior cerebral artery territory produces leg-predominant weakness rather than vertigo and diplopia, and there is no clinical indication here of combined anterior and posterior involvement since the findings are purely referable to the posterior fossa.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognizing the vertigo-diplopia-dysarthria-ataxia symptom cluster as posterior circulation, sometimes summarized with the mnemonic of the five Ds, is essential for triaging suspected stroke to the correct vascular imaging. COMLEX: Distinguishing posterior circulation stroke from benign peripheral vertigo is a critical emergency department skill, since missed posterior strokes carry high morbidity.","crossRef":"📖 Recognizing cerebellar and vestibular gait and exam patterns, the same posterior circulation findings localized here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q26","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish anterior circulation from posterior circulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man develops sudden right arm weakness, right facial droop, and difficulty producing fluent speech. Imaging confirms an infarct sparing the occipital lobe and cerebellum entirely. Which circulation was most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"The posterior circulation via the basilar artery","B":"The anterior circulation via the internal carotid system","C":"The posterior circulation via the vertebral artery","D":"The posterior circulation via the posterior cerebral artery","E":"A watershed zone between two posterior circulation vessels"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Right arm weakness, facial droop, and nonfluent speech reflect anterior circulation involvement, specifically the middle cerebral artery territory supplying the lateral convexity and language cortex, while the occipital lobe and cerebellum are spared because they belong to the posterior circulation. Basilar and vertebral artery involvement would instead be expected to affect the occipital lobe, cerebellum, or brainstem rather than sparing them, PCA involvement would produce visual field loss rather than arm weakness and aphasia, and a posterior watershed zone would still typically involve visual or occipital-adjacent territory rather than sparing it entirely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Localizing arm/face weakness plus aphasia to the anterior, MCA, circulation while confirming that occipital and cerebellar structures are uninvolved reinforces the anterior-versus-posterior distinction on imaging. COMLEX: This distinction guides which vascular territory to image first, carotid duplex for anterior symptoms versus CT/MR angiography of the posterior circulation for brainstem symptoms.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — MCA cortical territory and its expected deficits, the same anterior circulation pattern localized here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q27","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish anterior circulation from posterior circulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with poorly controlled hypertension presents with sudden left homonymous hemianopia but no weakness, no sensory loss, and no aphasia. Which circulation is most likely responsible for her deficit?","choices":{"A":"The anterior circulation via the middle cerebral artery","B":"The anterior circulation via the anterior cerebral artery","C":"The posterior circulation via the posterior cerebral artery","D":"The anterior circulation via the anterior choroidal artery","E":"The anterior circulation via lenticulostriate branches"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Isolated homonymous hemianopia without motor, sensory, or language deficit points to an occipital lobe lesion supplied by the posterior cerebral artery, part of the posterior circulation, sparing the anterior circulation structures responsible for motor, sensory, and language function. MCA territory would instead be expected to cause motor, sensory, or language deficits alongside any visual change, ACA territory affects leg motor and sensory function rather than vision, the anterior choroidal artery produces a hemiplegia-hemianesthesia-hemianopia triad rather than isolated hemianopia, and lenticulostriate branch occlusion produces pure motor or sensorimotor deficits without any visual field loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Isolated homonymous hemianopia as a posterior circulation, PCA territory, finding without accompanying motor or sensory loss is a classic exam pearl distinguishing occipital from parietal-lobe visual field defects. COMLEX: Hypertension is a major risk factor for PCA territory stroke just as it is for anterior circulation and lacunar stroke, reinforcing blood pressure control across all stroke subtypes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — PCA cortical territory producing homonymous hemianopia, the same posterior circulation deficit localized here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q28","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Predict the major deficits caused by ACA, MCA, PCA, anterior choroidal, lenticulostriate, PICA, AICA, and SCA lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man develops sudden onset of ipsilateral facial pain and temperature loss, contralateral body pain and temperature loss, hoarseness, dysphagia, ipsilateral ptosis and miosis, and ipsilateral limb ataxia. Occlusion of which vessel most likely explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"The anterior inferior cerebellar artery branch","B":"The posterior inferior cerebellar artery","C":"The superior cerebellar artery branch","D":"The anterior spinal artery trunk","E":"The middle cerebral artery trunk"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This constellation is the classic lateral medullary, or Wallenberg, syndrome from PICA occlusion, combining crossed pain-temperature loss, nucleus ambiguus dysfunction causing hoarseness and dysphagia, ipsilateral Horner syndrome from sympathetic fiber involvement, and ipsilateral cerebellar ataxia. AICA occlusion instead produces lateral pontine syndrome with monaural hearing loss and ipsilateral facial paralysis rather than this medullary pattern, superior cerebellar artery occlusion produces severe ipsilateral cerebellar signs without crossed sensory loss or bulbar findings, anterior spinal artery occlusion produces bilateral motor and dorsal column-sparing deficits rather than this crossed sensory pattern, and MCA occlusion produces cortical signs like aphasia and hemiparesis rather than crossed brainstem findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Wallenberg syndrome from PICA occlusion, the single most tested brainstem vascular syndrome, combines crossed sensory loss, bulbar signs, Horner syndrome, and ataxia. First Aid mnemonic: don't pick a PICA, lame, lateral medullary syndrome, horse, hoarseness, that can't eat, dysphagia. COMLEX: Dysphagia in Wallenberg syndrome carries aspiration risk, making early swallow evaluation an important management step.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 3 — interpreting cranial nerve findings to localize brainstem lesions, the same nucleus ambiguus and sympathetic pathway findings localized here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q29","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Predict the major deficits caused by ACA, MCA, PCA, anterior choroidal, lenticulostriate, PICA, AICA, and SCA lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old woman develops sudden left-sided monaural hearing loss, left facial paralysis, left facial sensory loss, and an inability to look toward the left side. There is no crossed sensory loss and no vertigo-associated Horner syndrome. Which artery territory is most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"The posterior inferior cerebellar artery","B":"The anterior inferior cerebellar artery","C":"The superior cerebellar artery","D":"The middle cerebral artery","E":"The anterior choroidal artery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AICA supplies the lateral pons, middle cerebellar peduncle, and inner ear via its labyrinthine branch, so its occlusion classically produces ipsilateral monaural hearing loss along with ipsilateral facial paralysis, facial sensory loss, and gaze paresis toward the lesion side. PICA occlusion instead produces crossed pain-temperature loss with Horner syndrome and dysphagia rather than hearing loss and facial paralysis, superior cerebellar artery occlusion produces severe ataxia and dysmetria without hearing loss or facial paralysis, MCA occlusion produces cortical deficits like aphasia rather than a brainstem or inner ear pattern, and the anterior choroidal artery produces the hemiplegia-hemianesthesia-hemianopia triad rather than hearing loss and facial weakness.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AICA syndrome uniquely combines cerebellopontine angle signs, hearing loss and facial paralysis, with lateral pontine findings, distinguishing it from PICA's medullary pattern. COMLEX: The labyrinthine artery branch of AICA explains why AICA infarcts, unlike PICA infarcts, characteristically involve sudden hearing loss.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 3 — interpreting cranial nerve findings to localize brainstem lesions, the same facial and vestibulocochlear findings localized here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q30","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Predict the major deficits caused by ACA, MCA, PCA, anterior choroidal, lenticulostriate, PICA, AICA, and SCA lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man develops sudden profound left-sided limb ataxia, intention tremor, and dysmetria after an episode of severe vertigo, but he has no hearing loss, no facial weakness, and no crossed sensory findings. Which artery is most likely occluded?","choices":{"A":"The anterior inferior cerebellar artery","B":"The posterior inferior cerebellar artery","C":"The superior cerebellar artery","D":"The posterior communicating artery","E":"The anterior communicating artery"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The superior cerebellar artery supplies the superior cerebellum and deep cerebellar nuclei, and its occlusion typically produces profound ipsilateral cerebellar ataxia, intention tremor, and dysmetria without the hearing loss of AICA occlusion or the crossed sensory and bulbar findings of PICA occlusion. AICA occlusion instead adds monaural hearing loss and facial weakness, which are absent here, PICA occlusion adds crossed pain-temperature loss and dysphagia, which are also absent, and the posterior and anterior communicating arteries are circle of Willis connector vessels whose occlusion does not produce isolated cerebellar territory infarcts.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SCA syndrome as severe cerebellar ataxia and dysmetria without hearing loss, facial weakness, or crossed sensory signs (any pain and temperature loss is contralateral in both face and body) completes the three named cerebellar vascular syndromes alongside AICA and PICA. COMLEX: Differentiating SCA from AICA and PICA syndromes by associated cranial nerve and sensory findings is a classic three-way discrimination tested on boards.","crossRef":"📖 Recognizing cerebellar and vestibular gait patterns, the same territory-specific SCA pattern localized here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q31","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the clinical importance of the circle of Willis and common saccular aneurysm sites.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman presents with a sudden severe headache and is found on angiography to have an aneurysm at the junction of the internal carotid and posterior communicating arteries. Which additional finding would most specifically suggest that the aneurysm is compressing an adjacent cranial nerve?","choices":{"A":"Bilateral papilledema without a focal cranial nerve finding","B":"Contralateral homonymous hemianopia","C":"Bilateral lower extremity weakness with urinary retention","D":"Ipsilateral monaural hearing loss with facial numbness","E":"A dilated, poorly reactive pupil with ptosis on the same side"},"correct":"E","explanation":"An aneurysm at the ICA-posterior communicating artery junction lies immediately adjacent to the oculomotor nerve, so its expansion classically compresses CN III, producing a dilated, poorly reactive pupil with ptosis and eventually ophthalmoplegia on the same side, a painful pupil-involving palsy that demands urgent vascular imaging. Bilateral papilledema without a focal finding instead suggests generalized elevated intracranial pressure rather than focal nerve compression, contralateral homonymous hemianopia would localize to the occipital cortex or optic tract rather than to this ICA-junction aneurysm, bilateral leg weakness with urinary retention suggests a spinal or cauda equina process, and monaural hearing loss with facial numbness would instead suggest a cerebellopontine angle process such as a vestibular schwannoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The ICA-posterior communicating artery aneurysm compressing CN III, producing a painful pupil-involving third nerve palsy, is distinguished from the pupil-sparing palsy of diabetic microvascular ischemia. COMLEX: Any new pupil-involving third nerve palsy should be considered an aneurysm until proven otherwise and warrants emergent CT or MR angiography.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 5 — differentiating subarachnoid hemorrhage from other hemorrhage types, since this ruptured aneurysm site is a classic cause of SAH presenting with sudden severe headache","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q32","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the clinical importance of the circle of Willis and common saccular aneurysm sites.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man with a ruptured aneurysm at the anterior communicating artery undergoes head CT, which shows blood tracking through a specific midline fissure between the frontal lobes. Which anatomic feature explains why blood localizes to this particular fissure?","choices":{"A":"The anterior communicating artery lies within the sylvian fissure near the limen insulae","B":"The anterior communicating artery lies within the posterior fossa near the cerebellopontine angle itself","C":"The anterior communicating artery lies within the interhemispheric fissure near the lamina terminalis","D":"The anterior communicating artery lies within the quadrigeminal cistern near the pineal gland","E":"The anterior communicating artery lies within the cavernous sinus near the pituitary stalk"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The anterior communicating artery and the adjacent A1 segments of the anterior cerebral arteries lie within the cistern of the lamina terminalis, located in the interhemispheric fissure between the frontal lobes, so rupture at this site characteristically produces blood tracking through that midline fissure and sometimes into the ventricles. The sylvian fissure and insula are instead related to the middle cerebral artery bifurcation rather than the anterior communicating artery, the posterior fossa and cerebellopontine angle relate to vertebrobasilar structures, the quadrigeminal cistern near the pineal gland is unrelated to this anterior circle of Willis vessel, and the cavernous sinus near the pituitary stalk is traversed by the cavernous ICA rather than the anterior communicating artery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognizing that anterior communicating artery aneurysms bleed specifically into the interhemispheric fissure helps interpret noncontrast CT patterns of subarachnoid hemorrhage and predict the likely aneurysm location before angiography. COMLEX: This same anatomic proximity to the anterior hypothalamus and optic chiasm explains occasional endocrine or visual complications after aneurysm rupture or clipping at this site.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 6 — interpreting high-yield CSF and CT patterns in subarachnoid hemorrhage, the same bleeding pattern explained here by aneurysm location","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q33","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the clinical importance of the circle of Willis and common saccular aneurysm sites.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with long-standing but well-controlled hypertension is found incidentally to have a 4-mm outpouching at the M1 bifurcation of the middle cerebral artery on MR angiography obtained for an unrelated headache. He is asymptomatic. Which statement best describes the clinical significance of this finding?","choices":{"A":"It is a fusiform aneurysm that rarely ruptures regardless of its overall size or location","B":"It is a mycotic aneurysm requiring urgent intravenous antibiotic therapy","C":"It is a normal anatomic variant carrying essentially no future rupture risk","D":"It is a saccular aneurysm at a common branch point requiring monitoring for growth","E":"It is an arteriovenous malformation requiring urgent embolization therapy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This 4-mm outpouching at the M1 bifurcation is a saccular, or berry, aneurysm at one of the three most common intracranial aneurysm sites, and even when incidental and asymptomatic it requires monitoring for growth or rupture risk given the eventual danger of subarachnoid hemorrhage. Fusiform aneurysms instead involve elongated segments of vessel rather than a discrete branch-point outpouching, and describing this lesion as rarely rupturing regardless of size would be inaccurate risk counseling, a mycotic aneurysm results from septic embolization and infection rather than simple branch-point wall weakness, calling it a normal variant with no rupture risk ignores the well-established risk associated with saccular aneurysms, and an arteriovenous malformation is a congenital tangle of vessels lacking an intervening capillary bed, a fundamentally different lesion from a discrete aneurysm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Saccular aneurysms at the M1 bifurcation, one of the three classic ICA-system sites along with the ACom and ICA-PCom junctions, require ongoing monitoring even when found incidentally. COMLEX: Incidental aneurysm management balances rupture risk based on size and location against the risks of surgical or endovascular intervention, a common shared decision-making scenario.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 5 — differentiating subarachnoid hemorrhage from other hemorrhage types, since this vessel's rupture would be the mechanism of that hemorrhage","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q34","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare ischemic stroke, lacunar infarction, subarachnoid hemorrhage, intraparenchymal hemorrhage, epidural hemorrhage, and subdural hemorrhage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with atrial fibrillation develops sudden right-sided weakness. CT shows no hemorrhage, and MRI later confirms a wedge-shaped area of restricted diffusion in the left MCA territory. Which mechanism best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Rupture of a deep penetrating vessel causing intraparenchymal hemorrhage","B":"Tearing of bridging veins causing subdural hemorrhage","C":"Rupture of a saccular aneurysm causing subarachnoid hemorrhage","D":"Laceration of the middle meningeal artery causing epidural hemorrhage","E":"Embolic occlusion of a cerebral vessel causing ischemic infarction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A wedge-shaped area of restricted diffusion in a discrete arterial territory without hemorrhage, in a patient with a known embolic source such as atrial fibrillation, is the classic pattern of ischemic stroke from embolic vessel occlusion causing tissue infarction. Intraparenchymal hemorrhage from penetrating vessel rupture would instead show as an area of hemorrhage on CT rather than restricted diffusion without bleeding, subdural hemorrhage from bridging vein tearing would appear as a crescent-shaped extra-axial collection rather than a wedge-shaped intraparenchymal territory, subarachnoid hemorrhage from aneurysm rupture would show blood in the cisterns rather than a wedge infarct, and epidural hemorrhage from middle meningeal artery laceration would appear as a lens-shaped extra-axial collection following trauma rather than an ischemic territorial pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distinguishing ischemic stroke, a wedge-shaped diffusion-restricting infarct without hemorrhage, from the various hemorrhagic patterns is a foundational neuroimaging skill. COMLEX: Atrial fibrillation as a cardioembolic source for territorial ischemic stroke reinforces the importance of anticoagulation in secondary stroke prevention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 5 — differentiating epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid hemorrhage by imaging pattern, contrasted here with the non-hemorrhagic ischemic stroke pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q35","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare ischemic stroke, lacunar infarction, subarachnoid hemorrhage, intraparenchymal hemorrhage, epidural hemorrhage, and subdural hemorrhage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman with poorly controlled hypertension develops sudden pure motor weakness of the face, arm, and leg without any sensory loss, visual change, or language deficit. MRI shows a small punctate infarct in the posterior limb of the internal capsule. Which classification best fits this event?","choices":{"A":"A large territorial ischemic stroke from embolic occlusion of the MCA trunk","B":"An intraparenchymal hemorrhage from lenticulostriate vessel rupture","C":"A subarachnoid hemorrhage from a ruptured saccular aneurysm","D":"A lacunar infarction from small penetrating vessel occlusion","E":"A subdural hemorrhage from torn bridging veins"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A small, punctate internal capsule infarct producing pure motor hemiparesis without cortical signs, in a patient with chronic hypertension, is the classic presentation of lacunar infarction from occlusion of a tiny penetrating vessel such as a lenticulostriate branch. A large territorial MCA trunk occlusion would instead be expected to produce cortical signs like aphasia or neglect in addition to a much larger infarct volume, intraparenchymal hemorrhage would appear as frank hemorrhage on imaging rather than restricted diffusion consistent with ischemia, subarachnoid hemorrhage would show blood in the cisterns with a sudden severe headache rather than a punctate capsular infarct, and subdural hemorrhage would appear as a crescent-shaped extra-axial collection rather than a small deep parenchymal infarct.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The pure motor hemiparesis lacunar syndrome from a small internal capsule infarct, without cortical signs, is one of the most heavily tested lacunar stroke patterns. COMLEX: Chronic hypertension management is the key modifiable risk factor to prevent recurrent lacunar strokes of this type.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 5 — differentiating epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid hemorrhage, contrasted here with lacunar ischemic infarction as a distinct, non-hemorrhagic small-vessel entity","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q36","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare ischemic stroke, lacunar infarction, subarachnoid hemorrhage, intraparenchymal hemorrhage, epidural hemorrhage, and subdural hemorrhage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy has a sudden headache followed by a focal seizure and left arm weakness. He has no history of hypertension, trauma, or drug use. CT shows a right frontal intraparenchymal hematoma, and angiography shows a tangle of abnormal vessels fed by enlarged arteries draining directly into enlarged veins. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the hemorrhage?","choices":{"A":"Rupture of an arteriovenous malformation","B":"Rupture of a saccular aneurysm at the circle","C":"Rupture of lenticulostriate penetrating arteries","D":"Tearing of bridging veins at the sagittal sinus","E":"Laceration of the middle meningeal artery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Arteriovenous malformations are congenital tangles of vessels in which arteries connect to veins without an intervening capillary bed. The most common presentation is hemorrhage, followed by seizure and mass effect, and the most common age for hemorrhage is about 15 to 20 years, matching this teenager's intraparenchymal bleed with a seizure. A saccular aneurysm at the circle of Willis classically produces subarachnoid, not lobar intraparenchymal, hemorrhage. Hypertensive rupture of lenticulostriate arteries causes deep basal ganglia hemorrhage in older adults with hypertension. Bridging vein tears produce a crescent-shaped subdural hematoma, and middle meningeal artery laceration produces an epidural hematoma after skull fracture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Intraparenchymal hemorrhage causes by age: AVM in the young, hypertension (deep structures) in older adults, amyloid angiopathy (lobar) in the elderly per First Aid. COMLEX: Treatment of choice for an accessible AVM is surgical removal, sometimes after endovascular embolization.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 5 — differentiating epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid hemorrhage by mechanism and imaging, the extra-axial bleeds contrasted here with an intraparenchymal one","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q37","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe superficial and deep venous drainage and identify clinically important venous sinus problems.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman who is severely dehydrated after a gastrointestinal illness develops headache, vomiting, and increased tone and weakness in both legs, with normal arm strength. MR venography shows thrombosis of the middle portion of the superior sagittal sinus. Which of the following best explains the weakness of both legs?","choices":{"A":"Compression of both corticospinal tracts by a midline cerebellar hematoma","B":"Bilateral anterior cerebral artery occlusion from arterial vasospasm","C":"Spinal cord infarction from reduced flow in the anterior spinal artery","D":"Venous stasis and infarction of the adjacent motor cortex for the legs","E":"Obstructive hydrocephalus stretching periventricular arm fibers first"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Occlusion of a venous sinus blocks venous return, so upstream veins and sinuses are engorged, the surrounding white matter becomes edematous, hemorrhage from the raised venous pressure is possible, and a venous infarct forms; raised intracranial pressure causes headache and vomiting. Thrombosis of the middle portion of the superior sagittal sinus may cause increased tone and weakness of one or both lower extremities because of the sinus's proximity to the leg area of the motor cortex. The scan shows sinus thrombosis rather than cerebellar hematoma, arterial spasm, or a spinal cord process. Hydrocephalus would not selectively spare the arms while weakening both legs from a sinus lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Course-listed causes of cerebral venous thrombosis include severe dehydration, hypercoagulable states, birth control pills, sickle cell disease, meningitis, sinus infection, and head trauma; deficits follow the location of the occluded sinus. COMLEX: Suspect venous sinus thrombosis in a young patient with headache, raised intracranial pressure, and deficits that do not fit one arterial territory.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — dural fold and venous sinus formation, the same superior sagittal sinus whose thrombosis is illustrated here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q38","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe superficial and deep venous drainage and identify clinically important venous sinus problems.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with sinusitis develops fever, proptosis, chemosis, and painful ophthalmoplegia of the right eye that rapidly progresses to involve the left eye as well. Which anatomic feature of the affected sinus best explains why the infection spread to the opposite side?","choices":{"A":"The two cavernous sinuses communicate directly through the intercavernous sinuses","B":"The two cavernous sinuses share a single confluence with the straight sinus","C":"The cavernous sinus drains directly into the contralateral internal jugular vein","D":"The cavernous sinus has no dural wall separating it from the opposite side","E":"The superior sagittal sinus connects the two cavernous sinuses at the vertex"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The paired cavernous sinuses communicate directly with each other through the intercavernous sinuses that cross the midline, which is why an infection or thrombus in one cavernous sinus can rapidly spread to involve the contralateral side, producing bilateral proptosis and ophthalmoplegia. The confluence of sinuses instead refers to the posterior junction of the straight, superior sagittal, and transverse sinuses, unrelated to bilateral cavernous sinus spread, the cavernous sinus drains ipsilaterally into the petrosal sinuses rather than directly crossing to the opposite jugular vein, there is a dural wall between the cavernous sinuses that is bridged specifically by the intercavernous connections rather than being entirely absent, and the superior sagittal sinus at the vertex has no direct connection to either cavernous sinus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The intercavernous sinus connections explaining bilateral spread of cavernous sinus thrombosis is a classic anatomic basis for a feared complication of facial or sinus infection. COMLEX: This same anatomy is why a carotid-cavernous fistula or aneurysm can also produce bilateral orbital signs if the intercavernous connections are involved.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — high-yield cavernous sinus relationships, the same intercavernous connections explaining bilateral spread illustrated here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q39","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe superficial and deep venous drainage and identify clinically important venous sinus problems.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-month-old infant presents with high-output heart failure, a cranial bruit, and hydrocephalus. Imaging reveals a grossly enlarged midline venous structure fed by abnormal arterial branches, located just deep to the splenium of the corpus callosum. Which structure is most likely malformed?","choices":{"A":"The straight sinus alone without venous malformation","B":"The great cerebral vein of Galen","C":"The superior sagittal sinus at the vertex","D":"The cavernous sinus near the sphenoid bone","E":"The basal vein of Rosenthal alone without arterial shunting"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A vein of Galen malformation is a congenital arteriovenous shunt directly into the great cerebral vein of Galen, located deep to the splenium of the corpus callosum, that can massively enlarge this vessel and produce high-output cardiac failure in infancy from the large left-to-right shunt, along with hydrocephalus from venous congestion impairing CSF absorption. The straight sinus alone without malformation would not explain the arterial shunting or massive enlargement described, the superior sagittal sinus is a superficial midline structure at the vertex rather than deep to the splenium, the cavernous sinus is a paired lateral structure near the sphenoid bone unrelated to this midline deep venous malformation, and the basal vein of Rosenthal alone, without arterial shunting, would not produce the described high-output failure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vein of Galen malformation as a cause of neonatal high-output heart failure and hydrocephalus is a classic pediatric vascular anomaly tying deep venous anatomy to systemic physiology. COMLEX: Early recognition is critical since endovascular embolization can prevent progressive cardiac decompensation in these infants.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 5 — differentiating hydrocephalus types, since impaired venous drainage here produces the same CSF absorption failure discussed there","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q40","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use a stroke-localization algorithm to move from neurologic deficit to likely vascular territory.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man presents with sudden left-sided weakness of the face, arm, and leg equally, without any sensory loss, visual field cut, aphasia, or neglect. Using the stroke-localization algorithm, which site is most likely responsible?","choices":{"A":"The cortical MCA territory of the lateral hemispheric convexity","B":"The cortical ACA territory of the medial hemispheric surface","C":"The cortical PCA territory of the medial occipital lobe","D":"The lateral medulla via the posterior inferior cerebellar artery","E":"The internal capsule via lenticulostriate or anterior choroidal branches"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Dense, equal weakness of the face, arm, and leg without any cortical signs such as sensory loss, visual field cut, aphasia, or neglect points, by the localization algorithm, to a subcortical lesion in the internal capsule, most often from lenticulostriate or anterior choroidal artery territory, where all corticospinal fibers are compactly bundled together. Cortical MCA territory would instead be expected to spare the leg and to include cortical signs like aphasia or neglect, cortical ACA territory would produce leg-predominant rather than equal face-arm-leg weakness, cortical PCA territory would produce visual field loss rather than motor weakness, and the lateral medulla via PICA would produce crossed sensory and bulbar findings rather than equal contralateral hemiplegia alone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognizing equal, dense face-arm-leg weakness without cortical signs as an internal capsule, subcortical, lesion rather than a cortical stroke is a cornerstone localization skill. COMLEX: This distinction changes prognosis and workup, since subcortical lacunar strokes carry a different secondary prevention strategy focused on blood pressure control.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 4 — localizing cortical lesions using motor and sensory patterns, contrasted here with the subcortical internal capsule pattern that lacks cortical signs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q41","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use a stroke-localization algorithm to move from neurologic deficit to likely vascular territory.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman develops sudden left facial pain and temperature loss along with right-sided body pain and temperature loss, hoarseness, and hiccups. Using the stroke-localization algorithm, which pattern identifies this as a brainstem rather than a hemispheric lesion?","choices":{"A":"The presence of any unilateral sensory loss regardless of its distribution","B":"The presence of hoarseness alone without any accompanying sensory findings","C":"The absence of any weakness or sensory loss in either limb","D":"The crossed, or alternating, pattern of ipsilateral face and contralateral body findings","E":"The presence of hiccups as an isolated, nonspecific finding alone"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The stroke-localization algorithm identifies brainstem lesions specifically by their crossed, or alternating, pattern, here ipsilateral facial pain and temperature loss from the spinal trigeminal nucleus paired with contralateral body pain and temperature loss from the ascending spinothalamic tract, since cranial nerve nuclei are uncrossed while long tracts eventually cross elsewhere. Sensory loss in any distribution alone does not specify brainstem versus hemispheric localization without the crossed pattern, hoarseness alone without any sensory findings would be a nonspecific and incomplete localizing clue, absence of limb weakness does not by itself indicate brainstem localization since some hemispheric lesions can also spare motor function, and hiccups alone are a nonspecific associated symptom rather than the defining localizing feature.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The crossed or alternating sensory pattern, ipsilateral face plus contralateral body, is the single most important localization principle for lateral medullary syndrome and brainstem lesions generally. COMLEX: Hiccups and dysphagia in this vignette reflect nucleus ambiguus and medullary respiratory center involvement typical of Wallenberg syndrome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 3 — interpreting cranial nerve findings to localize brainstem lesions, the same crossed face-body pattern used for localization here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q42","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use a stroke-localization algorithm to move from neurologic deficit to likely vascular territory.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man presents with sudden right-sided homonymous hemianopia, mild confusion, but no weakness, no sensory loss, and no language deficit. Applying the stroke-localization algorithm, which vascular territory should be evaluated first?","choices":{"A":"The posterior cerebral artery territory","B":"The middle cerebral artery territory","C":"The anterior cerebral artery territory","D":"The internal capsule via lenticulostriate branches","E":"The lateral medulla via the PICA"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Isolated homonymous hemianopia without motor, sensory, or language deficit fits the cortical forebrain branch of the algorithm and specifically points to the occipital cortex supplied by the PCA, with mild confusion reflecting occasional thalamic or medial temporal involvement in PCA territory strokes. The MCA territory would instead be expected to also produce contralateral arm and face weakness or aphasia rather than isolated visual field loss, the ACA territory would produce leg-predominant weakness rather than a visual field cut, the internal capsule would produce dense motor and sensory loss without visual field involvement, and the lateral medulla via PICA would produce crossed sensory and bulbar findings rather than an isolated visual field defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Applying the forebrain branch of the localization algorithm, isolated hemianopia without motor or language signs points to PCA, is a reliable shortcut for rapid vignette localization. COMLEX: Associated confusion or memory disturbance in a PCA stroke should raise suspicion for medial temporal lobe or thalamic involvement in addition to the occipital cortex.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — localizing cortical lesions using visual field patterns, the same PCA-territory algorithm step applied here","isHighYield":true}],"11":[{"id":"sdl11_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare ischemic stroke, lacunar infarction, subarachnoid hemorrhage, intraparenchymal hemorrhage, epidural hemorrhage, and subdural hemorrhage.","batch":1,"stem":"Subdural hemorrhage most often results from tearing of which structure, and what shape does the resulting collection classically take on noncontrast head CT?","choices":{"A":"Bridging veins crossing the dura-arachnoid interface, producing a crescent-shaped collection that crosses suture lines","B":"The middle meningeal artery beneath the pterion, producing a lens-shaped collection that does not cross cranial suture lines","C":"A ruptured saccular aneurysm at the circle of Willis, producing diffuse hyperdensity filling the cisterns","D":"Deep lenticulostriate penetrating vessels, producing a round collection confined to the basal nuclei","E":"The superior sagittal sinus itself, producing a midline collection that tracks along the falx cerebri"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Subdural hemorrhage results from tearing of the fragile bridging veins that cross the potential space between the dura and arachnoid as they drain cortical veins into the dural sinuses, producing a crescent-shaped collection that, unlike an epidural bleed, is able to cross suture lines because it is not confined by dural attachments. Middle meningeal artery laceration and a lens-shaped, suture-limited collection instead describe epidural hemorrhage, aneurysm rupture produces diffuse subarachnoid rather than subdural blood, lenticulostriate rupture produces a deep intraparenchymal hematoma, and isolated sinus injury produces a different, non-crescentic pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crescent-shaped and suture-crossing versus lens-shaped and suture-limited is the single fastest way to separate subdural from epidural hemorrhage on an imaging-based question. COMLEX: Elderly patients and those on anticoagulation are at particularly high subdural risk because cerebral atrophy stretches the bridging veins, making even minor trauma sufficient to tear them.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 5 — differentiating epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid hemorrhage by mechanism, vessel, location, imaging, and presentation, extended here to lacunar and intraparenchymal comparisons","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q02","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare ischemic stroke, lacunar infarction, subarachnoid hemorrhage, intraparenchymal hemorrhage, epidural hemorrhage, and subdural hemorrhage.","batch":1,"stem":"A lacunar infarct confined to the posterior limb of the internal capsule that spares all sensory pathways would most likely be classified under which named lacunar syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Pure sensory stroke","B":"Ataxic hemiparesis","C":"Dysarthria-clumsy hand syndrome","D":"Sensorimotor stroke","E":"Pure motor hemiparesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pure motor hemiparesis is the most common lacunar syndrome and results from a small infarct isolated to the descending corticospinal fibers in the posterior limb of the internal capsule, corona radiata, or basis pontis, producing weakness of the face, arm, and leg without any sensory, visual, or cognitive change. Pure sensory stroke instead localizes to the thalamus rather than the internal capsule, ataxic hemiparesis combines weakness with ipsilateral limb ataxia from combined corticospinal and cerebellar pathway involvement, dysarthria-clumsy hand syndrome localizes to the basis pontis or genu of the internal capsule with a distinct hand-clumsiness pattern, and sensorimotor stroke by definition includes sensory loss, which this description specifically excludes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognizing the five classic lacunar syndromes, pure motor, pure sensory, ataxic hemiparesis, dysarthria-clumsy hand, and mixed sensorimotor, by their distinct anatomic localization is a well-defined, testable list. COMLEX: All five lacunar syndromes share the same underlying risk factor of chronic hypertension causing lipohyalinosis of small penetrating vessels.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 5 — differentiating epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid hemorrhage by mechanism, vessel, location, imaging, and presentation, extended here to lacunar and intraparenchymal comparisons","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q03","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare ischemic stroke, lacunar infarction, subarachnoid hemorrhage, intraparenchymal hemorrhage, epidural hemorrhage, and subdural hemorrhage.","batch":1,"stem":"A sudden, severe headache described as the worst of the patient's life, accompanied by neck stiffness and photophobia but no focal weakness, most strongly points toward which hemorrhage type rather than intraparenchymal hemorrhage?","choices":{"A":"Intraparenchymal hemorrhage, since bleeding directly into brain tissue always produces meningismus before any focal deficit","B":"Epidural hemorrhage, since arterial bleeding outside the dura classically irritates the meninges and produces neck stiffness","C":"Lacunar infarction, since small penetrating vessel occlusion is a well-recognized cause of sudden meningismus","D":"Subarachnoid hemorrhage, since blood irritating the meninges produces meningismus without a focal parenchymal deficit","E":"Subdural hemorrhage, since venous bleeding at the dura-arachnoid interface is the most common cause of a thunderclap headache"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sudden severe headache with meningismus, from photophobia and neck stiffness, but without a focal motor or sensory deficit, is the classic presentation of subarachnoid hemorrhage, since blood spreading through the CSF-filled subarachnoid space chemically irritates the meninges rather than directly compressing focal brain tissue. Intraparenchymal hemorrhage instead typically presents with a focal neurologic deficit corresponding to the bleed's location rather than prominent meningismus, epidural hemorrhage classically presents with a lucid interval followed by focal deterioration rather than meningeal irritation, lacunar infarction is a small ischemic event that does not cause sudden headache or meningismus, and subdural hemorrhage typically evolves gradually with confusion rather than presenting as an acute meningismus syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The thunderclap headache with meningismus but no focal deficit is the classic presenting triad that should trigger an immediate workup for subarachnoid hemorrhage. COMLEX: A noncontrast head CT within six hours of symptom onset is highly sensitive for SAH, but a negative CT still requires lumbar puncture to look for xanthochromia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 5 — differentiating epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid hemorrhage by mechanism, vessel, location, imaging, and presentation, extended here to lacunar and intraparenchymal comparisons","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q04","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe superficial and deep venous drainage and identify clinically important venous sinus problems.","batch":1,"stem":"The superior cerebral veins, draining the convexity of the frontal and parietal lobes, empty directly into which dural venous sinus?","choices":{"A":"The cavernous sinus","B":"The straight sinus","C":"The superior sagittal sinus","D":"The transverse sinus directly","E":"The inferior sagittal sinus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The superior cerebral veins ascend from the convexity and cross the subdural space as bridging veins before emptying into the superior sagittal sinus, which runs along the attached margin of the falx cerebri, distinguishing this superficial drainage route from the superficial middle cerebral vein, which instead empties into the cavernous sinus. The straight sinus receives the great cerebral vein and inferior sagittal sinus rather than the superior cerebral veins directly, the transverse sinus receives blood only after it has already passed through the confluence of sinuses, and the inferior sagittal sinus drains the medial hemispheric surface near the corpus callosum rather than the superior convexity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Superior cerebral veins draining into the superior sagittal sinus is the anatomic basis for why these same vessels are the bridging veins torn in subdural hemorrhage. COMLEX: This drainage pattern also explains why parasagittal meningiomas can obstruct venous outflow and mimic signs of increased intracranial pressure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — tracing the major dural venous sinuses and recognizing high-yield cavernous sinus relationships, the same rigid channels whose thrombosis risk is described here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q05","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe superficial and deep venous drainage and identify clinically important venous sinus problems.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following is a well-established risk factor for cerebral venous sinus thrombosis?","choices":{"A":"Chronic well-controlled hypertension without any other comorbidity","B":"Remote history of a single uncomplicated febrile seizure in childhood","C":"Stable type 2 diabetes mellitus without vascular complications","D":"Use of combined oral contraceptives or an underlying hypercoagulable state","E":"A prior episode of migraine with aura that resolved completely"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Combined oral contraceptive use, pregnancy and the postpartum state, and inherited or acquired hypercoagulable states all promote venous stasis and clot formation within the rigid dural sinuses, making them the classic risk factors for cerebral venous sinus thrombosis. Well-controlled hypertension is instead a dominant risk factor for lacunar and intraparenchymal hemorrhage rather than venous thrombosis, a remote childhood febrile seizure carries no established venous thrombosis risk, stable diabetes without vascular complications is not a recognized venous thrombosis risk factor, and a resolved episode of migraine with aura does not meaningfully raise venous thrombosis risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Estrogen-containing contraceptives and hypercoagulable states as leading venous sinus thrombosis risk factors is a frequently tested pairing that distinguishes this diagnosis from arterial stroke risk factors like hypertension. COMLEX: A young woman on oral contraceptives presenting with headache, papilledema, and a focal deficit should prompt venous imaging even when arterial stroke risk factors are absent.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — tracing the major dural venous sinuses and recognizing high-yield cavernous sinus relationships, the same rigid channels whose thrombosis risk is described here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q06","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe superficial and deep venous drainage and identify clinically important venous sinus problems.","batch":1,"stem":"The sigmoid sinus, receiving blood from the transverse sinus, exits the skull through the jugular foramen to become which structure?","choices":{"A":"The external jugular vein","B":"The internal jugular vein","C":"The cavernous sinus","D":"The superior petrosal sinus","E":"The confluence of sinuses"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The sigmoid sinus curves inferiorly along the mastoid portion of the temporal bone and exits the skull base through the jugular foramen, where it becomes the internal jugular vein, which then carries essentially all intracranial venous outflow back toward the heart. The external jugular vein instead drains superficial extracranial scalp and facial structures rather than intracranial venous sinuses, the cavernous sinus is a separate anterior sinus that drains into the sigmoid system only indirectly via the petrosal sinuses, the superior petrosal sinus is a smaller channel connecting the cavernous sinus to the transverse-sigmoid junction rather than the sinus's terminal exit point, and the confluence of sinuses is a more posterior, superior junction point rather than the skull-base exit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The sigmoid sinus becoming the internal jugular vein at the jugular foramen is the final common pathway for essentially all dural venous drainage and is essential for interpreting jugular venous imaging. COMLEX: Mastoiditis can spread directly to the adjacent sigmoid sinus and precipitate sigmoid sinus thrombosis given their close anatomic relationship.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — tracing the major dural venous sinuses and recognizing high-yield cavernous sinus relationships, the same rigid channels whose thrombosis risk is described here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q07","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use a stroke-localization algorithm to move from neurologic deficit to likely vascular territory.","batch":1,"stem":"In the stroke-localization algorithm, contralateral leg and foot weakness accompanied by apathy, abulia, and urinary incontinence should be localized to which vascular territory?","choices":{"A":"The middle cerebral artery","B":"The posterior cerebral artery","C":"The anterior cerebral artery","D":"The lateral medulla via the PICA","E":"The superior cerebellar artery"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Leg-predominant weakness combined with behavioral changes such as apathy, abulia, and urinary incontinence reflects damage to the medial frontal and paracentral lobule supplied by the anterior cerebral artery, which also serves adjacent medial frontal lobe structures governing behavior and bladder inhibition. The MCA territory instead produces arm and face-predominant weakness with possible aphasia rather than a leg-predominant, behavior-change pattern, the PCA territory produces visual field loss rather than leg weakness, the lateral medulla via PICA produces crossed sensory and bulbar findings rather than isolated leg weakness with behavioral change, and the superior cerebellar artery produces ataxia rather than hemiparesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leg-predominant weakness plus frontal behavioral change and incontinence is the algorithm's signature combination for localizing to the ACA territory. COMLEX: Bilateral ACA territory infarction, from a shared trunk anomaly, can produce a dramatic akinetic mutism picture that is important to distinguish from a primary psychiatric presentation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 3 — predicting the major deficits caused by ACA, MCA, PCA, anterior choroidal, lenticulostriate, PICA, AICA, and SCA lesions, the deficit patterns this localization algorithm draws on directly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q08","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use a stroke-localization algorithm to move from neurologic deficit to likely vascular territory.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient's eyes are found deviated toward the side of a large hemispheric lesion. Applying the stroke-localization algorithm, what does this conjugate gaze deviation most likely indicate about the lesion?","choices":{"A":"A destructive brainstem lesion, since the unopposed pontine gaze center on the intact side pushes the eyes toward the lesion","B":"A destructive cortical lesion, since the unopposed frontal eye field on the unaffected side pushes the eyes toward the lesion","C":"An active seizure focus, since seizure activity classically deviates the eyes toward the seizing hemisphere itself","D":"A cerebellar lesion, since unopposed vestibular input from the intact cerebellum drives the eyes toward the lesion","E":"A lacunar infarct of the internal capsule, since pure motor lacunar strokes are the classic cause of gaze deviation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In a destructive cortical hemispheric lesion, the frontal eye field on the healthy side remains unopposed and drives conjugate gaze toward the side of the lesion, the opposite pattern from a pontine gaze center lesion, which instead classically deviates the eyes away from the lesion because the unopposed contralateral pontine center predominates. An active seizure focus typically deviates the eyes away from the seizing hemisphere rather than toward it, a cerebellar lesion does not produce this frontal-eye-field-driven gaze deviation pattern, and a lacunar internal capsule infarct affecting only the corticospinal tract does not typically produce conjugate gaze deviation at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Eyes look toward a cortical lesion but away from a pontine lesion is a classic bedside localizing rule that lets examiners distinguish supratentorial from infratentorial gaze palsies without imaging. COMLEX: This same rule helps separate a destructive hemispheric stroke from an active seizure, since seizure activity deviates gaze in the opposite direction from a destructive cortical lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 3 — predicting the major deficits caused by ACA, MCA, PCA, anterior choroidal, lenticulostriate, PICA, AICA, and SCA lesions, the deficit patterns this localization algorithm draws on directly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q09","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use a stroke-localization algorithm to move from neurologic deficit to likely vascular territory.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient develops quadriplegia and anarthria after a large basilar artery occlusion, yet remains fully conscious and can communicate only through vertical eye movements and blinking. Applying the stroke-localization algorithm, which syndrome and level best explain this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Locked-in syndrome, from bilateral ventral pontine infarction sparing the reticular activating system and vertical gaze pathways","B":"Locked-in syndrome, from bilateral medullary infarction sparing the ascending reticular activating system and cranial nerve nuclei entirely","C":"Persistent vegetative state, from diffuse bilateral cortical infarction with a completely intact brainstem","D":"Weber syndrome, from a unilateral midbrain lesion sparing the corticospinal tracts bilaterally","E":"Top-of-basilar syndrome, from bilateral thalamic infarction with preserved motor pathways throughout"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Locked-in syndrome results from bilateral infarction of the ventral pons, which interrupts the corticospinal and corticobulbar tracts to produce quadriplegia and anarthria while sparing the dorsally located reticular activating system, preserving consciousness, and sparing the vertical gaze and blinking pathways that allow limited communication. Bilateral medullary infarction would not classically produce this exact ventral pontine pattern, persistent vegetative state by definition involves loss of conscious awareness rather than a fully conscious but paralyzed patient, Weber syndrome is a unilateral midbrain syndrome producing crossed rather than bilateral quadriplegic findings, and top-of-basilar syndrome primarily produces visual and consciousness disturbances rather than the described quadriplegic, awake pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Locked-in syndrome from bilateral ventral pontine infarction, with preserved consciousness and vertical eye movement as the only communication channel, is a devastating and highly testable basilar artery occlusion syndrome. First Aid mnemonic: locked-in syndrome is being locked in the basement, the pons sitting at the base of the brain. COMLEX: Recognizing preserved consciousness in an apparently unresponsive quadriplegic patient is critical, since it changes both the ethical and clinical approach to communication and prognosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 3 — predicting the major deficits caused by ACA, MCA, PCA, anterior choroidal, lenticulostriate, PICA, AICA, and SCA lesions, the deficit patterns this localization algorithm draws on directly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q10","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare ischemic stroke, lacunar infarction, subarachnoid hemorrhage, intraparenchymal hemorrhage, epidural hemorrhage, and subdural hemorrhage.","batch":2,"stem":"An 82-year-old man on warfarin for atrial fibrillation is brought in by his daughter for three weeks of progressive gait unsteadiness, forgetfulness, and mild left-sided weakness. He recalls a minor fall against a door frame about a month ago that he did not think was serious. Head CT shows a crescent-shaped hypodense to mixed-density collection over the right cerebral convexity with mild midline shift. Which diagnosis and mechanism best fit this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Acute epidural hematoma from middle meningeal artery laceration, with a classic delayed lucid interval extending over several weeks before deterioration","B":"Subarachnoid hemorrhage from a slowly leaking saccular aneurysm producing gradually progressive meningismus","C":"Chronic subdural hematoma from slow bleeding of stretched bridging veins after minor trauma in an anticoagulated, atrophic brain","D":"Lacunar infarction of the internal capsule from chronic hypertension unrelated to his anticoagulation or reported fall","E":"Intraparenchymal hemorrhage from lenticulostriate rupture, appearing hypodense on CT due to warfarin-related dilution"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A crescent-shaped, mixed-density extra-axial collection developing over weeks after minor trauma in an elderly, anticoagulated patient with brain atrophy is the classic picture of a chronic subdural hematoma, in which slowly stretched bridging veins bleed at low venous pressure and the collection progressively liquefies and becomes hypodense relative to acute blood. Epidural hematomas are high-pressure arterial bleeds that evolve over hours rather than weeks, subarachnoid hemorrhage from aneurysm rupture presents acutely with meningismus rather than a gradually progressive cognitive and gait syndrome, an isolated lacunar infarct would not explain the extra-axial collection seen on CT, and acute intraparenchymal hemorrhage appears hyperdense rather than hypodense and would not evolve this slowly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Chronic subdural hematoma as an insidious cause of falls, cognitive decline, and mild focal weakness in elderly or anticoagulated patients is a frequently missed diagnosis that boards test through this exact vague, subacute presentation. COMLEX: Anticoagulation reversal alongside surgical evacuation is often required once a symptomatic chronic subdural hematoma is identified.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 5 — differentiating epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid hemorrhage by mechanism and imaging, extended here to the chronic, slowly evolving form of subdural bleeding rather than the acute pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q11","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare ischemic stroke, lacunar infarction, subarachnoid hemorrhage, intraparenchymal hemorrhage, epidural hemorrhage, and subdural hemorrhage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman develops an abrupt, excruciating occipital headache while lifting weights, describing it as the worst headache of her life, along with nausea, vomiting, photophobia, and neck stiffness. She has no focal weakness or sensory loss. Noncontrast head CT performed within two hours of onset is unremarkable. Which next step and underlying process are most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Lumbar puncture to check for xanthochromia, since early CT can miss a small subarachnoid hemorrhage from an aneurysm","B":"Reassurance and discharge with analgesics, since a normal CT within six hours effectively excludes any hemorrhage entirely","C":"Immediate surgical evacuation, since her presentation is classic for an expanding epidural hematoma requiring urgent decompression","D":"Empiric antibiotics for presumed bacterial meningitis, since her headache and neck stiffness are most consistent with infection","E":"Observation alone without further workup, since her normal CT and lack of focal deficit make an intraparenchymal bleed unlikely"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A sudden, exertional, thunderclap headache with meningismus but no focal deficit is the classic presentation of subarachnoid hemorrhage, most often from a ruptured saccular aneurysm, and while noncontrast CT is highly sensitive very early after symptom onset, a small or early SAH can still be missed, so a negative CT still warrants lumbar puncture looking for xanthochromia to definitively exclude the diagnosis. Discharging her based on a normal CT alone risks missing a small SAH, her presentation does not fit epidural hemorrhage since there is no trauma or lucid interval described, empiric antibiotics for meningitis are premature without supporting infectious findings and would delay the correct workup, and observation without further testing would leave a potentially life-threatening aneurysmal bleed unconfirmed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The exertional thunderclap headache with meningismus and a need for lumbar puncture after a negative early CT is one of the highest-yield emergency neurology vignettes tested. COMLEX: Xanthochromia, a yellow discoloration from breakdown of red blood cells in the CSF, typically takes several hours to develop and distinguishes true SAH from a traumatic lumbar puncture.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 6 — interpreting high-yield CSF patterns in subarachnoid hemorrhage, the same xanthochromia finding relied on here when CT imaging is falsely reassuring","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q12","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare ischemic stroke, lacunar infarction, subarachnoid hemorrhage, intraparenchymal hemorrhage, epidural hemorrhage, and subdural hemorrhage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man with longstanding, poorly controlled hypertension develops sudden numbness of the entire left face, arm, and leg without any accompanying weakness, visual field defect, or language disturbance. MRI reveals a small punctate infarct localized to a deep gray matter relay structure. Which vessel territory and structure are most likely involved?","choices":{"A":"The lenticulostriate branches of the middle cerebral artery supplying the posterior limb of the internal capsule","B":"The anterior choroidal artery supplying the optic tract and internal capsule","C":"The recurrent artery of Heubner supplying the head of the caudate nucleus","D":"The thalamogeniculate branches supplying the ventral posterior (VPL and VPM) thalamic nuclei","E":"The posterior cerebral artery supplying the calcarine visual cortex"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A pure sensory lacunar stroke, with numbness of the face, arm, and leg but no weakness or cortical signs, classically results from a small infarct of the thalamogeniculate branches of the PCA supplying the ventral posterolateral and ventral posteromedial nuclei of the thalamus, the somatosensory relays for the body and face. Lenticulostriate occlusion of the internal capsule instead produces a purely motor rather than purely sensory pattern, the anterior choroidal artery produces a hemiplegia-hemianesthesia-hemianopia triad rather than isolated sensory loss, the recurrent artery of Heubner supplies the caudate and produces subtle behavioral rather than sensory findings, and direct PCA cortical territory involvement of the calcarine cortex would produce visual field loss rather than isolated hemibody numbness.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pure sensory stroke localizing specifically to the ventral posterior (VPL/VPM) thalamus via thalamogeniculate vessels is a distinct lacunar syndrome that examiners use to test precise deep gray matter localization. COMLEX: Chronic thalamic pain syndrome can develop weeks to months after this type of infarct, producing delayed, often severe contralateral dysesthetic pain.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — relating ACA, MCA, and PCA cortical territories to expected deficits, contrasted here with a deep thalamic branch infarct producing a purely sensory rather than cortical pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q13","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe superficial and deep venous drainage and identify clinically important venous sinus problems.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman taking combined oral contraceptives presents with several days of worsening headache followed by a witnessed generalized tonic-clonic seizure. Contrast-enhanced CT venography shows a filling defect in the superior sagittal sinus surrounded by a rim of enhancement, described by radiology as an empty delta sign. Which condition does this finding confirm?","choices":{"A":"Superior sagittal sinus aplasia, a congenital absence of the sinus present since birth and entirely unrelated to her oral contraceptive use","B":"Superior sagittal sinus thrombosis, in which the clot itself fails to enhance while the surrounding collateral venous channels do","C":"An arteriovenous malformation draining directly into the superior sagittal sinus and rupturing under pressure","D":"A parasagittal meningioma compressing but not occluding the superior sagittal sinus from an adjacent dural surface","E":"Idiopathic intracranial hypertension with a patent superior sagittal sinus and normal venographic flow throughout"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The empty delta sign describes a triangular area of nonenhancing thrombus within the superior sagittal sinus surrounded by a rim of enhancing dural collateral vessels and sinus wall on contrast-enhanced imaging, a specific finding for sinus thrombosis, which oral contraceptive use predisposes to through an acquired hypercoagulable state and can present with headache progressing to seizure from venous congestion and cortical irritation. Sinus aplasia is a congenital variant without an active clot and would not produce this enhancement pattern, an arteriovenous malformation would show abnormal early arterial-phase enhancement rather than a nonenhancing filling defect, a compressive meningioma would narrow the sinus lumen without producing a discrete nonenhancing clot, and idiopathic intracranial hypertension by definition has patent venous sinuses on imaging.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The empty delta sign is the single most recognizable imaging finding for dural venous sinus thrombosis and is frequently paired with an oral-contraceptive risk factor in vignette form. COMLEX: New-onset seizure in a young woman with a preceding headache should prompt venous imaging even before considering a primary seizure disorder workup.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — explaining how dural venous sinuses are formed, the same rigid channel whose thrombosis is confirmed here by the empty delta sign","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q14","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe superficial and deep venous drainage and identify clinically important venous sinus problems.","batch":2,"stem":"An 8-year-old boy with a two-week history of untreated left ear pain and drainage develops fever, headache, and swelling behind the left ear. CT temporal bone shows opacification of the mastoid air cells with an adjacent filling defect in a nearby dural venous channel coursing along the posterior temporal bone. Which venous structure is most likely thrombosed, and by what route did the infection spread?","choices":{"A":"The sigmoid sinus, from direct spread of mastoid infection through the thin bone separating the mastoid air cells from the sinus","B":"The cavernous sinus, from retrograde spread through the valveless facial venous system draining the external ear canal, midface, and orbit","C":"The superior sagittal sinus, from direct extension through the calvarium overlying the mastoid process","D":"The straight sinus, from retrograde flow through the deep cerebral veins draining the middle ear structures","E":"The inferior petrosal sinus alone, from spread through the tympanic membrane into the middle cranial fossa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The sigmoid sinus runs directly adjacent to the mastoid air cells, separated only by a thin bony plate, so untreated mastoiditis can erode through this bone and directly thrombose the sigmoid sinus, a well-recognized otogenic complication that produces fever, headache, and postauricular swelling. Cavernous sinus thrombosis instead classically arises from spreading facial or paranasal sinus infection along the facial and ophthalmic veins rather than from mastoiditis, the superior sagittal sinus is anatomically distant from the mastoid process and not the expected spread route, the straight sinus receives deep venous drainage rather than being adjacent to the mastoid air cells, and the inferior petrosal sinus alone does not explain the mastoid-adjacent filling defect described on imaging.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Otogenic sigmoid sinus thrombosis from untreated mastoiditis is a classic pediatric complication illustrating direct anatomic spread of infection into an adjacent venous sinus. COMLEX: Prompt antibiotic therapy and, when needed, surgical mastoidectomy are used to prevent this complication before venous thrombosis develops.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 3 — interpreting cranial nerve and skull base findings to localize lesions, the same postauricular anatomic region relevant to localizing this otogenic venous complication","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q15","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Describe superficial and deep venous drainage and identify clinically important venous sinus problems.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with a known hypercoagulable disorder presents with progressive lethargy, bilateral leg weakness, and confusion. MRI shows bilateral symmetric infarcts of the thalami and basal ganglia with restricted diffusion, and MR venography shows absent flow in the deep midline venous channel that receives blood from the paired internal cerebral veins. Which condition best explains this bilateral, symmetric infarct pattern?","choices":{"A":"Superior sagittal sinus thrombosis, causing symmetric hemorrhagic infarction of the medial frontal and parietal convexity","B":"Bilateral cavernous sinus thrombosis, causing symmetric infarction of the anterior temporal and orbital regions","C":"Bilateral MCA territory embolic infarction, causing symmetric infarction of the lateral hemispheric convexities","D":"Deep cerebral venous thrombosis involving the great cerebral vein of Galen or straight sinus, causing bilateral thalamic venous infarction","E":"Bilateral PICA territory infarction, causing symmetric infarction confined to the cerebellar hemispheres"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The great cerebral vein of Galen and the straight sinus drain the deep venous system, including the paired internal cerebral veins that serve the thalami and basal ganglia, so thrombosis at this deep confluence produces bilateral, symmetric thalamic and basal ganglionic venous infarction, a pattern distinct from arterial territory strokes. Superior sagittal sinus thrombosis would instead be expected to produce convexity, not deep, infarction, cavernous sinus thrombosis affects orbital and cranial nerve structures rather than deep gray matter, bilateral MCA embolic infarction would affect the lateral convexities rather than the deep midline structures described, and PICA territory infarction is confined to the cerebellum rather than the thalami and basal ganglia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Deep cerebral venous thrombosis producing bilateral thalamic infarction is a distinctive, less common but highly testable venous stroke pattern that mimics a bilateral arterial process on imaging. COMLEX: Recognizing a venous rather than arterial infarct pattern is critical because treatment relies on anticoagulation rather than the antiplatelet therapy used for typical arterial stroke.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 5 — differentiating hydrocephalus and CSF flow disorders, since deep venous congestion here produces a comparably distinct, midline-structure imaging pattern that must be distinguished from ventricular pathology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q16","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use a stroke-localization algorithm to move from neurologic deficit to likely vascular territory.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man develops sudden right-sided tongue weakness, with the tongue deviating toward the right on protrusion, along with left-sided arm and leg weakness and reduced left-sided vibration and position sense. Pain and temperature sensation are intact on the face and body. Applying the stroke-localization algorithm, which brainstem syndrome and vessel best explain this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Lateral medullary syndrome from posterior inferior cerebellar artery occlusion, with prominent crossed pain and temperature loss","B":"Weber syndrome from a midbrain paramedian branch occlusion, with an associated ipsilateral oculomotor nerve palsy","C":"Locked-in syndrome from bilateral basilar artery occlusion, with quadriplegia and preserved vertical eye movement","D":"Lateral pontine syndrome from anterior inferior cerebellar artery occlusion, with ipsilateral hearing loss and facial weakness","E":"Medial medullary syndrome from anterior spinal artery or vertebral artery occlusion, sparing pain and temperature pathways"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This is the classic alternating hypoglossal, or medial medullary, syndrome, in which occlusion of the anterior spinal artery or a paramedian branch of the vertebral artery damages the hypoglossal nerve fascicles, producing ipsilateral tongue deviation toward the lesion, along with the medullary pyramid and medial lemniscus, producing contralateral limb weakness, while sparing the more laterally located spinothalamic tract and its pain-temperature function. Lateral medullary, or Wallenberg, syndrome instead prominently features crossed pain and temperature loss, which is absent here, Weber syndrome is a midbrain rather than medullary syndrome and involves CN III rather than CN XII, locked-in syndrome produces bilateral rather than crossed findings, and lateral pontine, or AICA, syndrome produces hearing loss and facial weakness rather than tongue deviation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medial medullary syndrome, with its alternating hypoglossal pattern of ipsilateral tongue deviation and contralateral hemiparesis while sparing pain and temperature sensation, is a classic counterpart to the far more commonly tested lateral medullary syndrome. First Aid mnemonic: ants love M&M's, anterior spinal artery for medial medullary syndrome. COMLEX: The tongue deviates toward the side of a lower motor neuron hypoglossal lesion, the opposite localizing rule from a supranuclear corticobulbar lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 3 — predicting the major deficits caused by ACA, MCA, PCA, anterior choroidal, lenticulostriate, PICA, AICA, and SCA lesions, the deficit patterns this localization algorithm draws on directly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q17","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use a stroke-localization algorithm to move from neurologic deficit to likely vascular territory.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation acutely develops fluctuating drowsiness, bilateral vertical gaze palsy, and small, poorly reactive pupils, followed shortly by bilateral visual field loss. Applying the stroke-localization algorithm, embolic occlusion at which specific site best explains this cluster of findings?","choices":{"A":"The proximal basilar artery near the pontomedullary junction, producing locked-in syndrome","B":"A single posterior inferior cerebellar artery, producing unilateral lateral medullary syndrome","C":"The rostral basilar artery at its terminal bifurcation, producing top-of-basilar syndrome","D":"A single middle cerebral artery M1 segment, producing contralateral hemiparesis with aphasia","E":"The anterior communicating artery, producing bilateral medial frontal infarction with abulia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Embolic occlusion at the rostral tip of the basilar artery, where it bifurcates into the two posterior cerebral arteries, can simultaneously compromise the midbrain reticular activating system, producing fluctuating consciousness, the pretectal and oculomotor structures governing vertical gaze and pupillary reactivity, and both occipital lobes, producing bilateral visual field loss, together constituting top-of-basilar syndrome. Proximal basilar occlusion instead produces locked-in syndrome from bilateral ventral pontine damage rather than this vertical gaze and visual pattern, a single PICA occlusion produces a unilateral crossed medullary syndrome rather than bilateral cortical blindness, MCA occlusion produces unilateral hemiparesis and aphasia rather than bilateral visual and vertical gaze findings, and anterior communicating artery territory infarction affects the frontal lobes rather than producing vertical gaze palsy or cortical blindness.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Top-of-basilar syndrome combining fluctuating consciousness, vertical gaze abnormalities, pupillary changes, and bilateral cortical blindness from a single embolus at the basilar apex is a distinctive, high-yield posterior circulation pattern. COMLEX: Embolic sources such as atrial fibrillation should be sought whenever a top-of-basilar pattern is recognized, since the terminal basilar bifurcation is a classic embolic watershed point.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 3 — predicting the major deficits caused by ACA, MCA, PCA, anterior choroidal, lenticulostriate, PICA, AICA, and SCA lesions, the deficit patterns this localization algorithm draws on directly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q18","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use a stroke-localization algorithm to move from neurologic deficit to likely vascular territory.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man develops bilateral proximal arm and leg weakness with relative sparing of the hands, face, and distal musculature after an episode of severe hypotension during cardiac surgery. He remains fully alert with no aphasia or visual field deficit. Applying the stroke-localization algorithm, which mechanism and location best explain this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Bilateral embolic occlusion of both middle cerebral artery M1 segments from paradoxical cardiac emboli","B":"Bilateral watershed infarction at the border zones between the ACA and MCA territories from a global hypoperfusion event","C":"Bilateral lacunar infarction of the internal capsules from chronic, longstanding untreated hypertension","D":"Bilateral posterior cerebral artery occlusion from simultaneous emboli lodging at the basilar apex bifurcation","E":"Bilateral lateral medullary infarction from simultaneous occlusion of both posterior inferior cerebellar arteries on each side"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This proximal arm and leg weakness pattern sparing the hands and face, sometimes called the man-in-the-barrel pattern, reflects bilateral watershed, or border zone, infarction at the junction between the ACA and MCA territories, where the medial-lateral homunculus boundary lies, and results from a global hypoperfusion event such as intraoperative hypotension rather than a single embolus. Bilateral MCA M1 occlusion would instead be expected to produce more complete hemiparesis with aphasia or neglect rather than this proximal, hand-sparing pattern, bilateral internal capsule lacunar infarction is an unusual simultaneous event and would produce dense rather than proximal-predominant weakness, bilateral PCA occlusion would produce visual field loss rather than this motor pattern, and bilateral lateral medullary infarction would produce crossed sensory and bulbar findings rather than isolated proximal limb weakness.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The man-in-the-barrel watershed pattern from global hypoperfusion, sparing the hands and face at the ACA-MCA border zone, is a classic non-embolic stroke mechanism distinguished by the clinical setting of hypotension rather than a discrete arterial occlusion. First Aid mnemonic for the structures most vulnerable to global hypoxic-ischemic injury, including watershed zones: vulnerable hippos need pure water, for hippocampus, neocortex, and Purkinje cells. COMLEX: Perioperative and cardiac arrest hypotension are the classic clinical settings that should raise suspicion for a watershed rather than embolic stroke mechanism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 3 — predicting the major deficits caused by ACA, MCA, PCA, anterior choroidal, lenticulostriate, PICA, AICA, and SCA lesions, the deficit patterns this localization algorithm draws on directly","isHighYield":true}],"12":[{"id":"sdl12_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the gross organization of the spinal cord, including spinal segments, roots, rami, enlargements, conus medullaris, and cauda equina.","batch":1,"stem":"In an adult, the spinal cord proper terminates as the conus medullaris at approximately which vertebral level?","choices":{"A":"The T12 vertebral level alone","B":"The L1 to L2 vertebral level","C":"The L4 to L5 vertebral level","D":"The S1 to S2 vertebral level","E":"The foramen magnum level"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The adult spinal cord tapers into the conus medullaris at approximately L1 to L2, since the vertebral column grows faster than the cord during development, leaving the lumbar, sacral, and coccygeal roots to descend further before exiting. T12 is slightly too high and does not match the standard adult termination point, L4-L5 and S1-S2 are far too caudal and correspond instead to the lumbar cistern where a spinal tap is safely performed, and the foramen magnum marks the cord's rostral origin rather than its caudal end.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The L1-L2 conus level is why lumbar puncture is performed at L3-L4 or L4-L5, safely below the cord itself. COMLEX: Confusing conus level with lumbar puncture level is a common board trap worth memorizing precisely.","crossRef":"📎 Recognizing urgent neurologic red flags such as cauda equina syndrome and acute cord compression, the same conus medullaris/cauda equina anatomy described here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the gross organization of the spinal cord, including spinal segments, roots, rami, enlargements, conus medullaris, and cauda equina.","batch":1,"stem":"There are eight cervical spinal nerve roots but only seven cervical vertebrae. Through which intervertebral opening does the C8 root exit?","choices":{"A":"Between the base of the skull and the C1 vertebra","B":"Between the C6 and C7 vertebrae","C":"Between the T1 and T2 vertebrae","D":"Between the C7 and T1 vertebrae","E":"Through the transverse foramen of C7"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The C1 root lies between the base of the skull and the C1 vertebra, so roots C1 through C7 exit above their same-numbered vertebrae and the extra C8 root exits between the C7 and T1 vertebrae. From T1 caudally, every root exits below its same-numbered vertebra. The opening between skull and C1 carries the C1 root, and the C6-C7 opening carries the C7 root. The T1-T2 opening carries the T1 root. The transverse foramina carry the vertebral artery, not spinal nerve roots.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cervical roots exit above their vertebrae (C8 between C7 and T1); thoracic and lumbar roots exit below theirs; there are 31 pairs of spinal nerves (8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, 1 coccygeal). COMLEX: A C6-C7 disc herniation compresses the C7 root, so know the root-to-foramen rule before matching imaging to symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 7 — dermatomes, myotomes, and reflexes used to localize root lesions, which depends on this root-to-vertebra rule","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the gross organization of the spinal cord, including spinal segments, roots, rami, enlargements, conus medullaris, and cauda equina.","batch":1,"stem":"The cervical enlargement of the spinal cord, spanning roughly C4 to T1, exists to accommodate which structures?","choices":{"A":"Large motor neuron pools for the upper extremity","B":"The extra nerve roots contributing to the phrenic nerve","C":"The dorsal root ganglia serving the brachial plexus","D":"The additional white matter of the corticobulbar tract","E":"The sympathetic chain ganglia lying near the upper limb"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The cervical enlargement reflects the expanded anterior horn gray matter needed to house the large pools of lower motor neurons that innervate the many muscles of the upper extremity via the brachial plexus. Extra phrenic nerve roots are a minor contribution from C3-C5 rather than the reason for the enlargement, dorsal root ganglia lie outside the cord in the intervertebral foramina and do not expand cord diameter, corticobulbar fibers terminate in the brainstem rather than accumulating in the cervical cord, and the sympathetic chain is a paravertebral structure lying outside the cord entirely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The cervical and lumbosacral enlargements correspond directly to limb innervation and are a classic gross-anatomy correlate of anterior horn cell density. COMLEX: A lesion within an enlargement can cause disproportionately severe limb weakness compared with an equivalent lesion size at a non-enlarged cord level.","crossRef":"📎 Recognizing urgent neurologic red flags such as cauda equina syndrome and acute cord compression, the same conus medullaris/cauda equina anatomy described here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish gray matter horns from white matter columns and explain how each supports localization.","batch":1,"stem":"A lesion confined entirely to spinal cord gray matter is expected to produce a deficit with which localizing pattern?","choices":{"A":"A deficit that begins two levels above the lesion","B":"A deficit extending diffusely below the lesion level","C":"A deficit affecting only the contralateral limb below the lesion","D":"A deficit restricted to structures rostral to the brainstem","E":"A deficit confined to the exact segmental level of the damage"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gray matter houses segmental sensory processing, lower motor neurons, and preganglionic autonomic neurons, so damage here produces deficits precisely at the affected segment, such as a discrete band of anesthesia or a single weakened myotome. A deficit beginning above the lesion is not how spinal segmental damage behaves, a diffuse deficit extending below the lesion instead describes white matter tract involvement, an isolated contralateral limb deficit does not match the bilateral segmental pattern of local gray matter damage, and gray matter lesions are a spinal cord phenomenon rather than one restricted to structures above the brainstem.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Segmental gray matter deficits versus below-level white matter deficits is the single most important localizing principle in spinal cord lesions. COMLEX: This distinction underlies why polio, which destroys anterior horn cells, causes patchy segmental weakness rather than a clean sensory level.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — differentiating gray matter from white matter structurally and functionally, the same principle applied here to spinal cord horns and columns","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish gray matter horns from white matter columns and explain how each supports localization.","batch":1,"stem":"Because white matter tracts carry information to and from the brain, a lesion confined to white matter columns is expected to produce which pattern of deficit?","choices":{"A":"A deficit at and below the lesion level","B":"A deficit confined strictly to one spinal segment","C":"A deficit present only above the level of the lesion","D":"A deficit affecting exclusively the ipsilateral face","E":"A deficit that resolves completely within hours"},"correct":"A","explanation":"White matter columns carry long ascending and descending tracts connecting the cord to the brain, so their interruption produces functional loss at and below the level of the lesion, since all information traveling through that tract distal to the injury is disrupted. A deficit confined to one segment instead describes gray matter damage rather than a tract lesion, a deficit appearing only above the lesion contradicts how ascending and descending pathways are organized, facial findings are a brainstem or cortical phenomenon unrelated to spinal white matter, and a self-resolving deficit within hours does not reflect structural fiber tract damage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 'Below the level of the lesion' is the defining phrase for white matter tract damage and appears constantly in cord syndrome vignettes. COMLEX: This principle is the anatomic basis for describing paraplegia or sensory loss as beginning at a specific dermatomal level after trauma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — differentiating gray matter from white matter structurally and functionally, the same principle applied here to spinal cord horns and columns","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish gray matter horns from white matter columns and explain how each supports localization.","batch":1,"stem":"Within the posterior columns, fibers are somatotopically arranged such that sacral fibers lie medially in the gracile fasciculus while fibers from which region lie more laterally in the cuneate fasciculus?","choices":{"A":"The perineum and lower sacral dermatomes","B":"The lower extremity and pelvic viscera","C":"The upper extremity and upper trunk","D":"The face carried by the trigeminal system","E":"The lumbar dermatomes exclusively"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The posterior columns are somatotopically organized so that lower body, including sacral and lumbar, fibers travel medially in the gracile fasciculus, while upper extremity and upper trunk fibers join later and travel more laterally in the cuneate fasciculus. Perineal and sacral fibers are themselves the medial gracile group rather than the lateral addition, lower extremity and pelvic fibers are also part of the medial gracile group, facial discriminative touch travels via the separate trigeminal system rather than the posterior columns at all, and lumbar fibers likewise belong to the medial gracile group rather than the lateral cuneate group.","boardPrep":"Step 1: This medial-to-lateral, sacral-to-cervical somatotopy arises because fibers entering at each higher level are added lateral to those already ascending from lower levels. First Aid mnemonics: grass on the ground, clouds in the sky for medial nucleus gracilis versus lateral nucleus cuneatus, and dorsal columns organized as you are, hands at sides, arms outside, legs inside. COMLEX: The mirror-image lateral-to-medial arrangement in the anterolateral system is a frequently paired board comparison.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — differentiating gray matter from white matter structurally and functionally, the same principle applied here to spinal cord horns and columns","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the dorsal column-medial lemniscus, anterolateral/spinothalamic, corticospinal, and spinocerebellar pathways.","batch":1,"stem":"Primary afferent fibers of the dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway enter the posterior funiculus and ascend to which structure before crossing the midline?","choices":{"A":"The gracile and cuneate nuclei of the medulla","B":"The posterior horn of the segment of entry itself","C":"The ventral posterolateral nucleus of the thalamus","D":"The primary somatosensory cortex directly","E":"The red nucleus found within the midbrain"},"correct":"A","explanation":"DCML primary afferents ascend ipsilaterally without synapsing until they reach the gracile and cuneate nuclei in the caudal medulla, where they synapse before the resulting second-order fibers cross as the internal arcuate fibers to form the medial lemniscus. The posterior horn is where anterolateral system fibers synapse instead, the ventral posterolateral thalamic nucleus is the third-order relay reached only after the medullary crossing, the somatosensory cortex is the final destination rather than an intermediate synapse, and the red nucleus belongs to the rubrospinal motor pathway rather than any ascending sensory route.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DCML fibers stay ipsilateral all the way to the medulla, unlike ALS fibers which cross within one to two cord segments, a distinction tested constantly. COMLEX: This late crossing point explains why a unilateral cord lesion produces ipsilateral, not contralateral, dorsal column loss below the lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29/30 → Obj 1 — the dorsal column-medial lemniscus and anterolateral pathways introduced here in overview, dissected in far greater depth in Exam 3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the dorsal column-medial lemniscus, anterolateral/spinothalamic, corticospinal, and spinocerebellar pathways.","batch":1,"stem":"Second-order anterolateral system fibers carrying pain and temperature information cross the midline through which structure shortly after entering the cord?","choices":{"A":"The posterior white commissure","B":"The anterior white commissure","C":"The internal arcuate fibers of the medulla","D":"The pyramidal decussation of the medulla","E":"The corpus callosum at the cortical level"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After synapsing in the posterior horn, second-order ALS fibers cross the midline through the anterior white commissure, typically ascending one to two segments before completing the crossing, and then travel contralaterally in the anterolateral funiculus. There is no posterior white commissure serving this crossing function, the internal arcuate fibers instead carry DCML fibers crossing at the medulla rather than at cord level, the pyramidal decussation is the crossing point for descending corticospinal motor fibers, and the corpus callosum connects cerebral hemispheres rather than crossing ascending spinal sensory fibers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crossing at the anterior white commissure just one to two segments above entry is why a central cord lesion there produces a bilateral, cape-like loss of pain and temperature. COMLEX: This crossing point is the anatomic basis of the classic syringomyelia presentation tested repeatedly on boards.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29/30 → Obj 1 — the dorsal column-medial lemniscus and anterolateral pathways introduced here in overview, dissected in far greater depth in Exam 3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the dorsal column-medial lemniscus, anterolateral/spinothalamic, corticospinal, and spinocerebellar pathways.","batch":1,"stem":"Descending upper motor neuron fibers of the lateral corticospinal tract cross the midline at which specific location before descending in the cord?","choices":{"A":"The anterior white commissure of the cord","B":"The internal capsule of the cerebrum","C":"The pyramidal decussation in the caudal medulla","D":"The sensory decussation of the medulla","E":"The cerebral peduncle of the midbrain"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Corticospinal fibers descend from the motor cortex through the internal capsule and brainstem uncrossed, then cross at the pyramidal decussation in the caudal medulla before descending ipsilaterally as the lateral corticospinal tract within the spinal cord. The anterior white commissure is the crossing site for ascending pain-temperature fibers rather than descending motor fibers, the internal capsule is a route of passage rather than a crossing point, the sensory decussation refers to the DCML crossing rather than the motor pathway, and the cerebral peduncle is simply a midbrain relay station through which the fibers pass without crossing there.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The pyramidal decussation as the corticospinal crossing point explains why a cord lesion produces ipsilateral, not contralateral, spastic weakness below the lesion. COMLEX: A lesion above this decussation, such as in the internal capsule, instead produces contralateral weakness, a distinction essential for stroke localization.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29/30 → Obj 1 — the dorsal column-medial lemniscus and anterolateral pathways introduced here in overview, dissected in far greater depth in Exam 3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Predict deficits produced by lesions of the dorsal columns, lateral corticospinal tract, anterior white commissure, anterior horn cells, dorsal roots, and lateral horn autonomic neurons.","batch":1,"stem":"A discrete lesion isolated to the anterior white commissure of the cervical cord is expected to produce which pattern of sensory loss?","choices":{"A":"Unilateral loss of vibration and proprioception only","B":"Bilateral pain and temperature loss in a cape pattern","C":"Bilateral loss of light touch with sparing of pain fibers","D":"Contralateral hemiparesis with ipsilateral limb ataxia","E":"Unilateral radicular pain without any true sensory loss"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because crossing ALS fibers pass through the anterior white commissure just above their entry level, a lesion here selectively disrupts these decussating fibers bilaterally, producing loss of pain and temperature sensation in a cape-like pattern over the shoulders and arms when the lesion is cervical, classically seen in syringomyelia. Unilateral loss of vibration and proprioception instead reflects a dorsal column lesion rather than commissural damage, bilateral light touch loss with pain sparing reverses the actual modality selectively affected here, contralateral hemiparesis with ipsilateral ataxia does not match an isolated sensory-crossing lesion, and radicular pain without sensory loss instead suggests root irritation rather than central commissural disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The cape distribution of bilateral pain and temperature loss is the single most recognizable sign of syringomyelia and anterior white commissure disruption. COMLEX: Patients often report painless burns on their hands because pain and temperature sensation is lost first while touch and proprioception remain intact.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 6 — predicting motor findings from lesions at successive levels of the corticospinal pathway, the same lesion-to-deficit logic applied here to cord-level lesions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Predict deficits produced by lesions of the dorsal columns, lateral corticospinal tract, anterior white commissure, anterior horn cells, dorsal roots, and lateral horn autonomic neurons.","batch":1,"stem":"Destruction of anterior horn cells at a given spinal segment produces which combination of findings in the corresponding myotome?","choices":{"A":"Ipsilateral spastic paralysis with hyperreflexia noted","B":"Contralateral flaccid paralysis below that segment","C":"Ipsilateral flaccid weakness, atrophy, hyporeflexia","D":"Bilateral spasticity with preserved muscle bulk","E":"Isolated sensory loss without any true weakness"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Anterior horn cells are lower motor neurons, so their destruction severs the final common pathway to skeletal muscle, producing ipsilateral flaccid paralysis, atrophy from denervation, and hyporeflexia or areflexia limited to the myotomes of the damaged segments, as classically seen in poliomyelitis. Spastic paralysis with hyperreflexia instead describes upper motor neuron damage rather than anterior horn cell loss, a contralateral pattern is inconsistent with the strictly ipsilateral, segmental organization of anterior horn cell output, bilateral spasticity with preserved bulk again describes an upper motor neuron process, and isolated sensory loss without weakness would instead implicate a purely sensory structure such as the dorsal root or dorsal horn.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anterior horn cell loss producing pure LMN signs without sensory involvement, since these are purely motor neurons, is the classic teaching point for poliomyelitis and spinal muscular atrophy. COMLEX: The absence of sensory loss despite severe weakness is the key clue distinguishing anterior horn cell disease from a mixed sensorimotor root or nerve lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 6 — predicting motor findings from lesions at successive levels of the corticospinal pathway, the same lesion-to-deficit logic applied here to cord-level lesions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Predict deficits produced by lesions of the dorsal columns, lateral corticospinal tract, anterior white commissure, anterior horn cells, dorsal roots, and lateral horn autonomic neurons.","batch":1,"stem":"A lesion of the lateral horn autonomic neurons at the T1 spinal segment can disrupt sympathetic outflow to the head, producing which classic clinical triad?","choices":{"A":"Ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis of Horner syndrome","B":"Mydriasis, exophthalmos, and facial flushing","C":"Bilateral ptosis with preserved pupillary size","D":"Ipsilateral hyperhidrosis with mydriasis","E":"Contralateral ptosis with normal sweating"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The lateral horn from T1 to L2 houses preganglionic sympathetic neurons, and damage at the T1 level interrupts the oculosympathetic pathway to the head, producing the classic ipsilateral Horner syndrome triad of ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis. Mydriasis, exophthalmos, and flushing describe sympathetic overactivity rather than loss, bilateral ptosis with normal pupil size does not match the unilateral, pupil-constricting picture of sympathetic loss, ipsilateral hyperhidrosis with dilation again describes the opposite, sympathetic excess rather than interruption, and a contralateral pattern is inconsistent with the ipsilateral course of this pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Horner syndrome from T1 lateral horn or sympathetic chain injury, ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis, is one of the most frequently tested localizing triads in neurology. COMLEX: A Pancoast tumor at the lung apex is a classic cause of this same triad by compressing the sympathetic chain near its T1 origin.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 6 — predicting motor findings from lesions at successive levels of the corticospinal pathway, the same lesion-to-deficit logic applied here to cord-level lesions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate UMN signs, LMN signs, sensory level, radicular pain, and peripheral nerve patterns.","batch":1,"stem":"Which finding is most characteristic of upper motor neuron weakness rather than lower motor neuron weakness?","choices":{"A":"Prominent fasciculations with muscle wasting","B":"Flaccid tone with absent muscle stretch reflexes","C":"Isolated weakness of a single named muscle","D":"Denervation atrophy confined to one myotome","E":"Velocity-dependent spasticity with a Babinski sign"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Upper motor neuron lesions classically produce velocity-dependent spasticity, hyperreflexia, and a pathologic Babinski response, reflecting loss of descending inhibitory control over spinal reflex circuits. Fasciculations with wasting instead reflect denervation from a lower motor neuron process, flaccid tone with absent reflexes describes acute LMN or spinal shock states rather than the eventual UMN picture, isolated single-muscle weakness suggests a focal peripheral nerve lesion rather than the group-muscle pattern of UMN disease, and myotome-confined atrophy again reflects lower motor neuron or root-level denervation rather than corticospinal tract damage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Spasticity plus Babinski sign is the signature UMN combination, contrasted directly with flaccidity, atrophy, and fasciculations of LMN disease. COMLEX: Distinguishing these patterns at the bedside is essential for localizing weakness to brain, cord, or peripheral structures before ordering imaging.","crossRef":"📎 Differentiating upper motor neuron, lower motor neuron, neuromuscular junction, and myopathic weakness patterns, the same UMN/LMN distinction applied here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate UMN signs, LMN signs, sensory level, radicular pain, and peripheral nerve patterns.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient reports sharp, shooting pain radiating down the posterior thigh and calf following a specific dermatomal band after lifting a heavy box. This presentation is most consistent with which pattern?","choices":{"A":"A stocking-glove peripheral neuropathy pattern","B":"A pure anterior horn cell syndrome","C":"An isolated central cord lesion pattern","D":"Radicular pain from nerve root compression","E":"A diffuse polyneuropathy of metabolic origin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sharp, shooting pain following a strict dermatomal distribution after a mechanical precipitant such as heavy lifting is the classic presentation of radicular pain from a compressed or irritated nerve root, most often due to disc herniation. A stocking-glove pattern instead describes a length-dependent, distal-predominant polyneuropathy rather than a single dermatomal band, a pure anterior horn cell syndrome would present with weakness and atrophy rather than dermatomal pain, an isolated central cord lesion produces a bilateral cape sensory pattern rather than unilateral radiating limb pain, and a diffuse metabolic polyneuropathy would present symmetrically and distally rather than in one nerve root's territory.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Radicular pain in a strict dermatomal band, classically sciatica from L5 or S1 root compression, is one of the highest-yield peripheral localization patterns tested. COMLEX: Overlapping dermatomes explain why root compression often produces severe pain with only mild measurable sensory loss on exam.","crossRef":"📎 Differentiating upper motor neuron, lower motor neuron, neuromuscular junction, and myopathic weakness patterns, the same UMN/LMN distinction applied here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate UMN signs, LMN signs, sensory level, radicular pain, and peripheral nerve patterns.","batch":1,"stem":"A diabetic patient develops symmetric numbness and tingling that began in both feet and has gradually ascended toward the knees over several months. This pattern is best classified as which type of lesion?","choices":{"A":"A length-dependent polyneuropathy","B":"A single mononeuropathy of the peroneal nerve","C":"A discrete dermatomal radiculopathy","D":"A focal spinal cord sensory level","E":"An isolated lateral horn autonomic lesion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Symmetric, distally predominant sensory loss that begins in the feet and gradually ascends reflects a length-dependent polyneuropathy, in which the longest peripheral axons degenerate first, a classic stocking-glove pattern seen in diabetes. A single mononeuropathy would instead cause an isolated deficit confined to one named nerve's territory rather than a symmetric ascending pattern, a discrete radiculopathy would follow one dermatomal band rather than a diffuse distal gradient, a focal spinal cord sensory level produces an abrupt horizontal boundary on the trunk rather than a gradual distal-to-proximal limb pattern, and an isolated lateral horn lesion would affect autonomic function rather than cause distal sensory symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The stocking-glove pattern of diabetic polyneuropathy, feet before hands, reflects length-dependent axonal degeneration and is a frequently tested contrast to focal root or cord lesions. COMLEX: Loss of vibration sense at the great toe is often the earliest detectable sign of diabetic peripheral neuropathy on exam.","crossRef":"📎 Differentiating upper motor neuron, lower motor neuron, neuromuscular junction, and myopathic weakness patterns, the same UMN/LMN distinction applied here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the spinal cord blood supply and recognize anterior spinal artery syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"The anterior spinal artery, formed by branches of the vertebral arteries and reinforced by segmental medullary arteries, supplies approximately what proportion of the spinal cord cross-section?","choices":{"A":"The posterior one-third of the cord only","B":"The entire cord cross-section equally","C":"Only the posterior columns bilaterally","D":"A narrow strip limited to the central canal","E":"The anterior two-thirds of the cord"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The single anterior spinal artery and its central branches supply the anterior two-thirds of the cord, including the anterior and lateral horns, the anterolateral system, and the medial corticospinal fibers, while the paired posterior spinal arteries supply the remaining posterior third. The posterior third is instead the territory of the posterior spinal arteries rather than the anterior spinal artery, the two vessel systems do not divide the cord equally since the anterior artery's territory is substantially larger, the posterior columns are supplied by the posterior spinal arteries rather than the anterior vessel, and describing the territory as a narrow central strip understates the much larger anterior two-thirds distribution.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The anterior two-thirds versus posterior one-third vascular split is the anatomic key to predicting the dissociated sensory loss of anterior spinal artery syndrome. COMLEX: This same split explains why posterior column function can remain intact even after a devastating anterior cord infarct.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 5 — comparing ischemic, lacunar, and hemorrhagic vascular syndromes by mechanism and deficit pattern, the same territory-to-deficit logic applied here to cord vasculature","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the spinal cord blood supply and recognize anterior spinal artery syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"The large radicular vessel known as the artery of Adamkiewicz is clinically significant chiefly because it provides critical blood supply to which region?","choices":{"A":"The cervical enlargement of the cord","B":"The lumbosacral enlargement of the cord","C":"The gracile and cuneate nuclei of the medulla","D":"The conus medullaris exclusively without collaterals","E":"The upper thoracic cord above T4"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The artery of Adamkiewicz, most often arising on the left between roughly T9 and L2, is the dominant segmental medullary artery supplying the lumbosacral enlargement, and its injury during aortic surgery or severe hypotension can cause devastating lower cord ischemia. The cervical enlargement instead receives its blood supply from separate cervical segmental medullary arteries higher in the neck, the gracile and cuneate nuclei are medullary structures unrelated to this cord-level vessel, describing it as supplying only the conus without any collateral flow overstates its isolation since the vasocorona provides some anastomotic support, and the upper thoracic cord above T4 is instead a vascular watershed zone relatively poorly supplied by this vessel.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The artery of Adamkiewicz as the dominant lumbosacral feeder explains why thoracoabdominal aortic aneurysm repair carries a real risk of paraplegia. COMLEX: Surgeons localize this vessel preoperatively with imaging specifically to avoid ligating it during aortic cross-clamping.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 5 — comparing ischemic, lacunar, and hemorrhagic vascular syndromes by mechanism and deficit pattern, the same territory-to-deficit logic applied here to cord vasculature","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the spinal cord blood supply and recognize anterior spinal artery syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"Anterior spinal artery syndrome classically spares which sensory modality below the level of infarction?","choices":{"A":"Pain and temperature on both sides","B":"Crude touch and deep pressure sensation","C":"Vibration and conscious proprioception sense","D":"Voluntary motor strength in both legs","E":"Bladder and bowel sphincter control"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because the posterior columns receive independent blood supply from the paired posterior spinal arteries, anterior spinal artery syndrome spares vibration sense and conscious proprioception even as it produces bilateral flaccid then spastic paralysis and loss of pain and temperature below the lesion, creating a classic dissociated sensory loss. Pain and temperature sensation is instead lost, since the anterolateral system lies within the anterior two-thirds territory, crude touch and pressure are also carried partly through anterior pathways and are affected, voluntary motor strength is severely impaired rather than spared because the corticospinal tracts lie in the anterior territory, and bladder and bowel control are typically disrupted due to loss of autonomic and motor pathways within the affected territory.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dissociated sensory loss, preserved vibration and proprioception with lost pain and temperature and paralysis, is the single highest-yield fact about anterior spinal artery syndrome. COMLEX: This pattern classically follows aortic cross-clamping, dissection, or severe hypotension and should prompt urgent spinal MRI.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 5 — comparing ischemic, lacunar, and hemorrhagic vascular syndromes by mechanism and deficit pattern, the same territory-to-deficit logic applied here to cord vasculature","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use dermatomes, myotomes, and reflexes to localize common cord and root lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient reports a horizontal band of altered sensation at the level of the umbilicus. This landmark corresponds most closely to which spinal dermatome?","choices":{"A":"The T4 dermatome","B":"The T6 dermatome","C":"The L1 dermatome","D":"The T12 dermatome","E":"The T10 dermatome"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The umbilicus is the classic surface landmark for the T10 dermatome, a fact used constantly to estimate the level of a spinal cord lesion or the height of a spinal anesthetic block. T4 instead corresponds to the nipple line, a more rostral landmark, T6 lies above the xiphoid level, with T7 at the xiphoid process, well above the umbilicus, L1 corresponds to the inguinal ligament region well below the umbilical landmark, and T12 lies just below T10 near the pubic symphysis region rather than at the umbilicus itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: T4 at the nipples and T10 at the umbilicus are two of the most heavily tested dermatomal landmarks for localizing a sensory level. COMLEX: These same landmarks are used clinically to gauge the height of a spinal or epidural anesthetic block in real time.","crossRef":"📎 Matching sensory deficits to dorsal column, spinothalamic, radicular, or peripheral nerve patterns, the same localization skill applied here with dermatomes and reflexes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use dermatomes, myotomes, and reflexes to localize common cord and root lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"Absence of the patellar tendon reflex on physical examination most directly suggests a lesion affecting which spinal segments?","choices":{"A":"The S1 and S2 segments","B":"The C5 and C6 segments","C":"The L3 and L4 segments","D":"The C7 and C8 segments","E":"The T10 and T11 segments"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The patellar, or knee-jerk, reflex tests the integrity of the L3 and L4 spinal segments and the femoral nerve, so its absence localizes a lesion to that reflex arc. S1 and S2 instead correspond to the Achilles reflex rather than the patellar reflex, C5 and C6 correspond to the biceps reflex, C7 and C8 correspond to the triceps reflex, and T10 and T11 are segments without a standard named deep tendon reflex used in routine examination.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Memorizing the biceps at C5-C6, triceps at C7-C8, patellar at L3-L4, and Achilles at S1-S2 reflex arcs is essential for rapid bedside localization. COMLEX: Hyporeflexia points to the exact segment involved, while hyperreflexia below an intact segment instead suggests a UMN lesion higher up.","crossRef":"📎 Matching sensory deficits to dorsal column, spinothalamic, radicular, or peripheral nerve patterns, the same localization skill applied here with dermatomes and reflexes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use dermatomes, myotomes, and reflexes to localize common cord and root lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient is unable to dorsiflex the great toe and has weakness of ankle dorsiflexion after a disc herniation. This weakness pattern is most consistent with compression of which nerve root?","choices":{"A":"The S1 nerve root","B":"The L3 nerve root","C":"The L4 nerve root","D":"The L5 nerve root","E":"The S2 nerve root"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The L5 myotome governs great toe and ankle dorsiflexion, so weakness of these movements after disc herniation localizes to L5 root compression, often accompanied by sensory loss over the dorsum of the foot. The S1 root instead governs ankle plantar flexion and eversion along with the Achilles reflex rather than dorsiflexion, L3 governs hip flexion and knee extension, L4 governs knee extension and contributes to the patellar reflex rather than toe dorsiflexion, and S2 contributes to plantar flexion and perianal sensation rather than dorsiflexion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: L5 radiculopathy causing foot drop and great toe dorsiflexion weakness is a classic disc herniation vignette, often from an L4-L5 disc. COMLEX: Distinguishing L5 radiculopathy from a common peroneal mononeuropathy, which also causes foot drop, requires checking for foot inversion weakness, which is spared in peroneal palsy but affected in L5 radiculopathy.","crossRef":"📎 Matching sensory deficits to dorsal column, spinothalamic, radicular, or peripheral nerve patterns, the same localization skill applied here with dermatomes and reflexes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize major spinal cord syndromes and apply them to board-style clinical vignettes.","batch":1,"stem":"Brown-Sequard syndrome, resulting from spinal cord hemisection, produces which classic combination of findings?","choices":{"A":"Bilateral weakness with preserved sensation throughout","B":"Ipsilateral weakness plus contralateral pain loss","C":"Contralateral weakness with ipsilateral pain loss only","D":"Bilateral cape-like loss of pain and temperature","E":"Bilateral loss of vibration with normal limb strength"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Brown-Sequard syndrome from lateral cord hemisection produces ipsilateral spastic weakness and loss of proprioception and vibration below the lesion, along with contralateral loss of pain and temperature beginning one to two levels below the lesion, reflecting the differing crossing points of the corticospinal and dorsal column pathways versus the anterolateral system. Bilateral weakness with preserved sensation does not capture the characteristic dissociated, hemisection pattern, a contralateral weakness pattern with ipsilateral pain loss reverses the actual laterality of each finding, a bilateral cape distribution instead describes central cord syndrome or syringomyelia rather than hemisection, and bilateral vibration loss with normal strength does not reflect the corticospinal involvement seen in hemisection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Brown-Sequard syndrome, ipsilateral motor/proprioceptive loss with contralateral pain/temperature loss, is one of the most frequently tested named cord syndromes on both exams. COMLEX: Penetrating trauma such as a stab wound is the classic clinical scenario producing this syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 30 → Obj 6 — recognizing classic clinical patterns including syringomyelia and Brown-Sequard syndrome, introduced here and revisited in depth in Exam 3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize major spinal cord syndromes and apply them to board-style clinical vignettes.","batch":1,"stem":"Central cord syndrome from cervical hyperextension in a patient with underlying spinal stenosis classically produces weakness that is disproportionately worse in which body region?","choices":{"A":"The lower extremities more than the upper extremities","B":"The face and tongue more than either limb pair","C":"One limb only, sparing the other three limbs","D":"The trunk muscles more than any limb","E":"The upper extremities more than the lower extremities"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Central cord syndrome preferentially damages the more centrally located fibers serving the arms before affecting the more peripherally located leg fibers within the corticospinal tract, producing weakness that is classically worse in the upper extremities, particularly the hands, than in the legs. Lower extremity predominant weakness instead reverses this classic pattern, facial and tongue weakness would suggest a corticobulbar or brainstem process rather than a cervical cord syndrome, a single-limb pattern does not match the bilateral, arm-predominant distribution of central cord syndrome, and isolated trunk weakness is not the recognized hallmark of this syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Central cord syndrome as the most common incomplete cord syndrome, arm-predominant weakness after hyperextension injury in an older patient with cervical spondylosis, is a classic vignette. COMLEX: Sensory changes and bladder dysfunction may accompany the motor findings, varying with lesion severity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 30 → Obj 6 — recognizing classic clinical patterns including syringomyelia and Brown-Sequard syndrome, introduced here and revisited in depth in Exam 3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize major spinal cord syndromes and apply them to board-style clinical vignettes.","batch":1,"stem":"In an incomplete spinal cord injury, preserved perianal pinprick sensation or the ability to voluntarily contract the anal sphincter is referred to by which term?","choices":{"A":"Central cord sparing","B":"Dermatomal escape","C":"Root avulsion sparing","D":"Sacral sparing","E":"Segmental preservation only"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sacral sparing describes preserved sensory or motor function in the most caudal, sacral segments, such as perianal pinprick sensation or voluntary anal sphincter contraction, and it indicates that the outermost fibers of the spinal cord, which carry sacral information, have survived even when more central fibers have been destroyed, distinguishing an incomplete from a complete lesion. Central cord sparing is not the accepted terminology for this finding, dermatomal escape and root avulsion sparing are not established named clinical signs in this context, and segmental preservation only is a vague, non-standard phrase rather than the specific recognized term used clinically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sacral sparing is the key bedside finding that upgrades a spinal cord injury classification from complete to incomplete, with major prognostic implications. COMLEX: Checking perianal sensation and voluntary anal tone is therefore a mandatory part of the acute spinal cord injury examination.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 30 → Obj 6 — recognizing classic clinical patterns including syringomyelia and Brown-Sequard syndrome, introduced here and revisited in depth in Exam 3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q25","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the gross organization of the spinal cord, including spinal segments, roots, rami, enlargements, conus medullaris, and cauda equina.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man is stabbed in the lower back. Imaging shows the injury is confined below the level of the L2 vertebra. He has severe low back pain radiating down both legs, asymmetric leg weakness, and patchy sensory loss, but no clean horizontal sensory level. Which structure was most likely injured given this clinical picture?","choices":{"A":"The conus medullaris without root involvement","B":"The cauda equina, since the cord itself has already ended","C":"The lateral corticospinal tract at the thoracic cord","D":"The gracile fasciculus at the cervical cord","E":"The anterior spinal artery at the cervical enlargement"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because the spinal cord ends at approximately L1-L2, an injury below this level involves the descending lumbosacral nerve roots of the cauda equina rather than the cord itself, producing the described asymmetric, patchy, radicular pattern without a discrete sensory level, since individual roots rather than a compact tract bundle are damaged. Describing the injury as the conus medullaris exclusively without root involvement does not match a lesion clearly below L2, the lateral corticospinal tract at the thoracic cord would instead be higher and would typically produce a defined bilateral sensory level, the gracile fasciculus at the cervical cord is anatomically unrelated and far too rostral, and the anterior spinal artery at the cervical enlargement is likewise an unrelated, far more rostral vascular structure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distinguishing conus medullaris syndrome, more symmetric with early sphincter involvement, from cauda equina syndrome, more asymmetric and radicular, is a classic board contrast. COMLEX: Both are surgical emergencies, but the absence of a sharp sensory level below L2 is a key clue pointing toward root rather than cord injury.","crossRef":"📖 Recognizing cauda equina syndrome as an urgent neurologic red flag, the same anatomy localized here from a penetrating injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q26","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the gross organization of the spinal cord, including spinal segments, roots, rami, enlargements, conus medullaris, and cauda equina.","batch":2,"stem":"An anesthesiologist performs a lumbar puncture at the L4-L5 interspace on an adult patient. The needle safely bypasses the spinal cord itself and instead passes through cerebrospinal fluid surrounding a bundle of descending nerve roots. Which anatomic fact explains why this level is safe?","choices":{"A":"The dura ends at L4-L5, exactly at this interspace","B":"The cervical enlargement extends down to this level","C":"The adult cord ends at L1-L2, well above this interspace","D":"The lumbosacral enlargement begins at this exact level","E":"The conus medullaris lies at this interspace"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because the adult spinal cord tapers into the conus medullaris at roughly L1-L2, a needle inserted at L4-L5 passes safely through the lumbar cistern, encountering only cerebrospinal fluid and the freely floating roots of the cauda equina rather than the cord itself. The dura actually extends further caudally, to approximately S2, rather than ending at L4-L5, the cervical enlargement is an entirely separate, far more rostral structure unrelated to this level, the lumbosacral enlargement lies higher in the cord around L1-S2 of the cord's own segments rather than at the L4-L5 vertebral interspace, and the conus medullaris itself is located more rostrally at L1-L2 rather than at L4-L5.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Knowing that the L1-L2 conus level is well above the L3-L4 or L4-L5 puncture site is essential for safe lumbar puncture technique. First Aid mnemonic: to keep the cord alive, keep the spinal needle between L3 and L5. COMLEX: This same anatomic gap is why spinal anesthesia and diagnostic lumbar puncture are both performed in this lower lumbar window.","crossRef":"📖 Recognizing spinal cord compression as an urgent red flag, the same conus/cauda anatomy applied here to procedural safety","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q27","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the gross organization of the spinal cord, including spinal segments, roots, rami, enlargements, conus medullaris, and cauda equina.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old man sustains a gunshot wound to the mid-cervical spine. On examination, he has dense weakness of both arms and both legs, with the arms affected at least as severely as the legs. Which anatomic feature of the spinal cord at this level best explains the profound bilateral upper extremity involvement?","choices":{"A":"The lumbosacral enlargement housing leg motor neurons","B":"The conus medullaris tapering at this exact level","C":"The cauda equina bundle occupying this region","D":"The thoracic sympathetic lateral horn at this level","E":"The cervical enlargement housing arm motor pools"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The cervical enlargement, spanning roughly C4 to T1, contains an expanded population of anterior horn lower motor neurons dedicated to the many muscles of the upper extremity, so a severe cervical cord injury at this level produces profound bilateral arm involvement in addition to leg weakness from the interrupted corticospinal tracts descending toward the lumbosacral cord. The lumbosacral enlargement is a caudal structure unrelated to this cervical-level injury, the conus medullaris is located far more caudally at L1-L2, the cauda equina similarly lies well below this cervical injury site, and the thoracic sympathetic lateral horn governs autonomic rather than the somatic motor function driving this patient's limb weakness.","boardPrep":"Step 1: High cervical cord injury threatening the cervical enlargement is why cervical spine trauma carries the highest risk of catastrophic quadriplegia among spinal injuries. COMLEX: Assessing for concurrent respiratory compromise is critical in high cervical injuries because phrenic nerve roots, C3 through C5, also arise near this level.","crossRef":"📖 Recognizing acute spinal cord compression as a red flag requiring rapid evaluation, applied here to a high cervical gunshot injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q28","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish gray matter horns from white matter columns and explain how each supports localization.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man develops progressive weakness and atrophy confined to isolated hand muscles over several months, without any accompanying sensory complaint. MRI reveals a small intramedullary lesion restricted to the anterior horn at C7-C8. Which structural feature explains why sensation was entirely preserved?","choices":{"A":"The lesion destroyed only sensory white matter columns","B":"The lesion involved the dorsal root ganglion bilaterally","C":"The lesion spared the surrounding sensory white matter","D":"The lesion was confined to the posterior horn laminae","E":"The lesion selectively spared all anterior horn neurons"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because the anterior horn contains only motor neuron cell bodies while the surrounding white matter columns carry the ascending sensory tracts, a lesion confined to the anterior horn gray matter produces pure motor findings, atrophic weakness in this case, while leaving sensation entirely intact. Describing the lesion as destroying sensory white matter columns contradicts the stated pure motor presentation, dorsal root ganglion involvement would instead produce sensory loss rather than sparing it, confinement to the posterior horn would likewise produce sensory rather than motor deficits, and stating the lesion spared all anterior horn neurons contradicts the described weakness and atrophy, which requires anterior horn cell loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pure lower motor neuron findings without sensory loss localize precisely to the anterior horn cell, the classic pattern of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis when combined with upper motor neuron signs elsewhere. COMLEX: The combination of both UMN and LMN signs without any sensory findings is a hallmark clue pointing toward ALS rather than a mixed sensorimotor process.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — differentiating gray matter and white matter function, applied here to explain pure motor sparing of sensation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q29","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish gray matter horns from white matter columns and explain how each supports localization.","batch":2,"stem":"A pathology resident examines a single spinal cord cross-section. The gray matter shows a small lateral projection between the posterior and anterior horns, and a rounded cluster of large cells sits at the medial base of the posterior horn in lamina VII. There is no ventral bulge of the anterior horn. From which region of the cord was this section most likely taken?","choices":{"A":"Upper cervical cord, at about C2","B":"Thoracic cord, at a midthoracic segment","C":"Cervical enlargement, at about C7","D":"Lumbosacral enlargement, at about L5","E":"Sacral cord, at about S3"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Lamina VII at thoracic levels is characterized by the posterior thoracic nucleus, the dorsal nucleus of Clarke, at the medial base of the posterior horn, and by the lateral horn, which contains the intermediolateral cell column of preganglionic sympathetic neurons; the anterior horn is slender because the trunk needs no large limb motor pools. Upper cervical segments lack both a lateral horn and Clarke's nucleus. The cervical and lumbosacral enlargements show large, laterally expanded anterior horns from the motor neuron pools of lamina IX for the limbs. Sacral segments have preganglionic parasympathetic neurons but no thoracic-type lateral horn or dorsal nucleus of Clarke.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Level clues on cord sections: lateral horn and Clarke's nucleus mean thoracic; large anterior horns mean an enlargement; a lot of white matter means cervical. COMLEX: Lateral horn damage at T1 to upper thoracic levels interrupts sympathetic outflow to the head and causes Horner syndrome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — differentiating gray matter and white matter, extended here to identify cord level from gray matter shape","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q30","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish gray matter horns from white matter columns and explain how each supports localization.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with a large cervical syrinx has progressive loss of pain and temperature sensation over both shoulders and arms, while vibration sense and strength remain largely intact. Which anatomic explanation best accounts for this selective pattern?","choices":{"A":"The expanding cavity destroyed crossing fibers near the central gray","B":"The expanding cavity destroyed the peripheral white matter columns first","C":"The lesion selectively spared all gray matter structures entirely","D":"The lesion destroyed the lateral corticospinal tract exclusively","E":"The lesion was confined to the dorsal root entry zone bilaterally"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A syrinx expands outward from the central canal, first destroying the crossing anterolateral fibers passing through the anterior white commissure near the central gray matter, producing the classic bilateral cape-like loss of pain and temperature while the more peripherally located corticospinal and posterior column fibers remain relatively spared until the cavity enlarges further. Describing destruction of the peripheral white matter columns first reverses the actual centrifugal, center-outward expansion pattern of a syrinx, stating that gray matter was entirely spared contradicts the central location of syrinx damage, exclusive destruction of the lateral corticospinal tract would produce weakness rather than the described sensory pattern, and confinement to the dorsal root entry zone bilaterally does not match the central commissural mechanism actually responsible.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The centrifugal, center-outward expansion of a syringomyelic cavity explains the classic sequence of symptoms, cape sensory loss first, then weakness, as the syrinx enlarges. First Aid notes syrinx is Greek for tube, as in syringe, matching this fluid-filled cavitation. COMLEX: Chiari malformation is a well-known associated cause of syringomyelia and should be considered when this presentation is found in a younger patient.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — differentiating gray matter and white matter function, applied here to the central-to-peripheral expansion pattern of syringomyelia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q31","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the dorsal column-medial lemniscus, anterolateral/spinothalamic, corticospinal, and spinocerebellar pathways.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with vitamin B12 deficiency develops progressive loss of vibration sense and proprioception along with bilateral leg spasticity and hyperreflexia, but pain and temperature sensation remain intact. Which two pathways are most likely affected together in this presentation?","choices":{"A":"The anterolateral system and the dorsal columns","B":"The anterior horn cells and the dorsal root ganglia","C":"The spinocerebellar tracts and the anterior horn cells","D":"The dorsal columns and the lateral corticospinal tracts","E":"The anterolateral system and the lateral corticospinal tracts"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Subacute combined degeneration from B12 deficiency selectively damages the myelin of the dorsal columns and the lateral corticospinal tracts, producing combined loss of vibration and proprioception with spastic weakness and hyperreflexia, while sparing the anterolateral system so that pain and temperature sensation remains intact, matching this presentation precisely. Involvement of the anterolateral system together with the dorsal columns would instead produce combined loss of pain-temperature and proprioception rather than the pain-sparing pattern described, anterior horn cells and dorsal root ganglia are not the classically affected structures in this disease, spinocerebellar tracts and anterior horn cells are also not the primary targets, and combined anterolateral and corticospinal involvement would produce weakness with pain-temperature loss rather than the described intact pain sensation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Subacute combined degeneration selectively striking the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts while sparing the anterolateral system is one of the most distinctive tract-selective diseases tested. First Aid mnemonic, SCD: Spinocerebellar tracts, lateral Corticospinal tracts, and Dorsal columns are the demyelinated pathways. COMLEX: Checking a B12 level and methylmalonic acid is essential whenever this combined sensory ataxia and spasticity pattern is encountered.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — the dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway traced in far greater anatomic detail in Exam 3, the same tract selectively damaged here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q32","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the dorsal column-medial lemniscus, anterolateral/spinothalamic, corticospinal, and spinocerebellar pathways.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with longstanding untreated syphilis develops a wide-based, stomping gait, loss of vibration and joint position sense in both legs, and a positive Romberg sign, but normal strength and normal pain sensation. Which single pathway is most selectively affected in this presentation?","choices":{"A":"The anterolateral spinothalamic pain pathway","B":"The lateral corticospinal motor tract","C":"The posterior spinocerebellar sensory tract","D":"The anterior horn motor neuron cell pool","E":"The dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tabes dorsalis, a late manifestation of neurosyphilis, selectively degenerates the dorsal roots and posterior columns, producing loss of vibration and proprioception, a sensory ataxic gait, and a positive Romberg sign, while sparing strength and pain sensation since the corticospinal and anterolateral pathways are not primarily involved. Anterolateral pathway involvement would instead impair pain sensation, which is preserved here, lateral corticospinal tract involvement would produce weakness and spasticity rather than normal strength, posterior spinocerebellar tract disease classically produces limb ataxia without the prominent Romberg sign and areflexia seen here, and anterior horn motor neuron loss would cause weakness and atrophy rather than the purely sensory picture described.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tabes dorsalis as a classic dorsal column and dorsal root disease producing sensory ataxia and a positive Romberg sign is a frequently tested late syphilis complication. COMLEX: Argyll Robertson pupils, which accommodate but do not react to light, are a classic associated finding worth pairing with this presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — the dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway explored in depth in Exam 3, the same pathway selectively degenerated here in tabes dorsalis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q33","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the dorsal column-medial lemniscus, anterolateral/spinothalamic, corticospinal, and spinocerebellar pathways.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man sustains a knife wound producing a right-sided spinal cord hemisection at T6. On examination three days later, he has right leg weakness and loss of vibration sense on the right, but loss of pain and temperature sensation beginning around T8 on the left. Which anatomic fact best explains why the pain and temperature loss is contralateral and begins slightly below the injury level?","choices":{"A":"ALS fibers cross the midline one to two segments above their entry","B":"Corticospinal fibers cross the midline at the level of injury itself","C":"Dorsal column fibers cross immediately upon entering the cord","D":"The anterior spinal artery was selectively occluded on the left","E":"Spinocerebellar fibers cross at the same level as the ALS"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Anterolateral system fibers synapse in the posterior horn and cross through the anterior white commissure one to two segments above their level of entry before ascending contralaterally, so a right-sided hemisection at T6 produces contralateral, left-sided pain and temperature loss beginning a couple of segments lower, around T8, exactly as described. Corticospinal fibers already crossed at the medulla long before reaching the cord, so they do not cross again at the injury level, dorsal column fibers do not cross at cord level at all, remaining ipsilateral until the medulla, an anterior spinal artery occlusion would produce a different, bilateral pattern rather than this classic hemisection picture, and spinocerebellar fibers are not the pathway responsible for the pain and temperature findings described here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The one-to-two segment ascent before ALS crossing is precisely why Brown-Sequard syndrome shows a contralateral pain-temperature level slightly below, rather than exactly at, the lesion. COMLEX: Recognizing this subtle level discrepancy helps confirm a hemisection diagnosis on a board-style vignette rather than mistaking it for a different syndrome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 30 Obj 3 — the anterior white commissure crossing point studied in depth in Exam 3, the same crossing responsible for this level discrepancy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q34","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Predict deficits produced by lesions of the dorsal columns, lateral corticospinal tract, anterior white commissure, anterior horn cells, dorsal roots, and lateral horn autonomic neurons.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with cervical spondylotic myelopathy develops progressive spastic weakness of both legs, hyperreflexia, and bilateral Babinski signs, without any sensory complaints. Imaging shows cord compression sparing the dorsal columns and anterolateral tracts but affecting the lateral funiculi bilaterally. Which specific tract explains this pure motor presentation?","choices":{"A":"The bilateral dorsal columns","B":"The bilateral anterolateral systems","C":"The bilateral lateral corticospinal tracts","D":"The bilateral posterior spinocerebellar tracts","E":"The bilateral dorsal root entry zones"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The lateral corticospinal tracts occupy the lateral funiculi, and bilateral compression here produces progressive spastic paraparesis with hyperreflexia and Babinski signs while sparing sensation entirely, matching this pure motor myelopathy presentation. The dorsal columns carry vibration and proprioception, and their involvement would instead produce sensory ataxia rather than isolated motor findings, the anterolateral systems carry pain and temperature and their involvement would produce sensory loss, the posterior spinocerebellar tracts carry unconscious proprioceptive information to the cerebellum rather than voluntary motor output, and the dorsal root entry zone is a site of sensory fiber entry rather than a descending motor pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cervical spondylotic myelopathy producing bilateral lateral corticospinal tract compression with spastic gait and hyperreflexia, sparing sensation, is a classic degenerative cord vignette in older patients. COMLEX: Hoffmann sign and inverted radial reflex are additional bedside findings that support cervical myelopathy in this population.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 6 — predicting motor findings from corticospinal lesions at various levels, applied here to bilateral lateral funiculus compression","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q35","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Predict deficits produced by lesions of the dorsal columns, lateral corticospinal tract, anterior white commissure, anterior horn cells, dorsal roots, and lateral horn autonomic neurons.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman is found to have an isolated intramedullary lesion selectively destroying the dorsal columns bilaterally at T4, sparing all other tracts and gray matter. Which combination of findings would be expected below the lesion?","choices":{"A":"Loss of vibration/proprioception with a wide gait","B":"Bilateral spastic paralysis with preserved sensation","C":"Bilateral loss of pain and temperature sensation only","D":"Flaccid paralysis with prominent segmental atrophy","E":"Isolated bladder and bowel dysfunction alone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Isolated dorsal column damage produces loss of vibration sense and conscious proprioception below the lesion, resulting in sensory ataxia manifested as a wide-based, stomping gait and a positive Romberg sign, while motor strength and pain sensation remain intact since the corticospinal and anterolateral tracts are unaffected. Bilateral spastic paralysis with preserved sensation instead describes isolated corticospinal tract damage rather than dorsal column disease, bilateral loss of pain and temperature only reflects anterolateral system involvement rather than dorsal column pathology, flaccid paralysis with atrophy describes anterior horn cell or lower motor neuron disease, and isolated bladder and bowel dysfunction without any limb sensory finding does not match the described selective dorsal column lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Isolated dorsal column disease producing sensory ataxia and a positive Romberg sign, without weakness, is a classic pattern seen in tabes dorsalis and subacute combined degeneration. COMLEX: A positive Romberg sign specifically implicates proprioceptive loss rather than cerebellar dysfunction, since cerebellar ataxia is not corrected by visual input.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 6 — predicting findings from lesions at successive cord levels, contrasted here with a pure dorsal column rather than corticospinal lesion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q36","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Predict deficits produced by lesions of the dorsal columns, lateral corticospinal tract, anterior white commissure, anterior horn cells, dorsal roots, and lateral horn autonomic neurons.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man sustains an isolated stab wound damaging only the left dorsal root at L5, sparing the cord parenchyma itself. Which finding is most specific to this dorsal root injury rather than a cord-level lesion?","choices":{"A":"A sensory level across the trunk below T10","B":"Bilateral spastic paraparesis of both lower extremities","C":"A cape-like bilateral loss of pain and temperature","D":"Anesthesia confined to the L5 dermatome with radicular pain","E":"Diffuse bilateral hyporeflexia throughout both legs"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Isolated dorsal root injury produces loss of all sensory modalities confined strictly to the corresponding dermatome, here L5, often accompanied by radicular pain, since only the single root supplying that segment's cutaneous territory is affected. A trunk-wide sensory level below T10 instead reflects a cord-level white matter lesion rather than a single root injury, bilateral spastic paraparesis would require corticospinal tract, not dorsal root, involvement, a cape-like bilateral pattern reflects central cord or anterior white commissure disease rather than a single unilateral root lesion, and diffuse bilateral hyporeflexia throughout both legs is far too widespread to result from an isolated single root injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A single dermatomal band of anesthesia with radicular pain, rather than a bilateral level or diffuse pattern, is the hallmark of an isolated dorsal root lesion. COMLEX: Overlapping adjacent dermatomes explain why a single root injury may produce prominent pain out of proportion to any measurable numbness on exam.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 8 — differentiating UMN from LMN signs using tone, reflexes, and localization, contrasted here with a purely sensory dorsal root pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q37","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate UMN signs, LMN signs, sensory level, radicular pain, and peripheral nerve patterns.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man presents two hours after an acute thoracic spinal cord injury with flaccid paralysis and absent reflexes below the lesion. His family asks whether this means the corticospinal tract has been spared. Which explanation best addresses their question?","choices":{"A":"Flaccidity confirms the corticospinal tract is intact","B":"This is spinal shock; UMN signs typically emerge over days to weeks","C":"Flaccidity always indicates a purely peripheral nerve injury","D":"This pattern proves the lesion is limited to gray matter alone","E":"This pattern rules out any upper motor neuron involvement permanently"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Acute severe spinal cord injury commonly produces a transient period of spinal shock, during which flaccid paralysis and areflexia occur despite corticospinal tract damage, and the classic upper motor neuron picture of spasticity, hyperreflexia, and a Babinski sign typically emerges only after days to weeks as spinal reflex circuits recover excitability. Flaccidity does not confirm an intact corticospinal tract, since it is precisely the acute phase seen even with severe UMN damage, it does not indicate a purely peripheral nerve injury given the clear central mechanism of trauma, it does not prove the lesion is limited to gray matter, since white matter tract injury is very likely with this severity, and it does not permanently rule out UMN involvement, since UMN signs are expected to develop later.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Spinal shock as a temporary flaccid phase before eventual UMN signs emerge is a frequently tested exception to the classic UMN/LMN dichotomy. COMLEX: Family counseling about expected clinical evolution over the following days to weeks is an important, testable communication point in acute spinal cord injury.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 8 — differentiating upper motor neuron from lower motor neuron signs, applied here to the temporary flaccid phase of acute cord injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q38","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate UMN signs, LMN signs, sensory level, radicular pain, and peripheral nerve patterns.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman presents with numbness and tingling isolated to the thumb, index finger, and middle finger of her right hand, worse at night, without any weakness of wrist extension or forearm sensory loss. Which pattern best describes this presentation?","choices":{"A":"A radiculopathy following the C6 dermatome pattern","B":"A length-dependent symmetric distal polyneuropathy","C":"A mononeuropathy of a single named peripheral nerve","D":"A central cord lesion in a cape-like distribution","E":"A lateral corticospinal tract lesion near C6"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sensory loss confined to the thumb, index, and middle fingers with sparing of the forearm and no weakness of wrist extension localizes to an isolated mononeuropathy of the median nerve, classically carpal tunnel syndrome, rather than a spinal root or central pattern. A C6 radiculopathy would typically also involve the forearm and could include weakness of wrist extension or the biceps reflex, a length-dependent polyneuropathy would present symmetrically in both hands and feet rather than in one hand's median distribution, a central cord lesion would produce a bilateral cape-like pattern rather than a unilateral hand-only finding, and a lateral corticospinal tract lesion would produce weakness and spasticity rather than isolated sensory symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recognizing median nerve mononeuropathy, thumb, index, and middle finger numbness worse at night, versus a C6 radiculopathy, which also involves the forearm, is a classic distinction. COMLEX: A positive Tinel or Phalen sign at the wrist supports carpal tunnel syndrome over a proximal radicular process.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 8 — differentiating UMN and LMN signs and peripheral localization, applied here to distinguish mononeuropathy from radiculopathy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q39","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate UMN signs, LMN signs, sensory level, radicular pain, and peripheral nerve patterns.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with a newly diagnosed small cell lung cancer develops numbness and loss of position sense that involves her upper arms and thighs as much as her hands and feet, along with sensory ataxia. Strength is normal. Which pattern best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"A length-dependent distal axonopathy affecting the longest fibers first","B":"A cervical radiculopathy following one dermatomal band down the arm","C":"A thoracic spinal cord sensory level with a horizontal boundary","D":"A motor neuronopathy from loss of anterior horn cells","E":"A sensory neuronopathy from loss of posterior root ganglion cell bodies"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A sensory neuronopathy is a loss of cell bodies in the posterior root ganglion, producing a sensory loss that involves both distal and proximal portions of an extremity and may include most or all sensory modalities; it is a recognized paraneoplastic pattern. A length-dependent distal axonopathy, as in diabetes, begins in the feet, reaches about the knees, then appears in the hands, sparing proximal limbs early. A radiculopathy causes sharp, shooting pain in a single dermatome. A cord sensory level produces a horizontal boundary on the trunk, not patchy proximal and distal limb loss. A motor neuronopathy causes flaccid weakness, fasciculations, and atrophy without sensory loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distal-only, longest-fiber-first loss means axonopathy; proximal plus distal loss with sensory ataxia and normal strength means a ganglionopathy (sensory neuronopathy). COMLEX: A new sensory neuronopathy in a smoker should prompt a search for an underlying malignancy such as small cell lung cancer.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 8 — differentiating UMN, LMN, and peripheral patterns, applied here to a non-length-dependent sensory pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q40","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the spinal cord blood supply and recognize anterior spinal artery syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man undergoes emergency repair of a ruptured thoracoabdominal aortic aneurysm. After surgery, he has flaccid paralysis of both legs, absent pain and temperature sensation below T10, urinary retention, but intact vibration and joint position sense. Which vascular event best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Occlusion of the paired posterior spinal arteries bilaterally","B":"Thrombosis of a dural venous sinus draining the cord","C":"Embolic occlusion of the middle cerebral artery trunk","D":"Occlusion of the anterior spinal artery territory","E":"Bilateral occlusion of the vertebral arteries in the neck"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This dissociated pattern, bilateral flaccid paraplegia with lost pain and temperature but preserved vibration and proprioception, is the classic anterior spinal artery syndrome, and its occurrence after thoracoabdominal aortic surgery strongly implicates injury to the artery of Adamkiewicz, the dominant segmental feeder of the anterior spinal artery in the lumbosacral cord. Occlusion of the paired posterior spinal arteries would instead spare pain and temperature while causing loss of vibration and proprioception, the opposite of what is described, dural venous sinus thrombosis is a cranial rather than spinal vascular event, middle cerebral artery embolism produces cortical hemispheric deficits rather than a spinal cord syndrome, and bilateral vertebral artery occlusion in the neck would produce brainstem and cerebellar signs rather than this thoracic-level dissociated cord syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anterior spinal artery syndrome as a recognized complication of aortic aneurysm repair, from artery of Adamkiewicz sacrifice or prolonged cross-clamp time, is a classic postoperative vignette. COMLEX: Preoperative spinal angiography to localize the artery of Adamkiewicz is used specifically to reduce this devastating complication.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 5 — comparing ischemic and hemorrhagic vascular syndromes by mechanism, applied here to a spinal rather than cerebral vascular territory","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q41","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the spinal cord blood supply and recognize anterior spinal artery syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman sustains a severe hyperextension injury of the cervical spine in a motor vehicle collision. She develops bilateral arm weakness worse than leg weakness, patchy sensory loss, and urinary retention, without a clear dissociated sensory pattern. Which mechanism best explains this presentation compared with classic anterior spinal artery syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Complete infarction of the entire cord cross-section","B":"Transient central cord ischemia or contusion","C":"Isolated infarction of the posterior spinal arteries alone","D":"Selective infarction sparing the central gray matter","E":"Embolic occlusion of a single lenticulostriate vessel"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cervical hyperextension injury classically produces central cord syndrome through transient ischemia, edema, or contusion centered on the vulnerable central gray and adjacent medial white matter, producing arm-predominant weakness and patchy findings rather than the clean dissociated sensory loss of a pure anterior spinal artery infarction. Complete infarction of the entire cord cross-section would instead be expected to produce a total, non-selective loss of all function rather than this arm-predominant pattern, isolated posterior spinal artery infarction would instead cause vibration and proprioceptive loss with relatively preserved strength and pain sensation, describing selective sparing of the central gray matter contradicts the central location of the injury actually responsible, and a lenticulostriate vessel is an intracranial structure entirely unrelated to spinal cord vascular supply.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Central cord syndrome from cervical hyperextension, often in an older patient with preexisting spondylosis, is a distinct entity from anterior spinal artery syndrome despite some overlapping arm weakness. COMLEX: Recovery in central cord syndrome typically follows a leg-before-arm and proximal-before-distal pattern as swelling resolves.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 5 — comparing vascular stroke mechanisms and deficit patterns, contrasted here with central cord ischemia versus a discrete arterial territory infarct","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q42","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the spinal cord blood supply and recognize anterior spinal artery syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A vascular surgeon explains to a patient that a segmental feeder vessel arising between roughly T9 and L2, usually on the left, will be carefully preserved during upcoming aortic surgery because of its importance to spinal perfusion. Which vessel is being described, and what is its primary clinical significance?","choices":{"A":"The artery of Adamkiewicz, main lumbosacral feeder","B":"The posterior spinal artery, the dorsal column feeder","C":"The vertebral artery, the dominant cervical cord feeder","D":"The anterior choroidal artery, a dominant cerebral feeder","E":"The basilar artery, the dominant brainstem feeder"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The vessel described, typically arising between T9 and L2 on the left, is the artery of Adamkiewicz, the largest segmental medullary artery and the dominant blood supply to the lumbosacral enlargement, making it essential to preserve during thoracoabdominal aortic surgery to avoid catastrophic lower cord ischemia. The posterior spinal artery is a paired vessel supplying the posterior third of the cord rather than this specific segmental feeder, the vertebral artery supplies the cervical cord and posterior cranial circulation rather than the thoracolumbar region described, the anterior choroidal artery is an intracranial branch of the internal carotid artery unrelated to spinal perfusion, and the basilar artery supplies the brainstem and cerebellum rather than the lumbosacral spinal cord.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The artery of Adamkiewicz's typical T9-L2 left-sided origin and its role as the dominant lumbosacral feeder is one of the most specific, frequently tested vascular facts in this SDL. COMLEX: Surgeons obtain preoperative imaging specifically to map this vessel before aortic cross-clamping to minimize infarction risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 5 — comparing vascular territories and their clinical significance, applied here to the dominant segmental feeder of the lumbosacral cord","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q43","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use dermatomes, myotomes, and reflexes to localize common cord and root lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman reports a band of numbness across her nipple line after a fall, along with mild bilateral leg weakness. Using dermatomal landmarks, at approximately which spinal level should this sensory finding be localized?","choices":{"A":"The T10 level","B":"The T6 level","C":"The T4 level","D":"The C8 level","E":"The L1 level"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The nipple line is the classic surface landmark for the T4 dermatome, so a sensory band at this location, together with leg weakness suggesting cord involvement, localizes the lesion to approximately the T4 spinal level. T10 instead corresponds to the umbilicus, a more caudal landmark, T6 lies just above the xiphoid level, with T7 at the xiphoid process, below the nipple line, C8 is a cervical level governing hand sensation rather than a trunk landmark, and L1 corresponds to the inguinal region, again far more caudal than the nipple line described.","boardPrep":"Step 1: T4 at the nipple line and T10 at the umbilicus are the two dermatomal landmarks most reliably tested for localizing a thoracic sensory level. COMLEX: Rapid recall of these landmarks is essential for efficient bedside neurologic localization in a timed clinical encounter.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 8 — differentiating UMN from LMN signs and localizing findings, applied here using a dermatomal landmark to estimate a cord level","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q44","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use dermatomes, myotomes, and reflexes to localize common cord and root lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man undergoes a routine neurologic exam and is found to have an absent triceps reflex on the right along with mild weakness of forearm extension. Which nerve root is most likely involved?","choices":{"A":"The C5 nerve root","B":"The C6 nerve root","C":"The T1 nerve root","D":"The C7 nerve root","E":"The C8 nerve root"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The triceps reflex and forearm extension are governed primarily by the C7 nerve root, so an absent triceps reflex with weak elbow extension localizes to a C7 radiculopathy, commonly from a C6-C7 disc herniation. C5 instead governs the biceps reflex and shoulder abduction, C6 governs the brachioradialis reflex and forearm supination, T1 primarily governs intrinsic hand muscle function without a major named reflex, and C8, although it contributes to the triceps reflex arc, mainly governs finger flexion and intrinsic hand strength, so weak elbow extension with an absent triceps jerk points to C7.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Biceps at C5-C6, brachioradialis at C6, and triceps at C7-C8 reflex arcs are a classic, heavily tested sequence for cervical root localization. COMLEX: Correlating the specific weak movement, elbow extension for C7, with the corresponding reflex loss strengthens confidence in the level of a suspected radiculopathy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 8 — differentiating UMN from LMN patterns and localizing weakness, applied here to a specific cervical root and reflex arc","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q45","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Use dermatomes, myotomes, and reflexes to localize common cord and root lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man reports difficulty walking on his heels and describes numbness over the top of his right foot after several weeks of low back pain. Ankle reflexes are normal and symmetric. Which nerve root deficit best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"An S1 radiculopathy affecting plantar flexion and heel sensation","B":"An L3 radiculopathy affecting knee extension and anterior thigh","C":"An L4 radiculopathy affecting the patellar reflex primarily","D":"A common peroneal mononeuropathy at the fibular head","E":"An L5 radiculopathy affecting dorsiflexion and foot sensation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Difficulty walking on the heels reflects weak ankle dorsiflexion, and numbness over the dorsum of the foot reflects the L5 dermatome, together localizing to an L5 radiculopathy; the ankle, or Achilles, reflex is normal here because that reflex tests S1 rather than L5 function. An S1 radiculopathy would instead impair toe-walking through weak plantar flexion and would typically reduce the ankle reflex, which is preserved in this vignette, an L3 radiculopathy affects the anterior thigh and knee extension rather than the foot, an L4 radiculopathy would be expected to reduce the patellar reflex, which is not described as involved, and while a peroneal mononeuropathy could mimic foot drop, the preceding radicular back pain and preserved reflex pattern favor a root rather than a peripheral nerve lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: L5 radiculopathy sparing the ankle reflex, since that reflex tests S1, is a classic distinguishing detail from S1 radiculopathy, which does reduce the ankle jerk. COMLEX: Testing hip abduction, an L5-innervated movement largely spared in a pure peroneal nerve palsy, helps separate L5 radiculopathy from peroneal mononeuropathy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 39 Obj 8 — differentiating UMN from LMN weakness and localizing lesions with reflex testing, applied here to distinguish L5 from S1 and peroneal patterns","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q46","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize major spinal cord syndromes and apply them to board-style clinical vignettes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old man dives into shallow water and sustains a complete spinal cord injury at C2. He becomes apneic at the scene and requires mechanical ventilation. Which of the following best explains his loss of spontaneous breathing?","choices":{"A":"The phrenic nerve fibers are torn as they exit at the C2 foramen","B":"The lesion destroys the medullary respiratory centers directly","C":"The intercostal motor neurons in the cervical cord are destroyed","D":"Phrenic motor neurons below the lesion lose input from the medulla","E":"Spinal shock abolishes all reflexes, including the cough reflex"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The phrenic nucleus lies in the central anterior horn at C3 to C6 and innervates the diaphragm; it is driven by respiratory centers in the medulla. A complete lesion above it, as at C2, disconnects the intact phrenic motor neurons from those medullary centers, so the diaphragm stops receiving its drive and the patient cannot breathe without support. The phrenic nerve does not exit at C2. The medulla lies above the injury and is not damaged directly. Intercostal motor neurons are in the thoracic cord and are also cut off, but losing them alone would not stop diaphragmatic breathing. Spinal shock abolishes reflexes below the lesion but does not explain apnea, which follows from the loss of descending drive.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The course places the phrenic nucleus at C3 to C6; First Aid's mnemonic is C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive. Lesions above C3 cause respiratory failure. COMLEX: In high cervical injury, airway and ventilatory support take priority, and lesions at C4 to C5 may leave partial diaphragmatic function.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 30 Obj 6 — recognizing classic spinal cord syndromes, revisited in far greater depth in Exam 3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q47","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize major spinal cord syndromes and apply them to board-style clinical vignettes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with cervical spinal stenosis falls forward and strikes his forehead, hyperextending his neck. He develops weakness that is markedly worse in both hands and arms than in his legs, along with patchy sensory loss and urinary retention, but no clear lateralized pattern. Which cord syndrome best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Brown-Sequard syndrome from lateral cord hemisection","B":"Central cord syndrome from cervical hyperextension","C":"Anterior cord syndrome from vascular occlusion","D":"Posterior cord syndrome from artery infarction","E":"Cauda equina syndrome from lumbar root compression"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cervical hyperextension in a patient with preexisting spinal stenosis is the classic mechanism for central cord syndrome, which produces arm-predominant weakness, variable patchy sensory loss, and bladder dysfunction due to preferential involvement of the more centrally located cervical fibers. Brown-Sequard syndrome would instead show a lateralized, crossed pattern rather than bilateral arm-predominant weakness, anterior cord syndrome would produce more uniform bilateral leg and arm motor loss with dissociated sensory loss rather than this arm-predominant pattern, posterior cord syndrome is an uncommon entity producing predominantly proprioceptive loss rather than this motor picture, and cauda equina syndrome results from lumbosacral root compression below the cord rather than a cervical hyperextension mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Central cord syndrome as the most common incomplete spinal cord injury pattern, arm-predominant weakness after hyperextension in an older patient with stenosis, is a frequently tested vignette. COMLEX: This same mechanism is a reason cervical collar precautions are emphasized even after seemingly minor falls in older patients with known spondylosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 30 Obj 6 — recognizing classic cord syndrome patterns, contrasted here between central cord and other named syndromes ahead of Exam 3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q48","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Recognize major spinal cord syndromes and apply them to board-style clinical vignettes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with cervical spondylosis falls forward and hyperextends his neck. He has bilateral arm and hand weakness much worse than leg weakness, patchy pain and temperature loss, and urinary retention, with posterior column sensation intact. His family asks what recovery to expect. Which statement best describes the typical course of this syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Leg function usually returns first, then bladder function, and the arms last","B":"Arm function usually returns first, then the legs, and the bladder last","C":"Bladder function usually returns first, then the arms, and the legs last","D":"Posterior column sensation is lost at first and is the last function to return","E":"Recovery is rare, because this is a complete cord injury below the lesion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This is the acute central cervical cord syndrome, an incomplete injury in which hyperextension, often over bone spurs, momentarily compromises supply through the anterior spinal artery. It produces bilateral weakness worse in the upper than the lower extremities, variable pain and thermal loss, and bladder dysfunction, with posterior column sensation unaffected. Many patients recover most or all function within 4 to 6 days; lower extremity function returns first, bladder function next, and upper extremity function last, so the reverse orders are incorrect. Posterior column sensation is not affected in this syndrome, and it is an incomplete, not complete, injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Central cord syndrome: arms worse than legs, from hyperextension in an older patient with spondylosis; it is incomplete and often improves. COMLEX: Counsel that hand function is usually the slowest and least complete to recover, which shapes rehabilitation goals.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 30 Obj 6 — recognizing classic spinal cord syndrome patterns, applied here to the recovery course of an incomplete injury","isHighYield":true}]};
